--[[
	ZONE GRID - PROTOTYPE

	What this is FOR
	----------------
	One question, and nothing else: is a limit on SPACE, alongside the
	limit on weight, any fun in GAMMA?

	Right now the only thing stopping you looting is what your back can
	carry. A thousand tiny valuables weigh nothing and go home with you;
	a rocket launcher weighs everything and does not. Tarkov's answer is
	that a thing also takes up ROOM, and the room you have comes from
	what you are wearing.

	So: your bag gets a rectangle of cells, and the backpack on your
	back is where they come from. With no backpack you have eight
	squares - enough to pick one up off the ground and no more. When it
	is full it is full, and what will not fit is dropped at your feet.

	What this deliberately is NOT
	-----------------------------
	* NOT hand placement. Anomaly has no "put this item in that square"
	  interaction at all - the window packs items for you - and building
	  one is weeks, not days. The Tetris comes later. This tests the
	  CEILING, which is the part that might not be fun.
	* NOT containers that hold more than they take. The ammo box that is
	  2x1 outside and forty rounds inside is the good bit, and it is
	  also the hard bit. Armor Mag Pouches already proves the trick
	  works - a magazine inside an unworn rig stops being an item - so
	  it is a known quantity, and it can wait until the ceiling has
	  earned it.
	* NOT the new layout. Gear on the left, the space it grants beside
	  it, one column: all of that is a rework of the inventory window
	  and none of it is needed to answer the question.

	It is a prototype. It is meant to be thrown away or rewritten.

	TURN THESE OFF while testing, or the test means nothing
	------------------------------------------------------
	SortingPlus, Seamless Inventory Sort, FIUT. Sorting re-packs the
	grid on every refresh, which is the opposite of everything this is
	about.
--]]

-- Squared Away is one MCM page with a section per part; this is
-- the page, and grid_general below is the section.
local MOD     = "squared_away"
local GROUP   = "grid_general"
-- ============================================================
-- THE PLAYER'S OWN LIST, WHATEVER THE WINDOW IS CALLING IT
--
--   "ofc i want only the player inventory side to have this tarkov
--    grid features and vendor and stash as it is."
--
-- One name was not enough. ui_inventory:Reset shows a DIFFERENT
-- container for the player's own inventory depending on the mode:
--
--     inventory / loot / repair     actor_bag
--     trade                         actor_trade_bag
--
-- So at a trader the grid was looking at actor_bag, which is hidden,
-- while the list on screen was actor_trade_bag - no sections, no rig,
-- nothing. That is the "vendor window uses base game" report.
--
-- Everything NOT in here is left exactly as the game draws it:
-- npc_bag (the stash or the body you are looting), npc_trade,
-- npc_trade_bag, and actor_trade - the pile you are offering, which is
-- a handful of cells and not an inventory.
-- ============================================================
-- In order, so a list of them comes out the same way every time. A
-- table keyed by name would be walked in whatever order Lua felt like,
-- and "which of my lists was laid out first" is not a thing to leave to
-- chance.
local OUR_ORDER = { "actor_bag", "actor_trade_bag" }

local OURS = {}
for i = 1, #OUR_ORDER do OURS[OUR_ORDER[i]] = true end

local function ours(id)
	return id ~= nil and OURS[id] == true
end

-- ============================================================
-- ONE VIEW WRITES THE LAYOUT. THE OTHER ONLY SHOWS IT.
--
--   "quiting vendor inventory resorts the items randomly, every time.
--    stash does not have that problem"
--
-- actor_trade_bag is a FILTERED copy of your pack - only what you can
-- sell. Laying that out is a different problem with a different answer:
-- the gaps are in different places, so anything with no square yet
-- lands somewhere that made sense among the sellable items and nowhere
-- near where it can go among all of them.
--
-- Writing those positions down is what re-sorted the inventory on the
-- way out of a trader, every single time. The stash never had the
-- problem because there your pack is the same container, whole.
--
-- So the trade view READS the layout and never writes it. An explicit
-- drag is still remembered wherever it is made - that is the player
-- saying where a thing goes, rather than a window guessing.
-- ============================================================
local function writes_layout(id)
	return id == "actor_bag"
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE GUTTER, AND WHAT EACH SECTION LOOKS LIKE
--
-- Two columns down the left of the grid belong to nobody. They held
-- the section icons; the icons are gone -
--
--   "remove all icons"
--
-- - and the two columns stay, because the BACKPACK SLOT stands in them
-- and there is nowhere else it can stand. See G.pack_slot.
--
-- IT COSTS TWO COLUMNS OF STORAGE, and it has to: the cells the window
-- draws are on the same grid as everything else, so the only way to
-- keep a strip clear is to own it. G.take_dead marks it taken so the
-- window's own packing cannot use it either. If a panel is too narrow
-- to give up the room, this shrinks to whatever fits rather than
-- pushing the sections off the right-hand edge.
-- ============================================================
local GUTTER = 2

-- ...but never so far that the grid cannot hold the widest thing in the
-- game. A rifle is five cells across; a panel with only five columns
-- has nothing to spare and gets no gutter at all rather than a grid
-- that can no longer take a rifle. Between the two, it gives what it
-- can.
local MIN_USABLE = 5

-- What each section is CALLED on screen. The zone's own name is what
-- the code keys on and the player never sees it; this is their word
-- for it - "pockets milkysh pink" - and the two are allowed to differ.
-- ============================================================
-- WHAT A SECTION IS DRAWN AS
--
--   "for backpack remove the green border and header, same for
--    pouches, just make a thin grey border and subtle grid
--    visualization"
--
-- It used to be four two-pixel lines in the section's own colour, a
-- dimmed bar in the row above it, and a word on the bar - BACKPACK in
-- dark green, POCKETS in milky pink. All of that is gone.
--
-- ONE GREY FOR THE EDGE and a fainter one for the squares inside it,
-- both measured off the picture that was asked for: a border of about
-- seventy-five and a division of about forty-five, over an inventory
-- background in the high twenties. The border is a line you can
-- follow; the division is a line you can find when you look for it.
--
-- NO COLOUR PER SECTION any more. There is only ever one section on
-- screen - the backpack, or the pockets you have without one - so a
-- colour that told them apart was telling you something you already
-- knew from what was in it.
local EDGE_CLR = { 255, 74, 77, 82 }

-- ============================================================
-- THE DARK GROUND UNDER THE SQUARES
--
--   "i need so the backpack grid also have this dark background the
--    mag slots have"
--
-- These are the pockets' own numbers, from A.pocket_clr: a rifle
-- pocket's fill is 22, 22, 26 behind a border of 86, 86, 94. Against
-- the inventory's own background in the high twenties that is a small
-- step down, which is the point - a recessed panel, not a black hole.
--
-- ONE COLOUR, like the border above it and for the same reason: there
-- is only ever one section on screen.
-- ============================================================
local FILL_CLR = { 255, 22, 22, 26 }

-- How many sections can be given a ground. G.zones has exactly ONE
-- band() call site, in an if/else, so there is never more than one on
-- screen; four is room for three more without anyone having to think
-- about the note in G.make_fill again, and a fifth section would go
-- unfilled rather than be drawn on top of the items.
local FILL_MAX = 4
local CELL_CLR = { 255, 44, 46, 50 }

-- How thick the four lines round a section are, in pixels. ONE, which
-- is what "thin" means at this size: two reads as a frame, one reads as
-- an edge.
local EDGE = 1

-- ...and the divisions inside it, which are thinner than the border can
-- be. One pixel is the floor, so what makes them fainter is the colour.
local CELL_EDGE = 1

-- THE HEADER'S ROW IS STILL A ROW, and still empty. Nothing is drawn
-- in it any more - the bar and the caption are gone - but it is the
-- half-cell of air between one section and the next, and every square
-- in the column is numbered downwards from the top of it. Taking the
-- row out would move every item in every save.

local VERSION = "0.65.0-test"

-- THE BUILTINS ARE PINNED. A global is a hash lookup every time it is
-- named; an upvalue is an array index. These five are named on every
-- hot path in the file - each redraw, each sync, each drag - so they
-- are read out of _G exactly once, here. Found by Priler's ALAO
-- linter, which flagged every uncached use; this one line answers
-- all of them.
local type, tostring, tonumber, pairs, ipairs
    = type, tostring, tonumber, pairs, ipairs

-- Bumped on every build that leaves here, written to the log at load.
-- The same discipline as the parent mod: "which version is actually
-- installed" is never a guess.

local DEFAULTS = {
	grid_enable  = true,

	-- ============================================================
	-- A THING MAY LIE ON ITS SIDE TO GET IN
	--
	--   "sometimes i try to pickup ammo that takes 2x1 but i dont have
	--    that slot in horizontal orientation ... it will be good if
	--    items when quick looting or picking up from the ground will
	--    rotate themselves if needed to fit."
	--
	-- Only ever as a LAST resort - see find_bounded - so nothing that
	-- fits as it is is ever turned. On, because the alternative is your
	-- ammunition on the ground.
	-- ============================================================
	grid_autorot = true,

	-- ============================================================
	-- A RED GROUND UNDER WHAT WILL NOT FIT
	--
	--   "to bodies and stashes add also a red highlight under items
	--    that could not fit into inventory in its current state (to
	--    show player if he needs to make some room)."
	--
	-- In the OTHER side's list, so it answers the question you are
	-- actually asking while you look at a corpse: what of this can I
	-- take. On, because a mark you did not ask for costs a glance and
	-- an item on the floor costs a walk back.
	-- ============================================================
	grid_mark_full = true,

	-- ============================================================
	-- ...AND WHAT HAPPENS WHEN IT DOES NOT FIT
	--
	--   "drop underplayer (the current one) / block pickup/loot (item
	--    stays where it is)"
	--
	-- "drop" is what has always happened. "block" refuses it at the
	-- door: a loot move is refused outright - the game has a callback
	-- for exactly that - and a pickup is put straight back down,
	-- because CInventory::Take is the engine and Lua has no veto over
	-- it.
	-- ============================================================
	grid_full   = "drop",


	-- The numbers under test. Flat on purpose - no tiering until the
	-- idea has earned it.
	grid_pack    = 70,

	-- WHAT YOU HAVE WITH NO BACKPACK ON, and only then. Eight squares
	-- is deliberately not enough to play out of - it is enough to pick
	-- a backpack up off the ground after dropping one by accident,
	-- which is the whole job it now has.
	grid_bare    = 8,

	-- THE RIG GRANTS NO CELLS. It is for magazines and nothing else -
	-- its pockets are Armor Mag Pouches' own, drawn in the block at the
	-- top of this column. A rig that also granted general storage was
	-- two ideas about the same item.

	-- THE WIDTH IS THE REAL RULE.
	--
	-- A long gun is five cells wide. A four-wide grid therefore cannot
	-- hold one, whatever its total - so "no backpack, no looted rifle"
	-- falls out of the shape and needs no code of its own. Widen this
	-- to 5 and that rule quietly disappears.
	grid_w_bare  = 4,
	grid_w_pack  = 7,


	-- Off makes this diagnostic rather than a rule: the grid still
	-- stops, but what will not fit is simply not shown rather than put
	-- on the ground. Useful for seeing how close you are; not a way to
	-- play.
	grid_drop    = true,

	-- The edge of the usable rectangle, drawn as four thin lines. There
	-- is no other way to see where the grid stops.
	grid_border  = true,

	-- ============================================================
	-- ONE THING, ONE SQUARE
	--
	--   "we need to make the items to not be able to be stacked. only
	--    ammo have kinda stacks but also is shown in one spot, so ...
	--    ammo is buyable in stacks of 10 bullets and i buy two stacks of
	--    10 then it is shown as 1 stack of 20 now. i want so there is
	--    two stacks of 10."
	--
	-- The window puts identical things in the SAME cell and writes a
	-- count on it - two boxes of ten read as one twenty. That is fine
	-- for a list and wrong for a grid, where the whole point is that a
	-- thing takes up room.
	--
	-- OFF by default, which is what was asked for. On, the window does
	-- what it always did; the option is here because "later we will add
	-- some stacks for some items like bandages" and this is the switch
	-- that argument will be had over.
	-- ON now, and the ceiling is what makes that safe: a section that
	-- names no ceiling still gets one square per item, exactly as
	-- before. See zzz_grid_stacks.ltx.
	grid_stack   = true,

	-- Every ceiling in that file, scaled, for a player who wants
	-- tighter or looser stacking without editing it. Per cent.
	grid_stack_mult = 100,

	-- The tenth-of-condition rule for food and drink. OFF: everything
	-- of a kind stacks whatever its freshness - "let everything stack
	-- no matter the % even meat". On: a fresh tin and a rotten one
	-- keep their own squares.
	grid_stack_fresh = false,

	-- A soft ground behind anything you have just picked up, so a thing
	-- taken without looking can still be found. Spent the moment the
	-- cursor passes over it. On by default.
	grid_new_glow = true,

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE KEY THAT TAKES ONE OFF A STACK YOU ARE HOLDING
	--
	--   "when a stack of items is held and i tap shift one of the items
	--    from stack gets placed when the highlight shows, and clicking
	--    shift in the same spot drops one more and one more there"
	--
	-- A number, because that is what the game calls a key. Left shift
	-- by default; changeable in MCM.
	--
	-- Asked of DIK_keys rather than written as 42, and 42 only if that
	-- table is not there yet - this file's chunk runs early and a nil
	-- here would be a keybind of nil, which is every key and no key.
	-- ============================================================
	grid_split_key = (function()
		local t = rawget(_G, "DIK_keys")
		local v = t and t.DIK_LSHIFT
		return (type(v) == "number") and v or 42
	end)(),

	-- ============================================================
	-- DEBUG BALANCING
	--
	--   "add option in ZONEGRID 'DebugBalancing' while this in ON then
	--    i could change every item max stack in inventory."
	--
	-- Off, and off for everybody who is not balancing: it puts a line
	-- on screen for whatever the cursor is over, which is the last
	-- thing you want while playing. What it does NOT do is change a
	-- number - see the top of layout/balance.py for why the file stays
	-- the only place a ceiling comes from.
	-- ============================================================
	grid_balance = false,

	grid_debug   = false,
}

-- ============================================================
-- HOUSEKEEPING
-- ============================================================
local function fmt_safe(fmt, ...)
	local ok, s = pcall(string.format, fmt, ...)
	return ok and s or tostring(fmt)
end

local function log(fmt, ...)
	printf("[GRID] %s", fmt_safe(fmt, ...))
end

local G = {
	enabled = DEFAULTS.grid_enable,
	pack    = DEFAULTS.grid_pack,
	bare    = DEFAULTS.grid_bare,
	w_bare  = DEFAULTS.grid_w_bare,
	w_pack  = DEFAULTS.grid_w_pack,
	drop    = DEFAULTS.grid_drop,
	border  = DEFAULTS.grid_border,
	stack   = DEFAULTS.grid_stack,
	stack_mult = DEFAULTS.grid_stack_mult / 100,
	split_key = DEFAULTS.grid_split_key,

	-- The ceilings, read once from zzz_grid_stacks.ltx, and the answer
	-- per section worked out from them and kept.
	stk     = nil,
	stk_memo = {},
	debug   = DEFAULTS.grid_debug,

	-- WHERE EVERY ITEM IS. [id] = {x=, y=, w=, h=} in grid cells.
	--
	-- This is the whole of "it does not re-sort itself". The window
	-- rebuilds its cells from scratch every time it opens, every time
	-- you loot, and every time you sell something - and packs them in
	-- sort order, which is why a bounded grid alone still shuffled
	-- everything each time you looked at it.
	pos     = {},

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND WHICH OF THOSE SQUARES THE PLAYER CHOSE
	--
	-- [id] = true for a thing put where it is BY HAND: nudged, dropped
	-- there, or tapped off a stack. The tidying leaves those alone -
	-- it gathers what nobody has arranged, and nothing else.
	--
	-- Without this the gathering wins every argument. Two piles of
	-- bandages cannot exist, because the next rebuild collects the
	-- second one back into the first - which is what "shift clicking
	-- dont work but something happes" was: it worked, and was undone
	-- between one frame and the next.
	-- ============================================================
	byhand  = {},

	-- WHICH WAY AN ITEM LIES. id -> true means sideways: its footprint
	-- is its section's, swapped. Written by the drops and by the
	-- sibling mod's reserve, saved with the layout, cleared with it.
	rot     = {},

	-- The four lines round the edge, one set per container we draw in.
	edge    = {},

	-- (The dark ground under the squares, the fresh glow, the drop
	-- highlight, the box wash and the red grounds all used to live
	-- here, keyed by container ID. They live on the container itself
	-- now - cc.g_fill and its four siblings - because a window builds
	-- several containers with the same ID and only one of them is on
	-- screen. See G.make_fill.)

	-- Ids the window could not find room for. Dropped on the next
	-- update rather than inside the draw pass that noticed them: the
	-- window is walking its own cells at that moment and removing an
	-- object from under it is how a UI mod crashes a game.
	queued  = {},

	last    = 0,

	-- The bag panel's column count, as last seen. nil until the window
	-- has been opened once.
	panel_cols = nil,

	-- Ids that have arrived and not yet been looked at. id -> true,
	-- set by G.on_take, spent by a hover, forgotten when the item
	-- leaves. In memory only - a reload starts with nothing new.
	fresh   = {},

	-- What fits in your bag right now, by section, thrown away on a
	-- throttle - see G.would_fit.
	fit_memo = {},

	-- The section captions, one text window per zone per container.
	txt     = {},

	-- What was last written to each static this file owns. Every
	-- rectangle here is re-stated on every frame, and a SetWndPos on a
	-- window inside a scroll view is not free.
	stm     = {},

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHAT WAS IN A LIST BEFORE THE WINDOW REFRESHED IT
	--
	--   "the fps drop for him when looting bodies but stay fine in
	--    stashes and vendors"
	--
	-- One set of object ids per container, and the size of it. Filled
	-- before the window's own refresh and compared after: if the same
	-- ids are there, the refresh added and removed nothing and there
	-- is nothing for this mod to lay out again. See G.install_ui.
	--
	-- Kept here and REUSED rather than made per call, because the path
	-- it is on runs several times a second while a corpse is open,
	-- which is the whole problem being fixed.
	-- ============================================================
	was     = {},
	nwas    = {},

	-- How many rebuilds that comparison has saved this session. Said
	-- once, on the way out of the window.
	idle    = 0,
}

local function dbg(fmt, ...)
	if G.debug then log(fmt, ...) end
end

local function clampi(v, lo, hi)
	v = tonumber(v) or lo
	if v < lo then return lo end
	if v > hi then return hi end
	return v
end

local function opt_get(id, fallback)
	if not ui_mcm then return fallback end
	local ok, v = pcall(ui_mcm.get, MOD .. "/" .. GROUP .. "/" .. id)
	if not ok or v == nil then return fallback end
	return v
end

-- ui_mcm.get returns BARE for a path it does not know, and a call in
-- the last argument position expands to all of its return values - so
-- tonumber(ui_mcm.get(p)) is tonumber() with no argument, which raises
-- in Lua 5.1. Assign first, convert after. (Learned in the parent mod.)
local function opt_num(id, fallback)
	local v = opt_get(id, fallback)
	return tonumber(v) or fallback
end

-- Bumped on every settings change, so anything that caches an answer
-- derived from them can notice with one comparison. See G.zones.
G.setgen = G.setgen or 0

-- ============================================================
-- HOW MUCH OF THE FRAME THIS MOD IS
--
-- The sibling mod's A.clock_in / A.clock_out, in the same words and
-- for the same reason: every guess about what a draw costs in this
-- engine has been arithmetic on call counts, and the only honest
-- answer is a clock in the running game.
--
-- One frame reads as 0 or 1 ms and means nothing; a hundred and twenty
-- of them mean a great deal. Debug only.
-- ============================================================
function G.clock_in()
	if not G.debug then return nil end
	return time_global()
end

function G.clock_out(t0)
	if not (G.debug and t0) then return end

	G.ms_sum = (G.ms_sum or 0) + (time_global() - t0)
	G.ms_n = (G.ms_n or 0) + 1

	if G.ms_n >= 120 then
		log("the grid used %s ms over %s frames", tostring(G.ms_sum),
		    tostring(G.ms_n))
		G.ms_sum, G.ms_n = 0, 0
	end
end

function G.refresh_settings()
	G.setgen = (G.setgen or 0) + 1
	G.enabled = opt_get("grid_enable", DEFAULTS.grid_enable) and true or false
	G.drop    = opt_get("grid_drop",   DEFAULTS.grid_drop)   and true or false
	-- Asked of MCM, and NOW DECLARED THERE. It was read here and never
	-- registered, which put "!MCM given bad path:
	-- AmpGrid/grid_general/grid_border" in the log on every start - an
	-- error line of mine, in a log people send me to read.
	G.border  = opt_get("grid_border", DEFAULTS.grid_border) and true or false
	G.stack   = opt_get("grid_stack",  DEFAULTS.grid_stack)  and true or false
	G.stack_fresh = opt_get("grid_stack_fresh", DEFAULTS.grid_stack_fresh) and true or false
	G.new_glow = opt_get("grid_new_glow", DEFAULTS.grid_new_glow) and true or false

	-- Whether an arriving item may lie on its side to fit. On, because
	-- the alternative is your ammunition on the ground; off for anybody
	-- who would rather see the gap and decide themselves.
	G.autorot = opt_get("grid_autorot", DEFAULTS.grid_autorot) and true or false
	G.mark_full = opt_get("grid_mark_full", DEFAULTS.grid_mark_full) and true or false

	-- ONE OF THE TWO OR THE DEFAULT. A value this file does not know
	-- would decide nothing and mean anything.
	local full = opt_get("grid_full", DEFAULTS.grid_full)
	G.full = (full == "block" or full == "drop") and full or DEFAULTS.grid_full

	-- The answers about what fits are about a bag that has just
	-- changed, so they go.
	G.fit_memo, G.fit_at = {}, nil

	-- A ceiling is a number of items, so the multiplier is applied
	-- where the ceiling is worked out - and every answer already worked
	-- out is thrown away, because it was worked out with the old one.
	G.stack_mult = clampi(opt_num("grid_stack_mult",
	                              DEFAULTS.grid_stack_mult), 25, 400) / 100
	G.stk_memo = {}
	-- WHAT ANSWERED, thrown away with the answers. Kept beside them
	-- rather than rebuilt on demand, so the readout cannot describe a
	-- ceiling the packer is no longer using.
	G.stk_why = {}
	G.bal_seen = {}
	G.pk_memo = {}
	G.pk = nil

	-- A key is a number and nothing else. A nil here would match every
	-- key the window ever sees.
	local k = opt_num("grid_split_key", DEFAULTS.grid_split_key)
	G.split_key = (type(k) == "number" and k > 0) and k or DEFAULTS.grid_split_key
	-- ...and the copy the other mod reads. One assignment, beside the
	-- one it mirrors, so the two cannot be changed apart.
	split_key = G.split_key
	G.debug   = opt_get("grid_debug",  DEFAULTS.grid_debug)  and true or false

	G.balance = opt_get("grid_balance", DEFAULTS.grid_balance) and true or false

	G.pack   = clampi(opt_num("grid_pack",   DEFAULTS.grid_pack),   0, 400)
	G.bare   = clampi(opt_num("grid_bare",   DEFAULTS.grid_bare),   1, 400)
	G.w_bare = clampi(opt_num("grid_w_bare", DEFAULTS.grid_w_bare), 2, 20)
	G.w_pack = clampi(opt_num("grid_w_pack", DEFAULTS.grid_w_pack), 2, 20)

	log("%s cells, pack %s, bare %s, width %s/%s, drop %s",
	    G.enabled and "on" or "off", tostring(G.pack),
	    tostring(G.bare), tostring(G.w_bare), tostring(G.w_pack),
	    tostring(G.drop))
end

-- ============================================================
-- TWO SECTIONS OF ONE GUN
--
-- A scope or a magazine adapter swaps the weapon's SECTION -
-- wpn_udp9 becomes wpn_udp9_e0t2_magd and back - destroying one
-- object and creating another a beat later, and every table this mod
-- keys by id loses the thread. Kinship is judged by name: a shared
-- prefix at least eight characters long that covers most of the
-- shorter name. Two different rifles can be kin by this rule; the
-- claim below is also gated on a three-second window and a departed
-- square, so the misfire needs a drop and a pickup of near-twins
-- inside one breath.
-- ============================================================
function G.kin(a, b)
	if type(a) ~= "string" or type(b) ~= "string" then return false end
	if a == b then return true end
	local n = math.min(#a, #b)
	local i = 0
	while i < n and a:byte(i + 1) == b:byte(i + 1) do i = i + 1 end
	return i >= 8 and i * 10 >= n * 6
end

-- The squares of the recently departed, waiting for a kin. Written
-- by G.on_drop, claimed in find_bounded, peeked by G.on_take.
G.legacy = G.legacy or {}

function G.legacy_find(sec, peek)
	local now = time_global()
	for i = #G.legacy, 1, -1 do
		local L = G.legacy[i]
		if now - L.at > 3000 then
			table.remove(G.legacy, i)
		elseif G.kin(L.sec, sec) then
			if not peek then table.remove(G.legacy, i) end
			return L
		end
	end
	return nil
end

-- ============================================================
-- HOW BIG A THING IS
--
-- The game already knows, and has always known: every item declares
-- how many cells wide and tall its icon is, and the inventory packs
-- them by those numbers. That is why this mod is small - the spatial
-- part of a spatial inventory is already running.
--
-- inv_grid_scale is the HD icon framework's doing: an icon drawn at
-- twice the resolution declares twice the cells and a scale of 2. The
-- window divides, so this divides too, or every item in a high
-- resolution icon pack would be four times its real size.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- ONCE PER SECTION, EVER
--
-- THREE ENGINE CALLS, and this is asked once per CELL per frame by
-- G.dress_cells and again by the hover, the hold and every placement -
-- for three numbers out of system.ltx that cannot change while the
-- game is running. A bag of sixty things was a hundred and eighty
-- engine calls a frame for an answer that was the same every time.
--
-- The sibling mod has done this since 1.x (A.memo_cells) and the note
-- there says why; this file simply never got it.
--
-- TWO NUMBERS, so the memo holds a pair. It is never handed out - the
-- caller gets the numbers, not the table - so nobody can write into
-- the cache by accident.
-- ============================================================
G.szmemo = G.szmemo or {}

function G.size_of(sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return nil end

	local had = G.szmemo[sec]
	if had ~= nil then
		if had == false then return nil end
		return had[1], had[2]
	end

	local w = SYS_GetParam(2, sec, "inv_grid_width")
	local h = SYS_GetParam(2, sec, "inv_grid_height")
	if not (type(w) == "number" and type(h) == "number") then
		G.szmemo[sec] = false
		return nil
	end

	local scale = SYS_GetParam(2, sec, "inv_grid_scale") or 1
	if type(scale) ~= "number" or scale <= 0 then scale = 1 end

	w = math.ceil(w / scale)
	h = math.ceil(h / scale)
	if w < 1 then w = 1 end
	if h < 1 then h = 1 end

	G.szmemo[sec] = { w, h }
	return w, h
end

-- ...and the same answer for a PARTICULAR item: swapped when the rot
-- table says it lies sideways. Every path that knows an id asks this
-- one instead.
function G.size_for(id, sec)
	local w, h = G.size_of(sec)
	if w and id and G.rot[id] and w ~= h then return h, w end
	return w, h
end

-- THE KEY IS THE SIBLING MOD'S. It owns the MCM binding and the
-- edge-detected poll; this asks whether the hand is currently turned
-- and never reads a keyboard of its own, so there is exactly one key.
function G.hand_turned()
	local amp = rawget(_G, "zzz_armor_mag_pouches")
	local f = amp and amp.grid_hand_turned
	if type(f) ~= "function" then return false end
	local ok, v = pcall(f)
	return (ok and v) and true or false
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT YOU ARE WEARING, AND THEREFORE WHAT YOU HAVE
--
--   "the items that provide the inventory just add up to one invnetory
--    instead of haveing their own seperate secions like in tarkov"
--
-- Quite right, and 0.1 said so in its own notes: the cells were added
-- together into one rectangle because separate containers looked like
-- a rebuild of the inventory window.
--
-- They are not. A ZONE is a band of rows with its own width, and one
-- grid can hold several of them stacked - which is what the window
-- draws anyway. Nothing may straddle a boundary, each zone is drawn
-- with its own edge, and each has the width its own container should
-- have:
--
--     your pockets      4 wide      no rifle will ever go in them
--     your backpack     7 wide      rifles live here
--
-- Only ever one of them at a time, now: the pockets are what you have
-- when no backpack is on. The zone machinery is kept whole because the
-- shape of it is what makes "nothing may straddle a boundary" a rule
-- rather than a drawing, and a second section is one line away.
--
-- So they are separate places that happen to share one panel. The
-- pieces that are still missing are a header on each and the ability
-- to drag between them and see it mean something - and those need the
-- window rebuilt, which this still is not.
--
-- Slot 7 is the outfit and slot 13 the backpack, the same two the
-- magazine system reads for its own loadout.
-- ============================================================
-- How many columns the icons actually get, on this panel.
function G.gutter()
	local want = GUTTER
	local panel = G.panel_cols

	if type(panel) == "number" and panel > 0 then
		local room = panel - MIN_USABLE
		if room < 0 then room = 0 end
		if want > room then want = room end
	end

	return want
end

-- ============================================================
-- A SLOT THAT IS EMPTY FOR A MOMENT IS NOT AN EMPTY SLOT
--
--   "when animations for backpaks are ON the character unequipes the
--    backpacks throws whole inventory onto the ground and equips new
--    backpack, with this fdda animation for backpacks OFF the backpacks
--    swap instant and it works like it should"
--
-- Swapping instantly is one event. With the animation on it is two,
-- seconds apart, and BETWEEN THEM SLOT 13 IS EMPTY - so this file saw
-- no backpack, gave you the eight squares you have without one, and the
-- tick put the rest of your inventory on the ground.
--
-- The same mistake as the rig block that had not finished drawing: a
-- transient read taken for a settled fact. Same answer, too - keep the
-- last real one until it has been gone long enough to believe.
--
-- FOUR SECONDS is longer than any swap animation and short enough that
-- taking the pack off on purpose still shrinks the grid while you are
-- looking at it. Being wrong this way costs a grid that stays big for a
-- moment; being wrong the other way costs your inventory.
--
-- ANSWERED AS TWO THINGS, worn and which, because the size now depends
-- on WHICH - and during the gap the honest answer to "which" is the one
-- that was there a moment ago.
-- ============================================================
local PACK_GRACE = 4000

function G.pack_worn()
	local actor = db.actor
	if not actor then return false, nil end

	local ok, pack = pcall(function() return actor:item_in_slot(13) end)

	if ok and pack then
		local sec
		pcall(function() sec = pack:section() end)
		G.pack_seen = { sec = sec, at = time_global() }
		return true, sec
	end

	-- EMPTY. Which is only news if it has been empty for a while.
	local seen = G.pack_seen
	if seen and type(seen.at) == "number" then
		local gone = time_global() - seen.at
		if gone >= 0 and gone < PACK_GRACE then
			return true, seen.sec
		end
	end

	G.pack_seen = nil
	return false, nil
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE SHAPE OF THE GRID, WORKED OUT WHEN IT CHANGES
--
-- Every drawing pass asks for this, and there are six of them per
-- container per frame - the bounds, the hover, the fresh marks, the
-- capacity, the placement. Each ask built the list again, allocated a
-- band table per section and then a SIGNATURE STRING by concatenating
-- four numbers per section on top.
--
-- Three things decide the answer: the backpack you are wearing, the
-- gutter, and how many rows the sibling mod's rig block is standing
-- on. All three are cheap to ask, so they are the key - and the fourth
-- is the settings, which bump a counter when they change rather than
-- being compared field by field.
--
-- THE SAME TABLE IS HANDED BACK. Everything that reads a zone list
-- walks it and nothing writes into it; and `sig`, which callers
-- compare across frames to notice a change, is the same string when
-- nothing has changed - which is the answer it was always meant to
-- give.
-- ============================================================
function G.zones()
	local actor = db.actor
	if not actor then return nil end

	local gut = G.gutter()

	-- NOT item_in_slot DIRECTLY. See G.pack_worn: a backpack being
	-- swapped is out of its slot for a second or two, and this file
	-- must not read that as having no backpack at all.
	local worn, psec = G.pack_worn()

	local z = G.zcache
	if z and G.zc_sec == psec and G.zc_gut == gut
	   and G.zc_gen == G.setgen and G.zc_head == G.head_rows() then
		return z
	end

	-- THE TOP OF THE COLUMN IS NOT OURS. The rig slot and the rig's own
	-- pockets are drawn there by Armor Mag Pouches, in the same
	-- scrolling content these rows live in - so they go up together
	-- when you scroll, which is the whole point. Nothing is ever placed
	-- in between.
	local out, y = {}, G.head_rows() + 1

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHAT EACH SECTION NOW OCCUPIES
	--
	--   * a row above it for its HEADER. The bar is drawn in the lower
	--     half of that row, so the empty upper half is the half-cell of
	--     air between one section and the next - spacing that costs a
	--     row of the grid rather than a fudge in the drawing, so an
	--     item can never sit in it.
	--   * a GUTTER of columns down the left, shared by every section,
	--     with the section's icon in it.
	--
	-- Both are held empty in the container's own occupancy map as well
	-- as here - see G.take_dead - so the window's packing cannot use
	-- them either.
	-- ============================================================
	-- NO COLOUR ARGUMENT. A section used to carry its own, and there
	-- is one grey now - see EDGE_CLR. Leaving the parameter would leave
	-- two call sites passing something nothing reads, which is how a
	-- colour comes back half way.
	local function band(cells, cols, name)
		if cells <= 0 or cols <= 0 then return end
		local rows = math.ceil(cells / cols)
		if rows < 1 then rows = 1 end

		y = y + 1                              -- the header's row
		out[#out + 1] = { name = name, y0 = y, rows = rows, cols = cols,
		                  x0 = gut + 1 }
		y = y + rows
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- ONE SECTION, AND WHICH ONE DEPENDS ON THE BACKPACK
	--
	-- The armour used to grant cells and the pockets used to be a floor
	-- under everything. Neither is true now:
	--
	--   "remove that armour gives inventory space as well as pouches
	--    that are always there"
	--
	-- The backpack is the whole of your storage. THE POCKETS ARE THE
	-- WAY BACK from having none, and nothing more:
	--
	--   "if backpack is dropped by accident thats an issue, so give the
	--    8x4 cells when there is no backpack to user can pick it up.
	--    but they are gone when backpack is equipped."
	--
	-- So they are an ELSE, not an extra. Four columns wide, which is
	-- the rule that was always doing the work: a long gun is five
	-- across, so no rifle goes in your pockets and that needs no code.
	-- ============================================================
	if worn then
		-- THE PACK'S OWN SIZE, not one number for all of them. See
		-- G.pack_cells: this exact section, then its kind, then what the
		-- pack itself says it lets you carry.
		band(G.pack_cells(psec), G.w_pack, "backpack")
	else
		band(G.bare, G.w_bare, "pouches")
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHAT MAKES A LIST OF SQUARES STALE
	--
	-- G.squares is a pure function of these numbers and the footprint
	-- it is asked about - the sections' tops, heights, widths and left
	-- edges, and nothing else. Written here, next to the arithmetic
	-- that produced them, so a section that ever grows a fifth number
	-- is a section whose signature is obviously short.
	--
	-- A STRING, NOT A COUNTER. A counter has to be bumped by whoever
	-- changes the shape, and the shape is changed by a backpack going
	-- on, an outfit coming off, a slider in the options menu and a
	-- level load - four places to remember and one to forget. This
	-- cannot be forgotten: it is derived from the answer itself.
	--
	-- It is a KEY on the list, not an entry in it. Everything that
	-- reads a zone list walks it with `for i = 1, #z`, so a named
	-- field is invisible to all of them.
	-- ============================================================
	local sig = ""
	for i = 1, #out do
		local b = out[i]
		sig = sig .. b.y0 .. "," .. b.rows .. "," .. b.cols .. "," .. b.x0 .. "/"
	end
	out.sig = sig

	G.zcache = out
	G.zc_sec, G.zc_gut, G.zc_gen = psec, gut, G.setgen
	G.zc_head = G.head_rows()

	return out
end

-- The whole grid: as wide as its widest zone and as tall as all of
-- them. The window needs one rectangle even when the rules inside it
-- are not.
function G.capacity()
	local z = G.zones()
	if not z then return nil end

	-- MEASURED FROM THE LAST ROW ANY SECTION USES, rather than by adding
	-- their heights up: there are rows between them now - a header each,
	-- and the rig block's above the first - and a sum would leave the
	-- bottom of the grid short by every one of them.
	local cols, rows, cells = 0, 0, 0
	for i = 1, #z do
		local right = z[i].x0 + z[i].cols - 1
		if right > cols then cols = right end

		local bottom = z[i].y0 + z[i].rows - 1
		if bottom > rows then rows = bottom end

		cells = cells + z[i].rows * z[i].cols
	end
	return cols, rows, cells
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHERE THE BACKPACK SLOT STANDS
--
--   "i want the back pack in the middle collumn where the green icon
--    is. its still next to pda and detector. in equipment collumn"
--
-- The green icon is the backpack section's own glyph, in the gutter,
-- level with the top of the section. That is exactly where the slot
-- belongs and the reasons are already true: the gutter is two columns
-- nothing can be placed in, the glyph is already two cells square, and
-- it is drawn in the SCROLLING content - so it goes up and down with
-- the list without anyone telling it to, which is what was asked for.
--
-- WHEN NOTHING IS WORN THERE IS NO SECTION, so there is no glyph, and
-- so there would be nowhere to drop a backpack to put one on. The
-- answer is in two halves: the section's own row when there is one, and
-- the row under the last section when there is not. Either way it is
-- the same two squares of gutter and it is always somewhere.
--
-- ANSWERED IN THE CONTENT'S OWN PIXELS. The caller draws it, and what
-- it needs is where to put a rectangle relative to the corner the first
-- row is drawn from - not a cell number it would have to convert with
-- the same three constants all over again.
--
-- NO GUTTER, NO SLOT. On a panel too narrow to spare the columns the
-- icons are already skipped rather than squashed (see G.gutter), and a
-- slot drawn on top of the first column of storage would be worse than
-- one that is not drawn.
-- ============================================================
function G.pack_slot(cc)
	if not (G.enabled and cc) then return nil end
	if G.gutter() < 2 then return nil end

	local size = cc.grid_size and cc.grid_line and (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line)
	if not (type(size) == "number" and size > 0) then return nil end

	local z = G.zones()
	if not (z and #z > 0) then return nil end

	local row
	for i = 1, #z do
		if z[i].name == "backpack" then row = z[i].y0 break end
	end

	if not row then
		-- THE ROW THE BACKPACK SECTION WOULD HAVE, which is the first
		-- section's - because there is only ever one section and the
		-- backpack replaces it rather than joining it. So the empty
		-- slot stands exactly where the full one will, and putting a
		-- backpack on does not make the slot jump; the section under it
		-- changes from the pockets to the pack and the slot does not
		-- move at all.
		--
		-- It does not collide with the pockets either: this is the
		-- GUTTER, two columns no section is ever given.
		row = z[1].y0
	end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (type(ratio) == "number" and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	-- The width carries the aspect correction and the height does not,
	-- the same way every other rectangle in this file is measured.
	return 0, (row - 1) * size, 2 * size * ratio, 2 * size
end

-- AN EMPTY BACKPACK SLOT SHOWS NOTHING. It used to borrow the
-- backpack section's glyph; there are no glyphs now, and the picture
-- that goes there is being drawn:
--
--   "the backpack ill make and provide later now make the slot empty"
--
-- Kept as a function that answers nil rather than deleted, because
-- Armor Mag Pouches calls it across the door between the two files and
-- one nil is a cheaper contract than a missing name.
function G.pack_glyph()
	return nil
end

-- Which zone a row belongs to, and whether a rectangle placed there
-- lies wholly inside it. THE STRADDLE IS THE WHOLE POINT: without this
-- the zones are a drawing and not a rule.
function G.zone_of(z, row)
	for i = 1, #z do
		if row >= z[i].y0 and row < z[i].y0 + z[i].rows then return z[i] end
	end
	return nil
end

-- Does ANY square of this footprint stand inside a section? "None"
-- means the drag is over dead panel - the gutter, the space below the
-- last section - and a rectangle there would mark squares the player
-- could never use. G.fits asks the stricter question (fully inside);
-- the highlight needs both.
function G.touches(z, row, col, w, h, panel_cols)
	if not (z and row and col and w and h) then return false end

	for r = row, row + h - 1 do
		local band = G.zone_of(z, r)
		if band then
			local right = band.x0 + band.cols - 1
			if panel_cols and right > panel_cols then right = panel_cols end
			if col <= right and col + w - 1 >= band.x0 then return true end
		end
	end
	return false
end

function G.fits(z, row, col, w, h, panel_cols)
	if not (z and row and col and w and h) then return false end
	if row < 1 or col < 1 then return false end

	local band = G.zone_of(z, row)
	if not band then return false end

	-- THE LEFT EDGE IS THE SECTION'S, NOT THE PANEL'S. Column one is
	-- the gutter the icons live in.
	if col < band.x0 then return false end

	local right = band.x0 + band.cols - 1
	if panel_cols and right > panel_cols then right = panel_cols end

	if col + w - 1 > right then return false end
	if row + h - 1 > band.y0 + band.rows - 1 then return false end
	return true
end

-- Every square of the grid, zone by zone, top-left first. One place
-- that knows the order, so the window, the drag and the simulation all
-- agree about where the next thing goes.
local function build_squares(z, w, h, panel_cols)
	local out = {}
	for i = 1, #z do
		local band = z[i]
		local right = band.x0 + band.cols - 1
		if panel_cols and right > panel_cols then right = panel_cols end

		for r = band.y0, band.y0 + band.rows - h do
			for c = band.x0, right - w + 1 do
				out[#out + 1] = { r = r, c = c }
			end
		end
	end
	return out
end

-- ============================================================
-- ...AND WORKED OUT ONCE PER SHAPE
--
-- This is the most-called allocating function in the file. Every
-- rebuild of the window runs find_bounded once per item and each call
-- built the whole list again - ninety small tables for a full pack,
-- made and thrown away, per item, per rebuild. The red mark's sweep
-- through a corpse does it per item too, and so does the simulation
-- that runs once a second whether or not anything is open.
--
-- The list depends on the zones' shape, the panel's width and the
-- footprint asked about. All three are in the key, so a hit is the
-- same answer the long way would have given.
--
-- THE RETURNED LIST IS SHARED AND MUST NOT BE WRITTEN TO. Every caller
-- reads sq[i].r and sq[i].c and none of them touches it - which is a
-- property of six call sites today and a rule from here on.
--
-- A HAND-BUILT ZONE LIST IS NEVER MEMOISED. Without a signature there
-- is nothing to tell two of them apart, and answering one with the
-- other's squares would be the worst kind of wrong. No signature, no
-- memo - it is built the long way and nothing is kept.
-- ============================================================
function G.squares(z, w, h, panel_cols)
	local sig = z and z.sig
	if not sig then return build_squares(z, w, h, panel_cols) end

	local m = G.sq_memo
	if not (m and m.sig == sig and m.panel == panel_cols) then
		m = { sig = sig, panel = panel_cols }
		G.sq_memo = m
	end

	-- Footprints are single figures; a thousand apart cannot collide.
	local key = w * 1024 + h
	local hit = m[key]
	if hit then return hit end

	hit = build_squares(z, w, h, panel_cols)
	m[key] = hit
	return hit
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE CEILING, IN THE WINDOW
--
-- UICellContainer:FindFreeCell walks its rows for a gap the item fits
-- in, and when it runs out it calls Grow() and asks itself again. That
-- is the infinity: the bag is a rectangle that is always exactly tall
-- enough, which is why you have never had to think about space.
--
-- Bounded, it is the same search over a fixed number of rows and a
-- fixed number of columns, and `false` when there is no room. `false`
-- is a shape the caller already handles - it is what an item too wide
-- for the panel has always returned - so nothing downstream needs to
-- learn anything new.
-- ============================================================
function G.find_bounded(self, obj, sec)
	sec = sec or (obj and obj:section())

	-- The id FIRST now: how big this thing is depends on which way it
	-- lies, and which way it lies is remembered against the id.
	local id
	pcall(function() id = obj:id() end)

	-- ============================================================
	-- "NO ROOM" AND "NO OPINION" ARE NOT THE SAME ANSWER
	--
	--   "the first inventory open is blank until i click somewhere in
	--    inventory"
	--
	-- THIS FUNCTION IS NOT A HELPER. It REPLACES the window's own
	-- FindFreeCell for our lists, so a `false` from here is not this
	-- mod declining to lay something out - it is the last word on
	-- whether the item can be in the container at all. AddItem, the
	-- window's own fill, Reinit, PutAll: every one of them stops when
	-- this says no.
	--
	-- Which means a transient read - a section whose grid size cannot
	-- be got for one pass, a zone list that comes out empty because the
	-- backpack could not be named yet - does not cost a bad layout. It
	-- costs THE WHOLE INVENTORY, blank, until something causes another
	-- rebuild. Which is what a click is, and which is exactly what was
	-- reported.
	--
	-- So the two answers are told apart. `false` alone still means what
	-- it always meant and is the reason the grid is bounded at all:
	-- your sections are full, and nothing may sit outside them. A
	-- SECOND return of true means "I could not work out where anything
	-- goes", and the wrapper hands the question back to the window,
	-- whose own packer is what fills this container when this mod is
	-- not installed.
	--
	-- Being wrong the first way is a list in the wrong squares for one
	-- frame. Being wrong the second way is no list at all.
	-- ============================================================
	local w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
	if not w then return false, true end

	-- #z == 0 IS TRUTHY, which is how an empty list of sections used to
	-- get all the way down this function and refuse every square in
	-- silence.
	local z = G.zones()
	if not (z and #z > 0) then return false, true end

	local cols, rows = G.capacity()
	-- Never wider than the panel can actually draw: a grid claiming
	-- columns the window does not have is a row of items off the edge
	-- of it.
	local panel = self.cols

	while #self.grid < rows do self:Grow() end

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHERE IT WAS LEFT COMES FIRST
	--
	--   "items that are getting unequpped by hand from the character are
	--    not placed when droped, most of the time they just got to the
	--    first fittable place, also before i had issue that reopening
	--    the inventory also caused a bit of reordering sometimes"
	--
	-- Both of those are this function not consulting the layout. It is
	-- the window's own packer - the thing that runs when an item ARRIVES
	-- rather than when the list is rebuilt - and it walked the grid from
	-- the top and took the first gap, every time. So:
	--
	--   * an item dragged out of a slot onto a square you chose landed
	--     wherever the top of the grid had room, because the square you
	--     chose was never asked about; and
	--   * anything arriving mid-session sat in the first gap until the
	--     next full rebuild moved it, which reads as the inventory
	--     quietly rearranging itself.
	--
	-- The layout is the answer to both. A square that is remembered and
	-- still free is where the thing goes.
	--
	-- ONLY IN THE REAL INVENTORY. A filtered view has its own idea of
	-- where things are and does not get to act on it - the same rule
	-- writing the layout follows.
	-- A KIN CLAIMS THE DEPARTED'S SQUARE. The scope swap destroyed
	-- wpn_udp9 and created wpn_udp9_e0t2_magd; without this the new
	-- object has no square and the packer resorts it - "the gun goes
	-- to first free space", verbatim. The lie and the by-hand mark
	-- come across with it, and the size is asked again afterwards so
	-- an inherited sideways lie is measured sideways.
	if id and writes_layout(self.ID) and not G.pos[id] then
		local L = G.legacy_find(sec)
		if L then
			G.pos[id] = { x = L.x, y = L.y, w = L.w, h = L.h }
			if L.mine then G.byhand[id] = true end
			if L.rot then G.rot[id] = true end
			w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
			dbg("%s adopts the square its kin %s left at %s,%s",
			    tostring(id), tostring(L.sec), tostring(L.y), tostring(L.x))
		end
	end

	local at = id and writes_layout(self.ID) and G.pos[id]
	if at and at.w == w and at.h == h
	   and G.fits(z, at.y, at.x, w, h, panel)
	   and self:IsFreeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h) then
		dbg("%s goes back to %s,%s", tostring(id), tostring(at.y), tostring(at.x))
		return self:TakeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h)
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND SAY WHEN THERE WAS NOTHING TO GO ON
	--
	--   "the ammo resort on gun reload still happens."
	--
	-- The layout is remembered per OBJECT ID, so anything arriving with
	-- a new id has no square and is placed by the search below - which
	-- is the inventory rearranging itself. A reload ends by handing the
	-- leftover rounds back like this, in the magazine mod's own words:
	--
	--     create_time_event("Mag_redux", "refund", 0.1, function()
	--         refund_ammo(pre_table) return true end)
	--
	--     alife_create_item(ammo_type, db.actor, {ammo = to_refund})
	--
	-- - a BRAND NEW OBJECT, a tenth of a second after every reload.
	-- That would do it, and it is a hypothesis, not a finding: there is
	-- no reload anywhere in the log this was read against.
	--
	-- So this says nothing clever. It names what arrived without a
	-- square, next to the line above that names what went back to one,
	-- and one log with a reload in it decides between them.
	-- ============================================================
	if id and writes_layout(self.ID) then
		dbg("no square remembered for %s (%s) - searching",
		    tostring(id), tostring(sec))
	end

	local sq = G.squares(z, w, h, panel)
	for i = 1, #sq do
		if self:IsFreeRoom(sq[i].r, sq[i].c, w, h) then
			return self:TakeRoom(sq[i].r, sq[i].c, w, h)
		end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- ...AND ON ITS SIDE, IF THAT IS THE ONLY WAY IT FITS
	--
	--   "sometimes i try to pickup ammo that takes 2x1 but i dont have
	--    that slot in horizontal orientation ... it will be good if
	--    items when quick looting or picking up from the ground will
	--    rotate themselves if needed to fit."
	--
	-- LAST, AND ONLY THEN. Everything above has already failed, so
	-- nothing that fits as it is is ever turned - a bag that rearranged
	-- your ammunition when it did not have to would be worse than the
	-- problem being solved. A square is never turned either: there is
	-- nothing to gain and a flag to explain afterwards.
	--
	-- G.rot IS THE ONE PLACE that answers which way a thing lies -
	-- size_for reads it, the draw reads it, the save writes it, the
	-- rotate key toggles it - so setting it IS the feature, and the
	-- turn is remembered like any other. Turn it back by hand and that
	-- is remembered instead.
	-- ============================================================
	if G.autorot and id and w ~= h then
		local sqt = G.squares(z, h, w, panel)
		for i = 1, #sqt do
			-- ============================================================
			-- ...BUT NOT INTO A ZONE THAT WOULD NOT TAKE IT STANDING UP
			--
			-- A zone's width is a RULE, not a measurement: "a long gun
			-- is five cells wide, so a four-wide grid cannot hold one".
			-- A rifle on its side is two wide and five tall and walks
			-- straight through it - and then your pockets have a rifle
			-- in them.
			--
			-- So the turn may find a HOLE; it may not get a thing into
			-- a room it was not allowed in. The test is the item's
			-- UPRIGHT width against the zone the turned square lands
			-- in, which is the same question the zone was refusing.
			-- ============================================================
			local band = G.zone_of(z, sqt[i].r)
			local wide = band and band.cols
			if wide and panel and panel < wide then wide = panel end

			if wide and w <= wide
			   and self:IsFreeRoom(sqt[i].r, sqt[i].c, h, w)
			then
				G.rot[id] = true
				dbg("%s (%s) went in sideways at %s,%s", tostring(id),
				    tostring(sec), tostring(sqt[i].r), tostring(sqt[i].c))
				return self:TakeRoom(sqt[i].r, sqt[i].c, h, w)
			end
		end
	end

	dbg("no square for %s (%sx%s)", tostring(sec), tostring(w), tostring(h))
	return false
end

-- ============================================================
-- IS THERE ROOM FOR THIS, WITHOUT PUTTING IT ANYWHERE
--
--   "when i detach rig (unequip it) and there are enouch cells to fit
--    the rig but they are not free they way the rig could fit ... i
--    rather have that if rig cannot fit in empty space between items
--    then it cannot be removed until player moves items around to
--    make a 2x3 empty space."
--
-- find_bounded's search, asked instead of acted on: the remembered
-- square first, then every square every zone offers, and nothing is
-- taken - so the sibling mod can ask "would this fit?" BEFORE it lets
-- a hidden rig arrive in the bag and shove everything else around.
--
-- Answers true, false, or nil for "no verdict" - grid off, no zones,
-- no size, no grid on the window - and the caller must treat nil as
-- yes, because a courtesy that locks the player out of their own rig
-- on one quiet frame is worse than no courtesy at all.
-- ============================================================
function G.room_for(cc, id, sec, turn)
	if not (G.enabled and cc) then return nil end

	local w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
	if not w then return nil end

	-- WHICH OF THE TWO QUESTIONS THIS IS.
	--
	-- Without `turn` it is the strict one - "is there a hole of this
	-- shape" - which is what the rig asks before it will come off, and
	-- the answer the player asked for:
	--
	--   "i rather have that if rig cannot fit in empty space between
	--    items then it cannot be removed until player moves items
	--    around to make a 2x3 empty space."
	--
	-- With it, it is the PACKER's question - "would this get in" - and
	-- the packer turns a thing when that is the only way. The red mark
	-- in a body's list asks this one, because what it predicts is the
	-- packer: marking what would have gone in sideways is a lie the
	-- player can see through in one pickup.
	turn = turn and G.autorot and w ~= h

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND THIS ONE DOES NOT TURN ANYTHING
	--
	--   "i rather have that if rig cannot fit in empty space between
	--    items then it cannot be removed until player moves items
	--    around to make a 2x3 empty space."
	--
	-- This is the question asked BEFORE a hidden rig is allowed to
	-- arrive, and the answer the player asked for is the strict one.
	-- The turn is for things arriving from outside - loot, a pickup, a
	-- transfer - and every one of those reaches the packer without
	-- coming through here.
	--
	-- The two therefore never disagree: where this is asked, it is
	-- asked first and its no is final, so the packer never gets the
	-- chance to turn what this refused.
	-- ============================================================

	local z = G.zones()
	if not z then return nil end

	local _, rows = G.capacity()
	-- The rows the zones promise may not be built yet - Grow is how
	-- the window adds them, and find_bounded does exactly this before
	-- its own search.
	pcall(function()
		while #cc.grid < rows do cc:Grow() end
	end)

	local panel = cc.cols

	-- Where it was left comes first, the same as the arrival will.
	local at = id and G.pos[id]
	if at and at.w == w and at.h == h
	   and G.fits(z, at.y, at.x, w, h, panel) then
		local okf, free = pcall(function()
			return cc:IsFreeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h)
		end)
		if okf and free then return true end
	end

	local sq = G.squares(z, w, h, panel)
	local okl, found = pcall(function()
		for i = 1, #sq do
			if cc:IsFreeRoom(sq[i].r, sq[i].c, w, h) then return true end
		end
	end)
	if okl and found then return true end

	-- ...AND ON ITS SIDE, for the caller that asked the packer's
	-- question. Same order as the packer - last, after everything
	-- upright - and carrying the same zone rule: a rifle on its side is
	-- two wide and five tall and still does not go in a four-wide
	-- pocket.
	--
	-- THE GUARD IS WRITTEN TWICE NOW, here and in find_bounded, which
	-- is a rule stated in two places and nothing checks that they agree.
	-- It is worth that only while there are two callers; a third is the
	-- moment to build the shared search this is a copy of.
	if turn then
		local sqt = G.squares(z, h, w, panel)
		local okt, gott = pcall(function()
			for i = 1, #sqt do
				local band = G.zone_of(z, sqt[i].r)
				local wide = band and band.cols
				if wide and panel and panel < wide then wide = panel end

				if wide and w <= wide
				   and cc:IsFreeRoom(sqt[i].r, sqt[i].c, h, w)
				then
					return true
				end
			end
		end)
		if okt and gott then return true end
	end

	dbg("no %sx%s room anywhere for %s (%s)", tostring(w), tostring(h),
	    tostring(sec), tostring(id))
	return false
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHERE EVERYTHING IS, AND WHY IT HAS TO BE REMEMBERED
--
--   "items resort on reopen but can be moved around just dont remember
--    where they were"
--
-- The window keeps no layout: opening the inventory throws every cell
-- away and adds everything again in sort order. First fit over a
-- sorted list is a tidy grid and a DIFFERENT tidy grid each time.
--
-- A cell that is on screen knows its own square, so the layout is read
-- back off the window rather than tracked alongside it - nothing to
-- drift. What matters is WHEN: the game's own open and refresh do not
-- go through Reinit for the bag at all (see G.install_ui), so reading
-- it there and only there was 0.2's bug.
-- ============================================================
function G.remember(id, area)
	if not (id and area and area.x and area.y) then return end
	G.pos[id] = { x = area.x, y = area.y, w = area.w, h = area.h }
end

-- The same, and this square was the player's idea.
function G.remember_mine(id, area)
	G.remember(id, area)
	if id and G.pos[id] then G.byhand[id] = true end
end

-- It is riding along in somebody else's square now, so it has no square
-- of its own to defend.
function G.rides(id)
	if id then G.byhand[id] = nil end
end

function G.forget(id)
	if id then
		G.pos[id] = nil
		G.byhand[id] = nil
		G.rot[id] = nil
		G.fresh[id] = nil
	end
end

-- A STACK IS ONE CELL WITH SEVERAL ITEMS IN IT. Each of them gets the
-- same square, or the ones that are not the cell's own id come back
-- homeless and scatter.
function G.capture(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return 0 end

	-- Reading a layout back off a filtered view records where things
	-- sat among HALF your kit. See writes_layout.
	if not writes_layout(cc.ID) then return 0 end

	local n = 0
	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local ok, shown = pcall(function() return ci:IsShown() end)
		if ci and ci.ID and ci.area and ok and shown then
			G.remember(ci.ID, ci.area)
			n = n + 1
			if ci.childs then
				for cid in pairs(ci.childs) do G.remember(cid, ci.area) end
			end
		end
	end
	return n
end

-- What the container is holding right now, as objects. Used to redo a
-- layout the game has just built for itself - it saves reimplementing
-- ParseInventory and whatever else the base does on the way.
function G.contents(cc)
	local out = {}
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return out end

	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		if ci and ci.ID then
			local o = level.object_by_id(ci.ID)
			if o then out[#out + 1] = o end
			if ci.childs then
				for cid in pairs(ci.childs) do
					local c = level.object_by_id(cid)
					if c then out[#out + 1] = c end
				end
			end
		end
	end
	return out
end

-- Reset empties the occupancy but leaves the rows, and a row that was
-- never made answers "not free" to everything.
function G.make_rows(cc, rows)
	while #cc.grid < rows do cc:Grow() end
	for r = 1, rows do
		cc.grid[r] = cc.grid[r] or {}
		for c = 1, cc.cols do
			if cc.grid[r][c] == nil then cc.grid[r][c] = true end
		end
	end
end

-- Rebuild the container from the remembered layout.
--
-- ORDER MATTERS TWICE. Everything with a remembered square goes FIRST,
-- so nothing homeless can squat where something belongs; and among
-- those, nearest the top left first, so two overlapping claims resolve
-- the same way every time rather than by table order.
-- ============================================================
-- THE BLOCK ABOVE THE GRID CHANGES HEIGHT, AND EVERYTHING BELOW IT
-- MOVES WITH IT
--
--   "when i change rigs, and some take less some takes more space
--    based on what slots thay have, the invneotry like pouches armour
--    and backpack needs to move row down or row up ect. and they do but
--    the items stay in the cells that they were so sometimes they
--    change positions."
--
-- Exactly right, and the whole of it. Armor Mag Pouches' rig block
-- stands on the top rows of this grid and how many rows that is depends
-- on the rig - a bandolier is two rows of pockets, a plate carrier
-- four. Every zone below starts at head_rows() + 1, so swapping a rig
-- moves every section up or down. A remembered square is an ABSOLUTE
-- row, so the sections slide out from under the items and the items are
-- suddenly in the wrong one.
--
-- THE OTHER FIX WAS TO GIVE EVERY RIG THE SAME RESERVED HEIGHT, and it
-- is simpler. It also costs the player squares: a block sized for the
-- biggest rig wastes four rows by nine columns while you are wearing
-- the smallest, in a mod whose entire subject is inventory space. So
-- the items move instead.
--
-- ONE NUMBER FOR ALL OF THEM. Every zone shifts by the same amount, so
-- shifting every remembered square by that amount puts everything back
-- where it was RELATIVE TO ITS OWN SECTION - which is what "where it
-- was" means to the person looking at it. Nothing is placed in the
-- block's rows, so there is nothing above the shift to leave behind,
-- and no zone changes size, so nothing that was inside one ends up
-- outside it.
--
-- ZERO IS NOT A HEIGHT. head_rows answers 0 when the block is not being
-- drawn at all - the inventory shut, the panel undocked, the trade
-- window's copy of your list, the mod switched off - and taking that
-- for a change would haul the whole layout up on every close and back
-- down on every open.
--
-- SEEDED FROM THE SAVE, and this is what "just had my inventory
-- resorted after a saveload" turned out to be. It used to start nil
-- after a load, on the reasoning that the same rig is still on so the
-- first rebuild can learn the height and shift nothing. The rig IS the
-- same. THE BLOCK IS NOT DRAWN AT ITS FULL HEIGHT YET:
--
--     layout: 17 of 37 kept, 13 outside the section (first: 33032 at 14,3)
--     layout: 30 of 37 kept, 0 outside the section
--     the rig block went from 3 rows to 5 - 82 squares moved with it
--
-- Three rows on the first pass, five two passes later. A section starts
-- at head + 2, so that is rows 5..13 where the save was written in
-- 7..15 - and every square the player had on row 14 was outside it,
-- refused, repacked and written down. By the time the block reached
-- five rows and this function shifted everything back, what it shifted
-- was the repacked arrangement.
--
-- head_sure did not stop it because three was not an assumption: the
-- block really was three rows tall right then. That guard is for a
-- block that cannot be measured at all, which is a different thing
-- from one that is still growing.
--
-- So the load hands this the height the squares were written against,
-- and the first rebuild becomes a height CHANGE like any other: five to
-- three lifts every square two rows, keeping the whole arrangement
-- inside the shorter section, and three back to five puts it down
-- again. Nothing is ever outside, so nothing is ever refused.
-- ============================================================
function G.reflow()
	-- No test of G.enabled here: head_rows answers 0 with the mod
	-- switched off, which is the same door as every other reason the
	-- block is not on screen. Two ways of saying the same thing is two
	-- things to keep in step.
	local now = G.head_rows()
	if type(now) ~= "number" or now <= 0 then return 0 end

	local was = G.head_was
	G.head_was = now

	if not (type(was) == "number" and was > 0) then return 0 end

	local d = now - was
	if d == 0 then return 0 end

	local n = 0
	for _, a in pairs(G.pos) do
		if type(a) == "table" and type(a.y) == "number" then
			a.y = a.y + d
			-- A block that grew cannot push anything off the top, and one
			-- that shrank cannot pull anything above the first row - but a
			-- row number is what the packer indexes an array with, and a
			-- zero would be a hole rather than an error.
			if a.y < 1 then a.y = 1 end
			n = n + 1
		end
	end

	dbg("the rig block went from %s rows to %s - %s squares moved with it",
	    tostring(was), tostring(now), tostring(n))
	return n
end

function G.restore(cc, list)
	-- FIRST, and what that has to be earlier than is the PLACING. The
	-- zones below are worked out from head_rows and the squares are not,
	-- so this is the one moment the two can be brought back into step,
	-- and everything after it reads one or the other.
	pcall(G.reflow)

	-- What the panel can really draw. The simulation that decides what
	-- gets dropped has no window to ask, so it borrows this - or the two
	-- disagree about how much room there is, which is a thing on the
	-- ground and a gap on the screen.
	--
	-- FROM THE REAL INVENTORY ONLY. The trade window's copy of your
	-- list is a different panel and can be a different width, and it is
	-- not the one that decides what you can carry.
	--
	-- BEFORE the zones are worked out, not after: the gutter's width
	-- depends on it, so asking in the other order lays the first frame
	-- out against last frame's panel.
	if cc.ID == "actor_bag" then G.panel_cols = cc.cols end

	-- ============================================================
	-- NOTHING TO LAY OUT IS NOT A REASON TO EMPTY THE INVENTORY
	--
	--   "the inventory is empty besides rig/rigslot, backpack/
	--    backpackslot, and wearable device slots and things inside them"
	--
	-- The log said it in two lines, and they are next to each other:
	--
	--   [GRID] layout: 0 of 0 kept, 0 outside the section, 0 stacked ...
	--   [GRID] layout: 13 of 17 kept, 0 outside the section, 4 stacked ...
	--
	-- The first is this function being handed an EMPTY list, calling
	-- cc:Reset() for it, laying out nothing, and REPORTING SUCCESS.
	--
	-- And reporting success is the damage, not the Reset. The Reinit
	-- wrap reads it:
	--
	--     local ok, done = pcall(G.restore, self, t)
	--     if not (ok and done) then return base_Reinit(self, t, tf) end
	--
	-- so a `true` means the base Reinit is NEVER CALLED. On this
	-- player's list that base is Inventory Antifreeze, whose whole
	-- design is to defer the fill: its Reinit sets self.deferred, stores
	-- itemsData, and drips the cells in over the following Update calls
	-- through self.sorter. Eat that call and the icons are never drawn -
	-- the container has been emptied and the mod that was going to
	-- refill it was never asked.
	--
	-- Which is exactly the picture: everything THIS mod draws is
	-- there - the rig, the slots, the devices - and everything the
	-- WINDOW draws is not, until a click causes a Refresh and a
	-- relayout from real contents.
	--
	-- AND `0 of 0` HAS TWO CAUSES, ONE HARMLESS AND ONE NOT.
	--
	-- The list building below drops any object whose id it cannot get:
	--
	--     local ok, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
	--     if ok and id then ... end
	--
	-- so a bag of twenty-five things every one of which cannot be named
	-- YET - which is what a game object looks like for the first
	-- moments after a load - counts as zero, exactly like an empty bag,
	-- and this function goes on to empty the container for it and
	-- report success. A genuinely empty bag is fine; that one is the
	-- whole bug.
	--
	-- So they are told apart HERE, before cc:Reset(), and being handed
	-- things we cannot name is answered "not this pass": the container
	-- is left exactly as it is and the base gets its turn.
	--
	-- COUNTED WITH pairs, because the id_list form of this argument is
	-- [id] = obj and `#` on it is zero however full it is - and the
	-- incremental refresh, the one that ADDS to an open bag, is the
	-- form that comes that way.
	-- ============================================================
	local n_in, n_named = 0, 0
	for _, obj in pairs(list or {}) do
		if type(obj) ~= "string" and obj then
			n_in = n_in + 1
			local oki, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
			if oki and id then n_named = n_named + 1 end
		end
	end

	if n_in > 0 and n_named == 0 then
		log("!handed %s thing(s) and could not name one of them - "
		    .. "leaving %s alone this pass", tostring(n_in), tostring(cc.ID))
		return false
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- SOMEBODY ELSE IS FILLING THIS CONTAINER AND IS NOT FINISHED
	--
	--   "still bugged ... thets how inventory looks on first open, and
	--    then i need to click with left click anywhere even outside the
	--    inventory"
	--
	-- The screenshot is the whole diagnosis: everything THIS mod draws
	-- is on screen - the rig, its pockets, the med squares, the
	-- backpack slot, the device slots - and the container itself is
	-- empty. Not mislaid. Empty.
	--
	-- Inventory Antifreeze (468- Inventory Antifreeze - Demonized)
	-- wraps Reinit, and the first thing it does is call the base with
	-- AN EMPTY TABLE, every single time:
	--
	--     utils_ui.UICellContainer.Reinit = function(self, t, tf, ...)
	--         self:InitDeferredDisplayData()
	--         self:InvalidateDeferredDisplay()
	--         utils_ui_UICellContainer_Reinit(self, {}, tf, ...)   <<<<
	--         ...
	--         -- and then adds the items itself, at once for a short
	--         -- list, or a few per frame through self.sorter
	--
	-- That empty table is the call this function sees. Answering `true`
	-- to it means the base Reinit below us NEVER RUNS - so the
	-- container is never set up for the adds that mod is about to
	-- make, and the panel stays empty until something rebuilds it.
	-- Which is what the click is, and it is why a click ANYWHERE works:
	-- it is not an interaction with the list, it is any event that
	-- causes another rebuild.
	--
	-- The same applies while its drip-feed is running: contents are
	-- partial by design, and laying out a partial list would Reset the
	-- container out from under the rest of it.
	--
	-- deferredInitialized is that mod's own mark, put on the container
	-- one line before it calls us. Nobody without it has the field, so
	-- this costs a nil read to everybody else.
	--
	-- WHEN IT FINISHES, WE LAY IT OUT. See G.deferred_done in the
	-- per-frame Update - otherwise the items would arrive in the
	-- window's order rather than in the squares the player left them.
	-- ============================================================
	if cc.deferredInitialized and (cc.deferred or n_in == 0) then
		G.defer_wait = G.defer_wait or {}
		G.defer_wait[cc.ID] = true
		return false
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AN EMPTY LIST OF ZONES IS NOT A LAYOUT
	--
	--   "the first inventory open is blank until i click somewhere in
	--    inventory"
	--
	-- `#z == 0` IS TRUTHY, and everything below this line then reads it
	-- as a grid with no room anywhere: cc:Reset() empties the
	-- container, make_rows makes none, G.fits refuses every square and
	-- first_gap has nowhere to look. Every item is dropped and the list
	-- is blank - until the next rebuild, which is exactly what a click
	-- causes.
	--
	-- Zones come out empty when band() is handed a size of zero, and
	-- band is handed a size that was READ: the backpack's cells, or the
	-- bare pocket count. A read that answers nothing for one pass - a
	-- pack whose section cannot be named yet, an ini not open, a
	-- setting not yet refreshed - is a transient, and the answer to a
	-- transient in this file has always been the same: DO NOT ACT ON
	-- IT. Returning false leaves the window's own packing on screen,
	-- which is a list, which is not blank.
	-- ============================================================
	local z = G.zones()
	if not (z and #z > 0) then
		if writes_layout(cc.ID) and not G.told_nozone then
			G.told_nozone = true
			log("!no sections to lay out in - the window keeps its own "
			    .. "packing this pass (pack %s, bare %s, widths %s/%s)",
			    tostring(G.pack), tostring(G.bare),
			    tostring(G.w_bare), tostring(G.w_pack))
		end
		return false
	end
	local _, rows = G.capacity()

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHERE THE LIST WAS LOOKING
	--
	--   "when item is missplaced, dropped when the highlight shows red
	--    ... or interacted with like dropping attachment or mags onto a
	--    guns ... the inventory scrolls maximum up instead of staying
	--    where it was. works fine when item is moved and dropped when it
	--    can be dropped tho."
	--
	-- Reset empties the container and Scroll_Reinit builds its scroll
	-- again, and a scroll built again starts at the top. That is fine
	-- when the player asked for a rebuild; it is not fine at all when
	-- the rebuild is the window's answer to something that did NOT move
	-- an item - a drop the grid refused, a magazine going into a gun,
	-- parts being swapped. Nothing about the list changed and it jumps
	-- to the top anyway.
	--
	-- A successful drag never showed it because the drag is handled
	-- here and returns before the window rebuilds anything.
	--
	-- So: remembered before, put back after.
	-- ============================================================
	local looking
	pcall(function() looking = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)

	-- BEFORE anything is placed: the branch below asks FindSimilar, and
	-- this is what FindSimilar answers from.
	G.claim(cc)

	cc:Reset()
	G.make_rows(cc, rows)

	-- Everything that is not inside a section: the rig block's rows, the
	-- gutter the icons live in, the header row above each section, and
	-- anything right of a section narrower than the panel.
	pcall(G.take_dead, cc, z, rows)

	cc.ignore_scroll = true

	local known, loose = {}, {}
	for _, obj in pairs(list) do
		if type(obj) ~= "string" and obj then
			local ok, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
			if ok and id then
				if G.pos[id] then known[#known + 1] = obj else loose[#loose + 1] = obj end
			end
		end
	end

	table.sort(known, function(a, b)
		local pa, pb = G.pos[a:id()], G.pos[b:id()]
		if pa.y ~= pb.y then return pa.y < pb.y end
		if pa.x ~= pb.x then return pa.x < pb.x end
		return a:id() < b:id()
	end)

	-- Same rule on the way in as on the way out: this view may put
	-- things where it likes, and only the real inventory's answer is
	-- written down.
	-- ...AND NOT FROM A PASS THAT IS ONLY ASSUMING WHERE THE TOP IS.
	-- G.head_rows answers with the last measured height when the block
	-- cannot be measured, which keeps the sections in the right place -
	-- but an assumption is not a thing to overwrite a saved layout
	-- with. Read the height again here so the flag belongs to this
	-- pass rather than to whoever asked last.
	G.head_rows()
	local write = writes_layout(cc.ID) and G.head_sure ~= false

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHAT WAS IN ONE SQUARE GOES BACK INTO ONE SQUARE
	--
	--   "shift clicking while holding item dont work but something
	--    happes as sometimes whole stack moves or ivnentory flicks"
	--
	-- It worked. The log has `one bandage put down at 14,7` for every
	-- tap. What it did not do was SURVIVE: the next rebuild gathered the
	-- item straight back into the stack it came from, so the split
	-- undid itself between one frame and the next - which is a thing
	-- that flicks and then looks like nothing happened.
	--
	-- Because the rule here used to be ONE ANCHOR PER SECTION: the first
	-- bandage kept its square and every other bandage in the grid was
	-- let go of and re-gathered, wherever it had been sitting and
	-- however it got there. That is a rule that cannot lose, and a
	-- player who wants two piles of bandages cannot win.
	--
	-- The rule now is per SQUARE. Everything that was in one square goes
	-- back into that square, and two squares stay two squares. A stack
	-- survives a rebuild because its riders remember the square they
	-- were in, not because a section is only allowed one pile.
	--
	-- Things ARRIVING still stack, and that was always the ask -
	-- "automatic up to the cap of the stack and if new item enters the
	-- ivnentory and cannot fit into stack then goes to new cell and
	-- makes new stack". An arrival has no square to go back to and finds
	-- a stack with room, below.
	-- ============================================================
	-- HOW MANY CAME IN WITH NO SQUARE AT ALL, counted before the
	-- gathering below adds to the same list. The two are not the same
	-- thing and a line that adds them up says a working mechanism is a
	-- fault.
	local no_square = #loose

	local anchored = {}
	local keep = {}

	-- ONE ANCHOR PER PILE, AND A PILE IS A SQUARE.
	--
	--   "all items after save are clumpted next to each other, if item
	--    was left at the bottom of the backpack it moves up ... item
	--    that are alone move so there is continous flow of items next
	--    to each other."
	--
	-- This was keyed by `sec` - by KIND - so the first bandage in the
	-- list kept its square and EVERY OTHER BANDAGE was let go of,
	-- wherever it was sitting. A released item that does not stack
	-- lands in the first gap, and in this game nearly everything
	-- stacks, so nearly everything was re-laid first-fit on every
	-- rebuild. That is the continuous flow, and it is why gaps never
	-- survived a save.
	--
	-- Keyed by the SQUARE, a lone bandage anchors its own square and
	-- stays there; two piles stay two piles; and the items that really
	-- are riding in one square are the only ones released - which is
	-- the gathering, and it still finds them their pile, because
	-- G.stack_at asks for the square they remember before anything
	-- else.
	--
	-- EVERY ITEM IN `known` HAS A SQUARE - that is what put it in this
	-- list - so the key can always be built. The `or` is not a fallback
	-- for a missing one, it is there so a table read cannot throw.
	for i = 1, #known do
		local obj = known[i]
		local id = obj:id()
		local ok, sec = pcall(function() return obj:section() end)
		local lim = ok and G.stack_limit(sec) or 1

		if lim > 1 and not G.byhand[id] then
			local at = G.pos[id]
			local key = at and (tostring(sec) .. "@"
			                    .. tostring(at.y) .. "," .. tostring(at.x))

			if key and not anchored[key] then
				anchored[key] = true
				keep[#keep + 1] = obj
			else
				loose[#loose + 1] = obj
			end
		else
			keep[#keep + 1] = obj
		end
	end

	known = keep

	local function put(obj, area)
		local id, sec = obj:id(), obj:section()
		local indx = cc.idxer + 1
		if cc:AddItemInCell(obj, sec, indx, area) then
			cc:AddIndex(id, sec, indx)
			cc.idxer = indx
			if write then G.remember(id, area) end
			return true
		end
		return false
	end

	-- COUNTED, AND THE TWO REFUSALS COUNTED APART. "Outside the
	-- section" means the grid moved under the layout - a different rig
	-- block, a different backpack, a saved square from another shape.
	-- "Something is in it" means another item got there first, which is
	-- an ordering problem and not a geometry one. They look identical
	-- on screen and they have nothing to do with each other.
	local gathered = #loose - no_square
	local kept, out_of, taken = 0, 0, 0
	local first_out

	for i = 1, #known do
		local obj = known[i]
		local w, h = G.size_for(obj:id(), obj:section())
		local at = G.pos[obj:id()]

		local inzone = w and at and G.fits(z, at.y, at.x, w, h, cc.cols)
		local free = inzone and cc:IsFreeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h)

		if free and put(obj, cc:TakeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h)) then
			kept = kept + 1
		else
			if at and not inzone then
				out_of = out_of + 1
				if not first_out then
					first_out = string.format("%s at %s,%s",
						tostring(obj:id()), tostring(at.y), tostring(at.x))
				end
			elseif at then
				taken = taken + 1
			end
			loose[#loose + 1] = obj
		end
	end

	-- ONLY FOR THE REAL INVENTORY. A filtered view legitimately refuses
	-- most of a layout and would fill the log with it.
	if writes_layout(cc.ID) then
		dbg("layout: %s of %s kept, %s outside the section, "
		    .. "%s stacked, %s gathering, %s had no square%s",
		    tostring(kept), tostring(#known), tostring(out_of),
		    tostring(taken), tostring(gathered), tostring(no_square),
		    first_out and (" (first: " .. first_out .. ")") or "")

		-- AND THE ONE THAT IS NOT A DEGREE BUT A KIND. Things carried
		-- and no squares at all is the layout having been thrown away
		-- rather than refused, and it is worth saying loudly.
		if #known == 0 and #loose > 0 then
			log("the layout is empty and %s things are carried - "
			    .. "every square has been forgotten", tostring(#loose))
		end
	end

	local said = {}

	for i = 1, #loose do
		local obj = loose[i]
		local id, sec = obj:id(), obj:section()
		if not cc.indx_id[id] then
			-- THE SQUARE IT WAS IN COMES FIRST. Otherwise a rider goes
			-- into whichever stack of its kind happens to have room -
			-- which quietly drains one pile into another and undoes any
			-- arrangement the player made by hand.
			--
			-- Ours, not the window's - see G.pick_stack.
			local why
			local ci = G.stack_at(cc, obj, sec, G.pos[id])
			-- A loose item has no remembered square - it is a genuine
			-- arrival - so it may join a by-hand pile. See G.next_stack.
			if not ci then ci, why = G.pick_stack(cc, obj, sec, not G.pos[id]) end
			if not ci then G.said_why(said, sec, why) end
			if ci and ci:AddChild(obj) then
				cc:AddIndex(id, sec, ci.indx)
				if write and ci.area then G.remember(id, ci.area) end
				G.rides(id)
			else
				-- ============================================================
				-- IT DID NOT STACK, SO IT GOES BACK WHERE IT WAS
				--
				--   "items instead of stacking are getting resorted and
				--    move by their own"
				--
				-- The gathering above lets go of a thing's square so it
				-- can fall into a stack. When the stack is then refused
				-- - spoiled food whose freshness does not match, an
				-- upgraded gun, anything the game marks dont_stack -
				-- this was the only path left, and it put
				-- the thing in the first gap it could find. Which reads
				-- as the inventory sorting itself while you watch.
				--
				-- A square it still remembers, that still exists and is
				-- still free, is where it belongs. The first gap is for
				-- something that has never had one.
				-- ============================================================
				local w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
				local at = w and G.pos[id]
				local area

				if at and G.fits(z, at.y, at.x, w, h, cc.cols)
				   and cc:IsFreeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h) then
					area = cc:TakeRoom(at.y, at.x, w, h)
				else
					area = w and G.first_gap(cc, w, h, z)
				end

				if area then put(obj, area) end
			end
		end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND NOTHING LEAVES THIS FUNCTION WITH AN EMPTY LIST
	--
	--   "the first inventory open is blank until i click somewhere in
	--    inventory"
	--
	-- cc:Reset() happened two hundred lines ago. From that moment until
	-- the loops above finish, the player's inventory does not exist -
	-- and EVERY road from there to "nothing was put back" ends in the
	-- same picture: an empty panel that stays empty until something
	-- else causes a rebuild. The zone guard at the top of this function
	-- closes the one road that was found. This closes the rest of them,
	-- including the ones nobody has found yet.
	--
	-- IT IS NOT A GUESS ABOUT THE CAUSE. It is the only invariant this
	-- function actually owes the player: you handed me things, you get
	-- things back. A list in the wrong squares is a bad layout. A list
	-- with nothing in it is a broken game, and the next rebuild will
	-- lay it out properly anyway.
	--
	-- THROUGH THE WINDOW'S OWN AddItem, deliberately: it is the code
	-- that fills this container when this mod is not installed, so the
	-- fallback is the unmodded game rather than a second packer of ours
	-- that could fail the same way.
	--
	-- AND IT SAYS SO OUT LOUD, with the numbers that name the cause -
	-- because a recovery that is silent is a bug that never gets fixed.
	-- ============================================================
	if #list > 0 then
		local got = 0
		for _ in pairs(cc.indx_id or {}) do got = got + 1 end

		if got == 0 then
			log("!nothing could be placed - %s thing(s) handed back to the "
			    .. "window (zones %s, rows %s, cols %s, head %s/%s)",
			    tostring(#list), tostring(#z), tostring(rows),
			    tostring(cc.cols), tostring(G.head_rows()),
			    tostring(G.head_sure))

			-- UNBOUNDED, FOR THESE CALLS ONLY. The bounded search has
			-- just answered no to every item in the list; asking it
			-- again would get the same answer and leave the panel
			-- empty. Cleared in the same breath, whatever happens, so
			-- one bad pass cannot turn the sections off for the rest of
			-- the session.
			G.recovering = true
			for i = 1, #list do
				local obj = list[i]
				pcall(function() cc:AddItem(obj, obj:section()) end)
			end
			G.recovering = nil
		end
	end

	pcall(G.probe, cc)

	-- SCROLLING.
	--
	-- The window sizes its scroll from row_end - the lowest row it has
	-- put anything in - so a grid eighteen rows deep with six rows of
	-- kit in it could not be scrolled to its own bottom. The empty part
	-- of your backpack was unreachable, and so was its edge.
	--
	-- The grid is as tall as the zones say, whether or not there is
	-- anything down there.
	if (cc.row_end or 0) < rows then cc.row_end = rows end

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE WINDOW'S OWN MEMORY, WHICH Reset HAD JUST WIPED
	--
	--   "still when items are dropped when the highlight is red, or are
	--    interacted with by dropping other items on them like guns and
	--    attachments then the inventory scrolles fully up"
	--
	-- Putting the scroll BAR back was never the whole job. The window
	-- keeps its own number, cc.scroll_pos, and every rebuild it does on
	-- its own goes back to that rather than to the top - so it is that
	-- number, not the bar, that has to be right when we hand control
	-- back.
	--
	-- And Reset wipes it. utils_ui's Reset ends in Scroll_Reinit, which
	-- ends in Scroll_SetPos, which WRITES scroll_pos every time it runs
	-- - and at that moment the container is empty, so the content is
	-- shorter than the window, so the answer is zero. Setting the bar
	-- afterwards looked right until the window rebuilt the list for its
	-- own reasons: a drop the grid refused, a magazine going into a
	-- gun, parts being swapped. Then it read zero and went to the top.
	--
	-- So the memory is written first and the rebuild below puts the bar
	-- where the memory says, clamped by the window's own arithmetic.
	-- ============================================================
	if type(looking) == "number" then
		cc.scroll_pos = looking
	end

	cc.ignore_scroll = false
	pcall(function() cc:Scroll_Reinit() end)

	-- AND THE BAR ITSELF, for a build of utils_ui whose Scroll_Reinit
	-- goes to the top instead of reading that number back - the base
	-- game's does, and so do some of the UI mods.
	--
	-- THROUGH G.put_back, which is the one place that knows how to do
	-- this: the engine's two-write trick for a view that has not got its
	-- scale back, the clamp for a list that has got shorter, and - the
	-- part this used to get wrong - leaving cc.scroll_pos holding what
	-- the view ACTUALLY took rather than what it was offered. A memory
	-- the view cannot reach is a disagreement On_Scroll spends every
	-- frame failing to settle. See the note in G.put_back.
	if type(looking) == "number" and looking > 0 then
		-- ALREADY THERE IS NOT WORTH TWO WRITES. Every write to a scroll
		-- is an engine call inside a scroll view, and on the build this
		-- is written against Scroll_Reinit has usually put the bar back
		-- already. The memory is still made to agree, because that is
		-- the whole point of the exercise.
		local at
		pcall(function() at = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)
		if at == looking then
			cc.scroll_pos = looking
		else
			looking = G.put_back(cc, looking) or looking
		end
	end

	-- Where the bar was when this started and where it is now. The other
	-- half of the pair in G.split_one: between the two of them, a list
	-- that moved says which of us moved it, or that neither did.
	local landed
	pcall(function() landed = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)
	if writes_layout(cc.ID) and looking ~= landed then
		dbg("!the rebuild moved the list: %s -> %s",
		    tostring(looking), tostring(landed))
	end

	pcall(G.draw_bounds, cc)

	-- WE HAVE LAID IT OUT, SO NOTHING IS WAITING ON A DEFERRED FILL.
	-- The flag would clear itself on the next frame anyway, but a note
	-- that outlives what it is about is a note that fires against the
	-- wrong container - the ids are reused for the life of the window.
	if G.defer_wait then G.defer_wait[cc.ID] = nil end

	return true
end

-- ============================================================
-- WOULD THIS FIT IN YOUR BAG, RIGHT NOW
--
-- TWO QUESTIONS, NOT ONE. An arriving item joins a pile if there is one
-- with room - FindSimilar runs before the packer ever sees it - and
-- only then needs a rectangle of its own. Asking about the rectangle
-- alone would put a red square under the twentieth bandage in a bag
-- that stacks bandages twenty to a square, and a mark that lies is a
-- mark the player learns to ignore.
--
-- MEMOISED BY SECTION. Forty cells in a stash times a walk of every
-- square of your bag is forty walks a frame, and the answer depends on
-- the KIND of thing rather than on the thing. Thrown away on a
-- throttle, so it follows the bag as it fills.
-- ============================================================
local FIT_MS = 250

function G.our_bag()
	local ui = rawget(_G, "ui_inventory")
	local gui = ui and ui.GUI
	local cc = gui and gui.CC and gui.CC["actor_bag"]
	if not cc then return nil end

	local ok, shown = pcall(function() return cc:IsShown() end)
	if ok and shown == false then return nil end
	return cc
end

-- HOW MANY FREE SQUARES THERE ARE, which is a different question from
-- "does this shape fit" and the one a take-all needs: forty transfers
-- happen in a frame and the window is not redrawn inside it, so the
-- shape test alone would answer for the bag as it looked before the
-- first of them.
-- Anything arriving changes what fits. Cheap on purpose: the answers
-- are worked out again the next time one is asked for, not here.
function G.on_take_fit()
	G.fit_memo, G.fit_at, G.fit_left = {}, nil, nil
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT NEEDS NO ROOM
--
--   "bodies sometimes have money that only takes space in their
--    inventory cuz when looted it just goes into the player money
--    stat. and this money cannot be looted when inventory is full"
--
--   "items that are in containers (example faction patch in wallet)
--    cannot be quicklooted when invetory is full"
--
-- Both are the same mistake: the door asks whether there is a square
-- for a thing that is never going to stand on one.
--
-- MONEY is a number wearing an item's clothes. Every note in the Zone
-- is banked the moment it reaches you - the item is gone, your wallet
-- is bigger, and the bag is exactly as full as it was. Asking whether
-- there is a square for one is asking about a thing that has stopped
-- existing by the time the answer matters.
--
-- HOW TO KNOW ONE, after two goes at it that did not:
--
--   `money_value` - dead. It is what a LOOT BOX's wad of cash carries;
--   the game's own notes do not, or do not spell it that way. The
--   fix that read it changed nothing at all.
--
--   `money_value` and no use action - worse. Every wad that HAS a
--   value also has a use action, because using it is how it becomes
--   money, so the rule excluded the one thing it was written for.
--
-- THE NAME IS THE THING THAT IS ACTUALLY THERE. The game names its
-- notes for what they are worth - money_50_100, money_200_300,
-- money_5000 - and the log has been printing them all along:
--
--     [GRID] no room for money_200_300 - left where it was
--     [GRID] ceiling money_200_300 (kind i_tool) = 4
--
-- `money_` followed by a DIGIT, which is what tells a note from a
-- money BOX (money_box_low): a box is a real item you carry until you
-- open it, and it takes a square like one.
--
-- `money_value` is still asked, second, for any mod that adds cash of
-- its own under some other name. It costs one lookup on a miss and it
-- is the only part of this that is not about one game's naming.
--
-- AND WHAT A BOX WILL FILE. Armor Mag Pouches takes an arriving item
-- straight into a case that already holds its kind - that is its
-- "file loot away" setting - so the grid never sees it either. The
-- question goes through the same door every other question to that mod
-- goes through, and a build without it just never gets a yes.
-- ============================================================
G.money_memo = G.money_memo or {}

function G.is_money(sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return false end

	local had = G.money_memo[sec]
	if had ~= nil then return had end

	local yes = string.find(sec, "^money_%d") and true or false

	if not yes then
		local v = SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "money_value")
		yes = (type(v) == "string" and v ~= "") and true or false
	end

	G.money_memo[sec] = yes
	return yes
end

function G.needs_no_room(sec)
	if G.is_money(sec) then return true end

	local amp = rawget(_G, "zzz_armor_mag_pouches")
	if amp and amp.grid_files then
		local ok, yes = pcall(amp.grid_files, sec)
		if ok and yes then return true end
	end

	return false
end

function G.free_cells(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.grid) then return nil end

	local rows = #cc.grid
	local cols = cc.cols or 0
	if rows < 1 or cols < 1 then return nil end

	-- ONE net for the whole count, not one per square. A hundred and
	-- fifty closures to count a hundred and fifty holes was the cost
	-- of a safety margin that either holds for the whole grid or for
	-- none of it.
	local free = 0
	local ok = pcall(function()
		for r = 1, rows do
			for c = 1, cols do
				if cc:IsFreeRoom(r, c, 1, 1) then free = free + 1 end
			end
		end
	end)
	if not ok then return nil end
	return free
end

function G.would_fit(obj, sec, id)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return true end

	local now = time_global()
	if not (G.fit_at and now - G.fit_at < FIT_MS) then
		G.fit_memo, G.fit_at = {}, now
		G.fit_left = nil
	end

	local had = G.fit_memo[sec]
	if had ~= nil then return had end

	-- ...and the two kinds of thing that never stand on a square. Asked
	-- INSIDE the memo, because the red mark asks this of forty cells a
	-- frame and one of the two answers walks another mod's cases.
	if G.needs_no_room(sec) then
		G.fit_memo[sec] = true
		return true
	end

	local cc = G.our_bag()
	if not cc then return true end

	-- THE OBJECT, FETCHED ONLY NOW.
	--
	-- The red mark asks this of every cell in the other side's list,
	-- every frame, and level.object_by_id is an engine call. Sixty
	-- items in a stash was sixty lookups a frame - three and a half
	-- thousand a second - to answer a question that is memoised by
	-- SECTION and changes a few times a minute.
	--
	-- Below the memo, so a hit costs nothing at all, and the caller
	-- passes an id rather than an object for the same reason.
	if not obj and id then
		local oko, got = pcall(level.object_by_id, id)
		obj = oko and got or nil
	end

	-- A PILE WITH ROOM IN IT IS ROOM. to_hand, because this is a
	-- brand-new arrival being asked about and those may join a pile the
	-- player made by hand - which is most piles.
	local ok, ci = pcall(G.next_stack, cc, obj, sec, G.stack_limit(sec), true)
	if ok and ci then
		G.fit_memo[sec] = true
		return true
	end

	-- ...OR A RECTANGLE OF ITS OWN. nil is "no verdict" - the grid off,
	-- no zones, no size - and no verdict has to mean yes, or a quiet
	-- frame paints the whole stash red.
	-- THE PACKER'S QUESTION, not the rig's. An arriving thing may lie
	-- on its side to get in, so a mark that ignored that would be red
	-- under half the ammunition in the Zone.
	local okr, room = pcall(G.room_for, cc, id, sec, true)
	local fits = (not okr) or (room ~= false)

	-- ...AND THE COUNT, WHICH IS WHAT SURVIVES A TAKE-ALL. The shape
	-- test reads the window; the count is ours and is decremented by
	-- every move let through, so the fortieth transfer of one frame is
	-- judged against what the first thirty-nine took.
	if fits then
		if G.fit_left == nil then G.fit_left = G.seed_left(cc, now) end
		local w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
		if G.fit_left and w and G.fit_left < w * h then fits = false end
	end

	G.fit_memo[sec] = fits
	return fits
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE COUNT THE WINDOW IS TOO SLOW TO GIVE
--
--   "if i drag loot with my mouse, so i loot a lot of items quickly...
--    the last items kinda go into inventory even where there is no
--    space and gets dropped"
--
-- Every count above starts by asking the window how many squares are
-- free, and the window answers for the bag AS IT IS LAID OUT. A move
-- the door has already let through is not laid out until the list is
-- built again, and a burst of drags outruns that by whole rebuilds -
-- the log says so out loud: `5 rebuild(s) skipped`.
--
-- So a fresh reading in the middle of a burst hands back squares that
-- were spent seconds ago, the door says yes to things there is no room
-- for, and the tick that runs a second later puts them on the ground.
-- Which is the one thing the person who chose `block` asked not to
-- happen.
--
-- THE READING IS A CEILING, NOT A REPLACEMENT. While a burst is
-- running the count may fall - a rebuild that reveals LESS room is
-- news - but it may not rise. Stop looting for a few seconds and the
-- window's own answer is believed again, so a bag emptied by hand is
-- not remembered as full.
-- ============================================================
local BURST_MS = 3000

function G.seed_left(cc, now)
	local fresh = G.free_cells(cc)
	if not fresh then return nil end

	now = now or time_global()
	if G.fit_was and G.spent_at and (now - G.spent_at) < BURST_MS
	   and G.fit_was < fresh
	then
		return G.fit_was
	end

	return fresh
end

-- A move was allowed: those squares are spoken for, whatever the window
-- still shows. Both the count and the per-section answers go, because
-- what fits changes with every one of them.
function G.fit_spend(sec, id)
	-- WHEN, before anything else. The burst is what makes a fresh
	-- reading of the window untrustworthy, so it is marked whether or
	-- not there was a count to take this out of.
	G.spent_at = time_global()

	local w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
	if not (w and G.fit_left) then
		G.fit_memo = {}
		return
	end
	G.fit_left = G.fit_left - w * h
	if G.fit_left < 0 then G.fit_left = 0 end

	-- WHAT IS LEFT, KEPT WHERE THE RESETS CANNOT REACH IT. fit_left is
	-- thrown away by every take and by the throttle; this is the
	-- ceiling the next reading is held to.
	G.fit_was = G.fit_left
	G.fit_memo = {}
end

function G.first_gap(cc, w, h, z)
	local sq = G.squares(z, w, h, cc.cols)
	for i = 1, #sq do
		if cc:IsFreeRoom(sq[i].r, sq[i].c, w, h) then
			return cc:TakeRoom(sq[i].r, sq[i].c, w, h)
		end
	end
	return nil
end

-- The game has just filled the container itself. Take what it put in
-- and lay it out again from memory.
-- ============================================================
-- THE CONTAINER'S OWN SWITCH
--
-- disable_stack is a field the base class already honours: FindSimilar
-- returns false the moment it is set, and FindSimilar is the ONE place
-- both the window's AddItem and this file's own layout ask "is there
-- already a cell for one of these". Setting it is therefore the whole
-- feature, and setting anything else would be a second answer to a
-- question that already has one.
--
-- Written on every rebuild and every frame rather than once, because it
-- is a field on an object this mod does not own and does not get told
-- about.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- HOW MANY OF A THING SHARE ONE SQUARE
--
-- Stacking used to be one switch with two settings: the window's own
-- behaviour, where a stack is unlimited, or none at all. Neither is an
-- inventory. Forty bandages in one square is a list with extra steps;
-- one square each is a grid you cannot walk out of the door with.
--
-- So there is a CEILING, per kind of thing, in a file anyone can edit -
-- and everything not named in it still gets one square per item, which
-- is what shipped and what people are playing.
--
-- WHAT CAN STACK IS NOT DECIDED HERE. The window refuses a weapon, an
-- outfit, anything with `dont_stack`, anything upgraded and any
-- part-used multiuse item, and only lets two conditioned things share a
-- square when their condition is within a tenth. None of that is
-- touched. This adds a ceiling to what the window already allows.
-- ============================================================
local STACK_LTX = "items\\settings\\zzz_grid_stacks.ltx"
local PACK_LTX  = "items\\settings\\zzz_grid_packs.ltx"

-- Read once. Three blocks, most specific first when they are asked.
-- ============================================================
-- WHITESPACE IS NOT PART OF A NAME
--
-- The ceilings are written in a column, so every key in the file is
-- followed by a run of spaces before the `=`. A key that comes back
-- with those spaces still on it is a key nothing will ever match, and
-- the count in the log still says the line was read - which is exactly
-- how a file that parses can still mean nothing.
-- ============================================================
local function trim(s)
	if type(s) ~= "string" then return nil end
	s = s:gsub("^[%s]+", ""):gsub("[%s]+$", "")
	return s
end

-- ============================================================
-- HOW MUCH ROOM EACH BACKPACK GIVES
--
--   "i want to make backpacks give different ammount of space based on
--    the backpack"
--
-- Read the same way the stack ceilings are, from a file of its own, and
-- answered the same way: this exact section, then its kind, then - and
-- this is the rung that matters - the pack's OWN carry bonus.
--
-- THE ARITHMETIC IS THERE SO NOBODY HAS TO WRITE A LIST. There are
-- dozens of backpacks in GAMMA and more in other mods, and a feature
-- that needs a line written for each is a feature that is wrong for
-- every one nobody got round to. additional_inventory_weight is the
-- game's own statement of how much more a pack lets you carry, which is
-- what a bigger bag means; per_kg turns that into squares.
--
-- The defaults here are only for a file that cannot be read at all. The
-- file's own numbers are the ones that ship.
-- ============================================================
function G.read_packs()
	local out = { sec = {}, kind = {}, per_kg = 2.5, min = 24, max = 200 }

	local ok, f = pcall(ini_file, PACK_LTX)
	if not (ok and f) then
		log("!no backpack sizes - %s could not be read", PACK_LTX)
		return out
	end

	-- Written as strings rather than as bare keys, because these names
	-- have to match the file exactly and the audit checks that they do
	-- by looking for them here.
	local into = {
		["grid_pack_section"] = "sec",
		["grid_pack_kind"]    = "kind",
	}

	for name, where in pairs(into) do
		local okc, n = pcall(function() return f:line_count(name) end)
		if okc and type(n) == "number" then
			for i = 0, n - 1 do
				local okl, _, id, val = pcall(function()
					return f:r_line_ex(name, i, "", "")
				end)
				id = trim(id)
				local cells = okl and tonumber(trim(val))
				if okl and id and id ~= "" and cells and cells > 0 then
					out[where][id] = math.floor(cells)
				end
			end
		end
	end

	-- THE DIAL, and its two fences. A nonsense value in the file is
	-- ignored rather than believed: per_kg of zero would make every pack
	-- the smallest one, and that is a file nobody could debug.
	local okc, n = pcall(function() return f:line_count("grid_pack_scale") end)
	if okc and type(n) == "number" then
		for i = 0, n - 1 do
			local okl, _, id, val = pcall(function()
				return f:r_line_ex("grid_pack_scale", i, "", "")
			end)
			id = trim(id)
			local v = okl and tonumber(trim(val))
			if okl and v and v > 0 then
				if id == "per_kg" then out.per_kg = v
				elseif id == "min" then out.min = math.floor(v)
				elseif id == "max" then out.max = math.floor(v)
				end
			end
		end
	end

	if out.max < out.min then out.max = out.min end
	return out
end

-- ============================================================
-- ...AND THE ANSWER FOR ONE BACKPACK
--
-- Memoised: zones() is asked on every draw, and this reads a file and
-- two section parameters. The memo is cleared with the settings, which
-- is the one moment any of it can change.
--
-- NO SECTION IS NOT NO BACKPACK. This is only ever asked about the
-- thing in the backpack slot; being unable to name it is a reason to
-- fall back to the setting, not a reason to answer zero and take
-- somebody's inventory away.
-- ============================================================
function G.pack_cells(sec)
	local fallback = G.pack

	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return fallback end

	local had = G.pk_memo[sec]
	if had then return had end

	if not G.pk then G.pk = G.read_packs() end

	local n = G.pk.sec[sec]

	if not n then
		local kind
		pcall(function() kind = trim(SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind")) end)
		if kind and kind ~= "" then n = G.pk.kind[kind] end
	end

	-- THE PACK'S OWN WORD FOR HOW BIG IT IS.
	if not n then
		local kg
		pcall(function()
			kg = SYS_GetParam(2, sec, "additional_inventory_weight")
		end)
		if type(kg) == "number" and kg > 0 then
			n = math.floor(kg * G.pk.per_kg + 0.5)
			if n < G.pk.min then n = G.pk.min end
			if n > G.pk.max then n = G.pk.max end
		end
	end

	if type(n) ~= "number" or n <= 0 then n = fallback end

	n = clampi(math.floor(n), 0, 400)
	G.pk_memo[sec] = n
	return n
end

function G.read_stacks()
	local out = { sec = {}, like = {}, kind = {}, flag = {}, n = 0 }

	local ok, f = pcall(ini_file, STACK_LTX)
	if not (ok and f) then
		log("!no stack ceilings - %s could not be read", STACK_LTX)
		return out
	end

	-- Written as strings rather than as bare keys, because these names
	-- have to match the file exactly and the audit checks that they do
	-- by looking for them here.
	local into = {
		["grid_stack_section"] = "sec",
		["grid_stack_kind"]    = "kind",
	}

	for name, where in pairs(into) do
		local okc, n = pcall(function() return f:line_count(name) end)
		if okc and type(n) == "number" then
			for i = 0, n - 1 do
				local okl, _, id, val = pcall(function()
					return f:r_line_ex(name, i, "", "")
				end)
				id = trim(id)
				local lim = okl and tonumber(trim(val))
				if okl and id and id ~= "" and lim and lim > 0 then
					out[where][id] = lim
					out.n = out.n + 1
				end
			end
		end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND THE SECTION FLAGS
	--
	--   "i need that magazines are unstackable in inventory only in the
	--    box"
	--
	-- A LIST, in the file's own order, because these are asked of the
	-- game one at a time and the first one a section answers to wins.
	-- Sorted by name so that two flags on one section cannot answer
	-- differently depending on what order a Lua table handed them back
	-- - the same reason the name matches are sorted.
	-- ============================================================
	local okf, nf = pcall(function() return f:line_count("grid_stack_flag") end)
	if okf and type(nf) == "number" then
		for i = 0, nf - 1 do
			local okl, _, id, val = pcall(function()
				return f:r_line_ex("grid_stack_flag", i, "", "")
			end)
			id = trim(id)
			local lim = okl and tonumber(trim(val))
			if okl and id and id ~= "" and lim and lim > 0 then
				out.flag[#out.flag + 1] = { id, lim }
				out.n = out.n + 1
			end
		end
	end

	table.sort(out.flag, function(a, b) return a[1] < b[1] end)

	-- The name matches are a LIST, longest first, so two patterns that
	-- both match cannot answer differently depending on the order a
	-- Lua table happened to hand them back.
	local okc, n = pcall(function() return f:line_count("grid_stack_like") end)
	if okc and type(n) == "number" then
		for i = 0, n - 1 do
			local okl, _, id, val = pcall(function()
				return f:r_line_ex("grid_stack_like", i, "", "")
			end)
			id = trim(id)
			local lim = okl and tonumber(trim(val))
			if okl and id and id ~= "" and lim and lim > 0 then
				out.like[#out.like + 1] = { id, lim }
				out.n = out.n + 1
			end
		end
	end

	table.sort(out.like, function(a, b)
		if #a[1] ~= #b[1] then return #a[1] > #b[1] end
		return a[1] < b[1]
	end)

	-- WHAT WAS ACTUALLY READ, not how many lines were seen. A count
	-- says the file parsed; only the names say it means anything.
	local said = {}
	for k, v in pairs(out.kind) do said[#said + 1] = "[" .. k .. "]=" .. v end
	for k, v in pairs(out.sec)  do said[#said + 1] = "[" .. k .. "]=" .. v end
	for i = 1, #out.like do
		said[#said + 1] = "[*" .. out.like[i][1] .. "*]=" .. out.like[i][2]
	end
	for i = 1, #out.flag do
		said[#said + 1] = "[!" .. out.flag[i][1] .. "]=" .. out.flag[i][2]
	end
	table.sort(said)

	log("%s stack ceilings: %s", tostring(out.n), table.concat(said, " "))
	return out
end

-- How many of this section may share one square. One means its own
-- square, which is the answer for everything the file does not name.
function G.stack_limit(sec)
	if not (G.enabled and G.stack) then return 1 end
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return 1 end

	local had = G.stk_memo[sec]
	if had then return had end

	if not G.stk then G.stk = G.read_stacks() end

	-- WHICH RUNG ANSWERED, noted as the ladder is climbed. Debug
	-- Balancing reads this; nothing else does, and nothing else may,
	-- because it is a description of a decision rather than part of it.
	local rung, via = nil, nil
	local kind

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHAT YOU SET IN THE MENU COMES FIRST
	--
	-- Above the exact section, which is the file's most specific rung,
	-- because a number you chose thirty seconds ago beats a number
	-- somebody wrote in a file - including a number YOU wrote in the
	-- file, which is the case that matters while balancing.
	--
	-- THE KIND IS ONLY ASKED FOR WHEN THERE IS A KIND OVERRIDE TO
	-- MATCH. This is the hottest function in the grid and the lookup is
	-- an engine call; with no kind overrides set - which is every
	-- player who has never opened the option - it costs one `next` on
	-- an empty table.
	-- ============================================================
	local base, over = nil, false
	local ov = G.stk_over
	if ov then
		base = ov.sec[sec]
		if base then rung, via, over = "override", sec, true end

		if (not base) and next(ov.kind) then
			kind = trim(SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind"))
			if kind and kind ~= "" and ov.kind[kind] then
				base = ov.kind[kind]
				rung, via, over = "override kind", kind, true
			end
		end
	end

	if not base then
		base = G.stk.sec[sec]
		if base then rung, via = "section", sec end
	end

	if not base then
		for i = 1, #G.stk.like do
			if string.find(sec, G.stk.like[i][1], 1, true) then
				base = G.stk.like[i][2]
				rung, via = "like", G.stk.like[i][1]
				break
			end
		end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- ...THEN A FLAG THE SECTION CARRIES, and this rung is ABOVE the
	-- kind on purpose.
	--
	--   "i need that magazines are unstackable in inventory only in the
	--    box"
	--
	-- A magazine's kind is w_ammo, which is also a box of cartridges -
	-- so the kind rung gives it the cartridge ceiling, twenty deep. The
	-- flag is the thing that says what it really is, and it has to be
	-- asked first or the kind answers before it.
	--
	-- Still BELOW the exact section and the name patterns, because
	-- those are somebody saying what they want about one particular
	-- thing and this is a rule about a whole class of them.
	-- ============================================================
	if not base then
		for i = 1, #G.stk.flag do
			if SYS_GetParam(1, sec, G.stk.flag[i][1]) then
				base = G.stk.flag[i][2]
				rung, via = "flag", G.stk.flag[i][1]
				break
			end
		end
	end

	if not base then
		-- The game's own word for what a thing is - the one the sorting
		-- tabs read - so a mod that adds a bandage gets the bandage
		-- ceiling with nobody editing anything. Asked once: the
		-- override rung above may already have needed it.
		kind = kind or trim(SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind"))
		if kind and kind ~= "" then
			base = G.stk.kind[kind]
			if base then rung, via = "kind", kind end
		end
	end

	local n = 1
	if base then
		-- AN OVERRIDE IS ABSOLUTE. The per-cent slider scales the FILE -
		-- it is there so somebody can have every ceiling the mod ships
		-- be tighter or looser at once. A number you picked in a menu
		-- five seconds ago is not a thing to scale; you picked twelve
		-- because you wanted twelve.
		local mult = over and 1 or (G.stack_mult or 1)
		n = math.floor(base * mult + 0.5)
		if n < 1 then n = 1 end
	end

	-- ONE LINE PER SECTION, the first time it is asked about, saying
	-- what the game calls the thing and what that got it. Without this
	-- a ceiling that never matches looks exactly like a ceiling that
	-- does, and the only symptom is that nothing stacks.
	dbg("ceiling %s (kind %s) = %s", tostring(sec), tostring(kind), tostring(n))

	-- ...AND WHAT PRODUCED IT. `kind` is nil here whenever a rung above
	-- the kind answered - the lookup is deliberately not made until it
	-- is needed - so the readout asks for it separately rather than
	-- this hot path asking for it always.
	G.stk_why[sec] = { n = n, base = base, rung = rung or "none",
	                   via = via, kind = kind }

	G.stk_memo[sec] = n
	return n
end


-- ============================================================
-- WHAT THIS MOD THINKS A THING IS, IN ONE LINE
--
--   "i could change every item max stack in inventory"
--
-- You change it in zzz_grid_stacks.ltx, and the reason that is harder
-- than it sounds is that the file names things four different ways and
-- none of the names are visible in game. This says all of them:
--
--     bandage | kind i_medical | ceiling 5 from [kind] i_medical
--
-- and, when the thing cannot pile at all whatever the ceiling says,
-- the reason it will not:
--
--     wpn_ak74 | kind w_rifle | ceiling 1 from [nothing names it]
--       | will not stack: the game marks it dont_stack
--
-- THE CEILING IS ASKED FOR, NOT LOOKED UP. Calling stack_limit is what
-- fills stk_why, and it is the same call the packer makes - so the
-- line cannot describe a rule the grid is not actually using.
-- ============================================================
function G.why_text(obj, sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return nil end

	local okn, n = pcall(G.stack_limit, sec)
	if not okn then return nil end

	local w = G.stk_why[sec] or {}

	-- The kind, which the ladder only asks for when it gets that far.
	local kind = w.kind
	if type(kind) ~= "string" or kind == "" then
		local okk, k = pcall(function()
			return trim(SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind"))
		end)
		kind = (okk and type(k) == "string" and k ~= "") and k or "?"
	end

	local from
	local rung = w.rung
	if rung == "override" then from = "[you said] " .. tostring(w.via)
	elseif rung == "override kind" then
		from = "[you said] kind " .. tostring(w.via)
	elseif rung == "section" then from = "[section] " .. tostring(w.via)
	elseif rung == "like" then from = "[like] *" .. tostring(w.via) .. "*"
	elseif rung == "flag" then from = "[flag] " .. tostring(w.via)
	elseif rung == "kind" then from = "[kind] " .. tostring(w.via)
	else from = "[nothing names it]" end

	local line = fmt_safe("%s | kind %s | ceiling %s from %s",
	                      sec, kind, tostring(n), from)

	-- ...AND WHETHER IT CAN PILE AT ALL, which is a different question
	-- from how many. A ceiling of five on something the game marks
	-- dont_stack is a line in the file doing nothing, and that is
	-- exactly the kind of dead rule this readout exists to find.
	if obj then
		local okm, may, whynot = pcall(G.may_stack, obj, sec)
		if okm and may == false then
			line = line .. " | will not stack: " .. tostring(whynot or "?")
		end
	end

	return line
end


-- ============================================================
-- SAID WHILE YOU LOOK AT IT
--
-- On the game's own message line rather than a window of our own: this
-- is read for a second and forgotten, it must not fight the inventory
-- for space, and there is no layout to get wrong in a feature nobody
-- ships turned on.
--
-- SAID AGAIN WHILE THE CURSOR HAS NOT MOVED. The message line fades by
-- itself after a few seconds, and a readout you have to jiggle the
-- mouse to bring back is one you stop trusting halfway through a
-- balancing pass.
--
-- WRITTEN TO THE LOG ONCE PER SECTION, not once per frame and not once
-- per glance. What that leaves behind is the actual artefact of a
-- session: a list of everything you looked at, what the mod called it,
-- and what it got - which is the thing you edit the file from.
-- ============================================================
local BAL_HOLD = 4000

function G.draw_balance(cc)
	if not G.balance then
		G.bal_said, G.bal_at = nil, nil
		return
	end
	if not cc then return end

	-- GetCell_Focused's THIRD return is the object. This project has
	-- been wrong about that once already, in a build that read an id
	-- off the first one, looked right in the harness and answered nil
	-- in the game.
	local ok, _, _, obj = pcall(function() return cc:GetCell_Focused() end)
	if not (ok and obj) then return end

	local oks, sec = pcall(function() return obj:section() end)
	if not (oks and type(sec) == "string") then return end

	local txt = G.why_text(obj, sec)
	if not txt then return end

	local now = 0
	local okt, tg = pcall(time_global)
	if okt and type(tg) == "number" then now = tg end

	if txt == G.bal_said and G.bal_at and (now - G.bal_at) < BAL_HOLD then
		return
	end
	G.bal_said, G.bal_at = txt, now

	if not G.bal_seen[sec] then
		G.bal_seen[sec] = true
		log("balance: %s", txt)
	end

	pcall(function() actor_menu.set_msg(1, txt, 5) end)
end




-- ============================================================
-- THE EIGHT ROWS
--
--   "make it that i can, idk... rightclick an item when the debug
--    option is ON and it gets new option to change stack size."
--
--     stack  bandage = 5   (kind i_medical)
--     stack  -5
--     stack  -1
--     stack  +1
--     stack  +5
--     stack  changing: this item only
--     stack  back to the file's number
--     stack  write 3 change(s) to the log
--
-- The first is a readout you can click harmlessly - it says the number
-- and where it is coming from, and clicking it just brings the menu
-- back with everything still true. The last two only appear when there
-- is something for them to do.
-- ============================================================
local BAL_MAX  = 999
local BAL_ROWS = 8

-- An id can name a client object or only a server one - a corpse two
-- rooms away has the second and not the first - and the menu is opened
-- on both. Asked in that order because the client object is the one
-- everything else in this file speaks to.
local function bal_obj(id)
	if type(id) ~= "number" then return nil end
	local ok, o = pcall(level.object_by_id, id)
	if ok and o then return o end
	ok, o = pcall(alife_object, id)
	if ok and o then return o end
	return nil
end

function G.bal_sec(id)
	local o = bal_obj(id)
	if not o then return nil end
	local oks, sec = pcall(function() return o:section() end)
	return (oks and type(sec) == "string" and sec ~= "") and sec or nil
end

function G.bal_kind(sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return nil end
	local ok, k = pcall(function() return trim(SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind")) end)
	return (ok and type(k) == "string" and k ~= "") and k or nil
end

-- WHICH TABLE THE PLUS AND MINUS WRITE TO. The scope is remembered
-- across right-clicks, and a thing the game gives no kind cannot be
-- changed by kind however the scope is set - so it falls back to
-- itself rather than writing a number under an empty name.
function G.bal_target(sec)
	if G.bal_scope == "kind" then
		local k = G.bal_kind(sec)
		if k then return "kind", k end
	end
	return "sec", sec
end

-- Everything worked out from a ceiling is now wrong, and the window is
-- still showing the old piles.
function G.bal_apply()
	G.stk_memo, G.stk_why = {}, {}
	G.fit_memo, G.fit_at, G.fit_left = {}, nil, nil
	G.bal_seen = {}
	G.bal_said, G.bal_at = nil, nil

	pcall(function()
		local g = ui_inventory and ui_inventory.GUI
		if g and g.On_Item_Update then g:On_Item_Update() end
	end)
end

-- nil is "forget what I said and use the file again".
function G.bal_set(sec, value)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return false end

	local where, key = G.bal_target(sec)
	if type(key) ~= "string" or key == "" then return false end

	if value == nil then
		G.stk_over[where][key] = nil
	else
		if type(value) ~= "number" then return false end
		G.stk_over[where][key] = clampi(math.floor(value), 1, BAL_MAX)
	end

	log("balance: %s %s = %s", where == "kind" and "kind" or "section",
	    tostring(key), tostring(G.stk_over[where][key] or "the file's number"))
	G.bal_apply()
	return true
end

-- FROM WHAT YOU CAN SEE, not from what is stored. Plus one on a five
-- the file gave you is six, and that is the first thing that has to be
-- true or the buttons feel broken on the first press.
function G.bal_bump(sec, d)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or type(d) ~= "number" then return false end
	local ok, now = pcall(G.stack_limit, sec)
	if not (ok and type(now) == "number") then now = 1 end
	return G.bal_set(sec, now + d)
end

-- ============================================================
-- ...AND THE WAY OUT OF THE SAVE
--
-- The numbers live in the save, which is right for a balancing pass
-- and useless for shipping one. This writes every one of them to the
-- log in the file's own shape, ready to paste - so a session that
-- started as fiddling can end as a diff.
-- ============================================================
function G.bal_dump()
	local s, k = {}, {}
	for a, b in pairs(G.stk_over.sec)  do s[#s + 1] = a .. " = " .. tostring(b) end
	for a, b in pairs(G.stk_over.kind) do k[#k + 1] = a .. " = " .. tostring(b) end
	table.sort(s)
	table.sort(k)

	if #s == 0 and #k == 0 then
		log("balance: nothing has been changed yet")
		return 0
	end

	log("balance: ---- 8< ---- for zzz_grid_stacks.ltx ----")
	if #k > 0 then
		log("balance: [grid_stack_kind]")
		for i = 1, #k do log("balance: %s", k[i]) end
	end
	if #s > 0 then
		log("balance: [grid_stack_section]")
		for i = 1, #s do log("balance: %s", s[i]) end
	end
	log("balance: ---- >8 ----")

	pcall(function()
		actor_menu.set_msg(1, fmt_safe(
			"Zone Grid: %s change(s) written to the log",
			tostring(#s + #k)), 4)
	end)
	return #s + #k
end

function G.bal_count()
	local c = 0
	for _ in pairs(G.stk_over.sec)  do c = c + 1 end
	for _ in pairs(G.stk_over.kind) do c = c + 1 end
	return c
end

function G.bal_row_name(i, sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return nil end

	local okn, cur = pcall(G.stack_limit, sec)
	if not (okn and type(cur) == "number") then cur = 1 end
	local w = G.stk_why[sec] or {}

	if i == 1 then
		-- WHERE THE NUMBER IS COMING FROM, in the same words the hover
		-- readout uses. A row that only said "= 5" would leave you
		-- unable to tell your own change from the file's.
		local from
		local rung = w.rung
		if rung == "override" then from = "yours"
		elseif rung == "override kind" then
			from = "yours, kind " .. tostring(w.via)
		elseif rung == "none" or rung == nil then from = "nothing names it"
		else from = tostring(rung) .. " " .. tostring(w.via) end
		return fmt_safe("stack  %s = %s   (%s)", sec, tostring(cur), from)

	elseif i == 2 then return "stack  -5"
	elseif i == 3 then return "stack  -1"
	elseif i == 4 then return "stack  +1"
	elseif i == 5 then return "stack  +5"

	elseif i == 6 then
		local k = (G.bal_scope == "kind") and G.bal_kind(sec) or nil
		if k then
			return fmt_safe("stack  changing: everything of kind %s", k)
		end
		return "stack  changing: this item only"

	elseif i == 7 then
		return "stack  back to the file's number"

	elseif i == 8 then
		return fmt_safe("stack  write %s change(s) to the log",
		                tostring(G.bal_count()))
	end
	return nil
end

function G.bal_row_show(i, sec)
	if not (G.enabled and G.balance) then return false end
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return false end
	if type(i) ~= "number" or i < 1 or i > BAL_ROWS then return false end

	-- Nothing to put back, and nothing to write out: the two rows that
	-- would do nothing are the two that are not offered.
	if i == 7 then
		local where, key = G.bal_target(sec)
		return (key and G.stk_over[where][key]) and true or false
	end
	if i == 8 then
		return G.bal_count() > 0
	end
	return true
end

function G.bal_row_do(i, sec)
	if not G.bal_row_show(i, sec) then return false end

	if i == 1 then return true          -- the readout; the menu comes back
	elseif i == 2 then return G.bal_bump(sec, -5)
	elseif i == 3 then return G.bal_bump(sec, -1)
	elseif i == 4 then return G.bal_bump(sec,  1)
	elseif i == 5 then return G.bal_bump(sec,  5)
	elseif i == 6 then
		G.bal_scope = (G.bal_scope == "kind") and "sec" or "kind"
		return true
	elseif i == 7 then return G.bal_set(sec, nil)
	elseif i == 8 then
		G.bal_dump()
		return true
	end
	return false
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE ROWS, PUT INTO THE MENU'S OWN TABLE
--
-- UIInventory.properties is read by InitProperties every time a menu
-- is built: index for the order, a name function, which bags and modes
-- it belongs to, preconditions, an action. Adding a row is adding an
-- entry, which is why this is eight lines rather than a fork of the
-- window.
--
-- ONCE PER MENU OBJECT, and never taken out again. With the option off
-- every precondition answers false and not one of them is drawn, which
-- is the same result as removing them and cannot go wrong halfway.
--
-- INDEX 91 AND UP. The stock entries run to 25 and other mods add
-- their own; ours sit under all of it, where a debug block belongs.
--
-- mode and cont ARE ALREADY IN KEY FORM. The window converts the
-- entries it was born with in its constructor, which has long since
-- run by the time this is called - so an array here would be a row
-- that never matches any bag.
-- ============================================================
local BAL_CONT = {
	actor_bag = true, actor_equ = true, actor_belt = true,
	npc_bag = true, actor_trade = true, actor_trade_bag = true,
	npc_trade = true, npc_trade_bag = true,
}
local BAL_MODE = { inventory = true, loot = true, trade = true }

function G.bal_rows_into(gui)
	if not (gui and type(gui.properties) == "table") then return false end
	if gui.grid_bal_rows then return true end
	gui.grid_bal_rows = true

	for i = 1, BAL_ROWS do
		gui.properties["grid_bal_" .. i] = {
			index         = 90 + i,
			name_func     = { "GRID_BalName", i },
			mode          = BAL_MODE,
			cont          = BAL_CONT,
			precondition1 = { "GRID_BalCond", i },
			action        = { "GRID_BalAct", i },
		}
	end

	log("balance: %s stack rows on the item menu", tostring(BAL_ROWS))
	return true
end

-- ============================================================
-- HOW MANY ARE IN THIS SQUARE, COUNTED HERE
--
-- MEASURED, NOT BELIEVED. Four releases of stacking failed in the game
-- while passing every check here, and this is the line they all died
-- on. The probe in 0.18.0 asked the player's own game and it answered:
--
--   [GRID]   the first cell counts its children as THREW
--   [GRID]   and its childs field is table
--
-- `UICellItem:CountChilds` is
--
--     return self.childs and table_size(self.childs) or 0
--
-- and the function is called `size_table`. `table_size` does not exist.
-- The line is a typo, it is in ALL SEVEN copies of utils_ui.script in
-- this pack, and nothing in the base game ever calls CountChilds - so
-- it has never thrown for anybody but me. A cell with no children
-- returns 0 without evaluating it; the moment one thing is put in a
-- square, asking how many are in it raises.
--
-- Which came back as "I could not count it", which this read as "no
-- room", which the log printed as `every stack of them is full`. Four
-- releases of nothing stacking, from one word in somebody else's file.
--
-- So it is not asked. `childs` is a plain Lua table on the cell and
-- counting it is three lines that cannot throw.
-- ============================================================
function G.count_in(ci)
	if not ci then return 0 end

	local ok, n = pcall(function()
		local t = ci.childs
		if type(t) ~= "table" then return 0 end
		local k = 0
		for _ in pairs(t) do k = k + 1 end
		return k
	end)

	return (ok and type(n) == "number") and n or 0
end

-- Is there room in this cell for one more? A cell holds the item it was
-- made for plus its children, so what is in it is one more than the
-- children.
function G.room_in(ci, lim)
	if not ci then return false end
	if type(lim) ~= "number" or lim <= 1 then return false end

	return (1 + G.count_in(ci)) < lim
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE OTHER STACK OF THE SAME THING
--
-- FindSimilar hands back the FIRST cell of a section and does not ask
-- how full it is - it has never had to. So once a stack is capped, a
-- full one must not be the end of the answer, or everything after the
-- first five bandages gets a square of its own for ever.
--
-- THE RULES FOR WHAT MAY SHARE A SQUARE WITH WHAT ARE THIS MOD'S NOW.
-- They began as the window's, copied out of it, and two of them have
-- since been widened deliberately - a part-used thing stacks with its
-- own kind, and wear decides nothing. What is left is: dont_stack,
-- upgrades, and food whose freshness does not match. See G.may_stack
-- and G.same_stack, each of which says why.
-- ============================================================
-- ONLY A REASON REFUSES
--
--   "when loading a save some things get stacked but then i move
--    something or equip a mask and inventory resorts items get
--    unstacked and manual stacking by droping the same item onto
--    another not work at all"
--
-- Both halves of that were one gate. Filling the window stacks things,
-- because the window's own FindSimilar hands back a cell with room and
-- this mod only checks the CEILING on that path. Laying the grid out
-- again asks this instead - and so does a drop by hand - and this said
-- no.
--
-- It said no because it was written to say no whenever it could not
-- tell. A question this could not answer - an object the level would
-- not hand over, a call that threw - came back as "these do not go
-- together", so every unreadable field was a refusal, and a refusal is
-- an item back in a square of its own.
--
-- That is the wrong default here, and it is not the window's. The
-- window refuses on POSITIVE EVIDENCE: this section is marked
-- dont_stack, this one has been part used, this one has upgrades, these
-- two conditions are more than a tenth apart. Anything it cannot
-- establish, it lets through. So does this now, and every refusal says
-- which rule it was, in the log, once.
-- ============================================================
-- Does this thing have wear on it? The window's own test, and the ORDER
-- it comes in matters more than the test does - see G.may_stack.
function G.worn_kind(obj, sec)
	if SYS_GetParam(1, sec, "use_condition") then return true end

	local ok, yes = pcall(function()
		local c = obj:clsid()
		return IsWeapon(nil, c) or IsOutfit(nil, c) or IsHeadgear(nil, c)
	end)
	return (ok and yes) and true or false
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE ONE PLACE A CONDITION STILL DECIDES ANYTHING
--
-- For a gun part, a tool or a repair kit the bar is WEAR, and once you
-- have decided worn things stack, wear is cosmetic - a pile of springs
-- is a pile of springs.
--
-- For food and drink the same bar is HOW ROTTEN IT IS, and that is not
-- cosmetic: a pile that mixes a fresh tin with a putrid one is a pile
-- you eat out of without knowing which you got.
--
-- BY KIND, not by section, so a mod that adds a new tin gets the rule
-- without anybody editing anything - the same reasoning the stack
-- ceilings are keyed by kind.
-- ============================================================
local SPOILS = {
	i_food          = true,
	i_drink         = true,
	i_mutant_cooked = true,
	i_mutant_raw    = true,
}

G.spoil_memo = G.spoil_memo or {}

-- Declared here as well as in refresh_settings, because stack_limit can
-- be asked a question before the settings have ever been read - a
-- callback on the first frame, a container built during the load - and
-- a nil here would be an error inside the one function the whole grid
-- leans on.
G.stk_why  = G.stk_why or {}
G.bal_seen = G.bal_seen or {}

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT YOU SET IN THE MENU
--
-- Two tables, matching the two rungs of the file it stands in front of:
-- a number for one exact section, and a number for a whole kind. Kept
-- in the SAVE rather than in the file, because the point of the menu is
-- that you never leave the game.
--
-- NOT CLEARED BY refresh_settings. Everything else worked out from the
-- file is thrown away whenever an option changes, and these are not
-- worked out from anything - they are what you said.
-- ============================================================
G.stk_over = G.stk_over or { sec = {}, kind = {} }

-- Which of the two the menu's plus and minus write to. Remembered
-- across a right-click, and saved, because picking it again for every
-- item would be the tool getting in the way of the job.
G.bal_scope = G.bal_scope or "sec"

function G.spoils(sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" or sec == "" then return false end

	local had = G.spoil_memo[sec]
	if had ~= nil then return had end

	local kind = trim(SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind"))
	local yes = (kind and SPOILS[kind]) and true or false
	G.spoil_memo[sec] = yes
	return yes
end

function G.may_stack(obj, sec)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return false, "no section" end
	if SYS_GetParam(1, sec, "dont_stack") then
		return false, "the game marks it dont_stack"
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- A PART-USED ONE IS STILL THE SAME THING
	--
	--   "the items with uses dont combine into stacks in inventory"
	--
	-- The window refuses to stack a multiuse item that has been used at
	-- all, and this used to copy it - a medkit with two of its three
	-- uses left got a square to itself, and so did the next one, and
	-- the player watched their medical pouch turn into a row of
	-- singles. Their log said it in as many words:
	--
	--     medkit would not go in that square: it has been part used
	--
	-- IT IS NOT THE WINDOW THAT DECIDES HERE. The grid does its own
	-- stacking - G.same_stack and ci:AddChild - so copying that rule was
	-- a choice rather than a constraint, and the choice was wrong.
	--
	-- The number on the square is the TOTAL USES LEFT, which is what the
	-- window already writes on a stack of full ones, so a pile of mixed
	-- medkits reads as "six uses of medkit" and that is the honest
	-- number.
	--
	-- AND NOTHING READS max_uses ANY MORE. It used to be handed back so
	-- same_stack could shortcut on it - "a multiuse thing that got this
	-- far is full, so let it in" - and with the shortcut gone there was
	-- one call left that nothing used the answer of. A value returned
	-- to nobody is a value somebody restores a rule around.
	-- ============================================================

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND UPGRADES ARE ASKED ABOUT LAST, OR NOT AT ALL
	--
	-- This is where nothing stacked. The window asks `has_upgrades`
	-- only AFTER it has established that the thing has wear on it -
	-- anything with no condition has already returned a cell two lines
	-- earlier and never reaches the question. A bandage is never asked
	-- whether it is upgraded.
	--
	-- This copy asked everything, so whatever that function answers for
	-- a bandage - and anything at all that is not `false` is a yes in
	-- Lua, including a zero and an empty table - refused every
	-- unconditioned item in the game. Which is bandages, food, parts,
	-- patches: everything the ceilings are for.
	--
	-- It cost a release to find because the WINDOW's copy still worked:
	-- filling the list stacks through FindSimilar, which returns before
	-- this question, and only laying the grid out afterwards asked it.
	-- So things stacked on load and came apart on the next rebuild.
	--
	-- The order is the rule. Same order as the window, same answers.
	-- ============================================================
	if not G.worn_kind(obj, sec) then return true end

	local up_of = rawget(_G, "has_upgrades")
	if type(up_of) ~= "function" then
		local ui = rawget(_G, "utils_item")
		up_of = ui and ui.has_upgrades
	end
	if type(up_of) == "function" then
		local ok_g, up = pcall(up_of, obj)
		if ok_g and up then return false, "it has upgrades on it" end
	end

	return true
end

function G.same_stack(ci, obj, sec)
	if not ci then return false, "no cell" end
	if ci.section ~= sec then return false, "a different thing" end

	local mine, why = G.may_stack(obj, sec)
	if not mine then return false, why end

	-- The one already in the cell has to pass the same test, or a full
	-- medkit joins a half-used one from the side nobody was looking at.
	-- If the level will not hand the object over there is nothing to
	-- test, and nothing to test is not a reason to say no.
	local o2 = ci.ID and level.object_by_id(ci.ID)
	if o2 then
		local theirs, why2 = G.may_stack(o2, ci.section)
		if not theirs then return false, "the one already there: " .. tostring(why2) end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND WEAR NO LONGER DECIDES ANYTHING, EXCEPT FOR FOOD
	--
	-- The window puts two worn things together only when their
	-- condition is within a tenth, and this used to copy that. It meant
	-- a handful of gun springs off four different rifles was four
	-- squares of one spring, which is not what a pile of springs is.
	--
	-- FOOD IS THE EXCEPTION AND IT IS NOT AN ARBITRARY ONE. On a part
	-- the bar is wear and wear is cosmetic once worn things stack; on a
	-- tin it is how rotten the tin is, and a square that mixes a fresh
	-- one with a putrid one is a square you eat out of blind.
	-- ============================================================
	-- THE FRESHNESS GATE IS OPT-IN. "let everything stack no matter
	-- the % even meat" - so by default a fresh tin and a rotten one
	-- share a square. Nothing is averaged or lost: the pile is only
	-- DRAWN together, each object keeps its own condition and comes
	-- back out with it. grid_stack_fresh puts the tenth-of-condition
	-- rule back for anyone who wants food and drink kept apart.
	if not (G.stack_fresh and o2 and G.spoils(sec)) then return true end

	local ok_c, apart = pcall(function()
		return math.abs(o2:condition() - obj:condition())
	end)
	if not (ok_c and type(apart) == "number") then return true end
	if apart >= 0.1 then
		return false, "one of them is further gone than the other"
	end
	return true
end

--
-- AND NOT ONE WORD ABOUT THE CELL WE CAME FROM.
--
--   ! No such operator [__eq] defined in class [UICellItem]
--   ! zzz_amp_grid.script(1130) : FindSimilar
--   FATAL ERROR
--
-- The first version of this skipped the full cell by writing
-- `ci ~= skip`. A UICellItem is an engine class with no equality
-- operator, so comparing two of them is not false - it takes the game
-- down, on the first inventory opened. It is a house rule in this
-- project and it was written down, and I wrote it anyway.
--
-- Nothing is lost by leaving it out: the only cell ever handed in is
-- one the caller has just found to be FULL, and a full cell is refused
-- by the room check like any other.
function G.next_stack(cc, obj, sec, lim, to_hand)
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return false, "no container" end

	local seen, full, why = 0, 0, nil

	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		-- NOT INTO A PILE THE PLAYER MADE - UNLESS THIS IS A NEW ARRIVAL.
		--
		--   "item stacking automatically when pickup is still not working"
		--
		-- The by-hand skip is here to stop the TIDYING from draining one
		-- squared pile into another the player arranged. But it also
		-- stopped a freshly PICKED-UP thing - which has no square at all
		-- - from joining a pile you made by hand, and since most piles
		-- ARE made by hand, that read as "stacking on pickup does not
		-- work, 99% of the time". A pickup joining your pile is exactly
		-- what you want; a squared item keeps its own square and never
		-- reaches here. So `to_hand` (a genuinely new arrival) is allowed
		-- into a by-hand pile; the tidying still is not.
		if ci and ci.section == sec
		   and (to_hand or not G.byhand[ci.ID]) then
			seen = seen + 1
			if not G.room_in(ci, lim) then
				full = full + 1
			else
				local ok, said = G.same_stack(ci, obj, sec)
				if ok then return ci end
				why = why or said
			end
		end
	end

	-- WHY NOT, in the words of the rule that said so. A stack that does
	-- not happen looks exactly like a stack that is not allowed, and
	-- "nothing stacked" is not something a log should have to be read
	-- twice to explain.
	if seen == 0 then return false, "nothing of its kind on the grid yet" end
	if full == seen then return false, "every stack of them is full" end
	return false, why or "no reason given"
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE STACK THIS THING GOES IN, ASKED OF NOBODY ELSE
--
--   "stacking does not work at all. sometimes badnage gets stackes
--    sometimes not but 99% not"
--
-- Laying the grid out used to ask the window's FindSimilar and take
-- whatever came back. That is one function on a shared class, and in a
-- three-hundred-mod pack it is not ours: Anomaly Magazines replaces the
-- whole of it (rax_stacking_control.script), the icon framework has its
-- own copy, and whoever's chunk runs last owns it. Ours can sit on top
-- of theirs or underneath it depending on load order, and underneath it
-- the ceilings are simply not consulted.
--
-- So the layout no longer asks. It looks through the cells itself, with
-- this mod's ceiling and the window's own compatibility rules copied
-- into G.may_stack. FindSimilar is still wrapped, because the window
-- calls it when something arrives and that path has to honour the
-- ceiling too - but nothing in here depends on which wrap won.
-- ============================================================
function G.pick_stack(cc, obj, sec, to_hand)
	if not (cc and obj and type(sec) == "string") then return false end
	if not (G.enabled and G.stack) then return false end

	-- The container's own disable_stack is NOT asked here, and asking
	-- would be a dead line: G.claim writes that flag from this mod's
	-- switch at the top of every rebuild and this only ever runs after
	-- it. G.pick_one, which the player's own drop goes through, has no
	-- rebuild in front of it and does ask.
	local lim = G.stack_limit(sec)
	if lim <= 1 then return false, "no ceiling for it" end

	return G.next_stack(cc, obj, sec, lim, to_hand)
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE STACK THAT IS AT THE SQUARE THIS ONE REMEMBERS
--
-- Not "a stack of these with room in it" - THAT one. A rider going back
-- into any pile of its kind that happens to have space is how a pile
-- the player made by hand quietly fills up with things they did not put
-- there, and how the pile it came from empties.
-- ============================================================
function G.stack_at(cc, obj, sec, at)
	if not (cc and cc.cell and obj and at and type(sec) == "string") then
		return false
	end
	if not (G.enabled and G.stack) then return false end

	local lim = G.stack_limit(sec)
	if lim <= 1 then return false end

	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local a = ci and ci.section == sec and ci.area
		if a and a.y == at.y and a.x == at.x
		   and G.room_in(ci, lim) and G.same_stack(ci, obj, sec) then
			return ci
		end
	end
	return false
end

-- One line per SECTION per rebuild, not per item: forty bandages that
-- would not stack is one thing gone wrong, not forty.
function G.said_why(said, sec, why)
	if not (G.debug and said and type(sec) == "string") then return end
	if said[sec] then return end
	said[sec] = true
	dbg("%s did not stack: %s", sec, tostring(why))
end

function G.claim(cc)
	if not (cc and ours(cc.ID)) then return end
	-- The window's own switch is still the master: off means nothing
	-- shares a square with anything, whatever the ceilings say. On, the
	-- ceilings decide - and a section with no ceiling has one of one,
	-- which is the same answer.
	cc.disable_stack = (G.enabled and not G.stack) and true or false
end

-- ============================================================
-- ONE MEASUREMENT, ONCE, CHANGING NOTHING
--
-- This is how the counting bug above was found, after four releases of
-- reasoning about it. It stays, because the next thing I am wrong about
-- will be found the same way: ask the running game, in the player's own
-- save, and write the answer down.
--
-- Every line of it is a QUESTION. Nothing here places, moves, merges or
-- removes anything, and it runs once per session behind the debug
-- switch. disable_stack is put back exactly as it was found, because
-- the one question worth asking is what the window says when it is
-- allowed to answer.
-- ============================================================
function G.probe(cc)
	if G.probed or not G.debug then return end
	if not (cc and cc.cell and ours(cc.ID)) then return end

	-- Two cells holding the same kind of thing: the exact situation a
	-- stack is supposed to collapse.
	local seen, one, two = {}, nil, nil
	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		if ci and ci.ID and type(ci.section) == "string" then
			if seen[ci.section] then
				one, two = seen[ci.section], ci
				break
			end
			seen[ci.section] = ci
		end
	end

	if not two then return end
	G.probed = true

	local sec = two.section
	local obj = level.object_by_id(two.ID)

	local function ask(f)
		local ok, v = pcall(f)
		if not ok then return "THREW" end
		if v == nil then return "nil" end
		return tostring(v)
	end

	log("--- one measurement, %s ---", tostring(sec))
	log("  two of them are in two cells, %s and %s",
	    tostring(one.indx), tostring(two.indx))
	log("  kind=%s dont_stack=%s use_condition=%s multiuse=%s",
	    ask(function() return SYS_GetParam(0, sec, "kind") end),
	    ask(function() return SYS_GetParam(1, sec, "dont_stack") end),
	    ask(function() return SYS_GetParam(1, sec, "use_condition") end),
	    ask(function() return IsItem("multiuse", sec) end))
	log("  ceiling %s, and this file counts %s in the first cell",
	    tostring(G.stack_limit(sec)), tostring(G.count_in(one)))
	log("  the window's own count says %s",
	    ask(function() return one:CountChilds() end))

	-- What the window itself says, with its own switch out of the way.
	local was = cc.disable_stack
	cc.disable_stack = false
	local answer = ask(function()
		local ci = cc:FindSimilar(obj, sec)
		if not ci then return "nothing" end
		return "a cell, index " .. tostring(ci.indx)
	end)
	cc.disable_stack = was

	log("  asked whether one of these has a cell already, it says: %s", answer)
	log("--- end of measurement ---")
end


-- The lists of ours this window has. Asked of the window rather than
-- worked out from its mode: the trade window fills actor_trade_bag and
-- leaves actor_bag hidden, and a wrap that only knew one name did
-- nothing at all at a trader.
-- ============================================================
-- WHAT IS IN A LIST, BY THE CONTAINER'S OWN RECORD
--
-- indx_id is [object id] = cell index. It is the table the window's own
-- refresh reads and writes:
--
--     local list_a = cc_a.indx_id
--     for id,obj in pairs(inv_a) do
--         if (not list_a[id]) then cc_a:AddItem(...) end
--
-- so it is the exact statement of what the list holds, rather than a
-- count or a checksum that two changes could cancel out. Being fooled
-- would cost the player their layout, so this is not the place to be
-- clever.
--
-- Written into a table the caller owns: no allocation on a path that
-- runs several times a second.
-- ============================================================
function G.id_set(cc, into)
	for k in pairs(into) do into[k] = nil end

	local n = 0
	if cc and cc.indx_id then
		for id in pairs(cc.indx_id) do
			into[id] = true
			n = n + 1
		end
	end
	return n
end

-- The same list again, against what was written down. Same members and
-- same count - a subset of the right size is the same set.
function G.same_ids(cc, was, n_was)
	if not (was and n_was) then return false end

	local n = 0
	if cc and cc.indx_id then
		for id in pairs(cc.indx_id) do
			if not was[id] then return false end
			n = n + 1
		end
	end
	return n == n_was
end

function G.our_ccs(gui)
	local out = {}
	if not (gui and gui.CC) then return out end

	for i = 1, #OUR_ORDER do
		local cc = gui.CC[OUR_ORDER[i]]
		if cc then out[#out + 1] = cc end
	end
	return out
end

function G.relayout(cc)
	if not (G.enabled and cc and ours(cc.ID)) then return end
	local ok, err = pcall(function() return G.restore(cc, G.contents(cc)) end)
	if not ok then log("!relayout: %s", tostring(err)) end
end

-- ============================================================
-- MOVING SOMETHING BY HAND
--
-- The window has no such interaction: dropping a dragged item on empty
-- space means "put it in the other container", and inside its own
-- container it means nothing at all. So the cell under the cursor is
-- worked out from the panel's rectangle and the drop is answered here,
-- before the base is told about it.
--
-- The arithmetic is the container's own: a cell is grid_size plus the
-- line between cells, and the horizontal one is stretched by the screen
-- ratio exactly as the cells themselves are.
--
-- MEASURED FROM THE CONTENT, NOT THE WINDOW. cc.st is the static the
-- scroll view carries and the parent of every cell, so row one of the
-- grid is at its top wherever the list has been scrolled to. The window
-- does not move, so counting from it needs a scroll offset - and the
-- only one to hand, cc.scroll_pos, is written once when a DRAG BEGINS
-- and is the wrong container's when the drag began somewhere else.
-- ============================================================
function G.cell_at(cc)
	local rect = G.content(cc)
	if not rect then return nil end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	local cur = GetCursorPosition()
	local cw = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line) * ratio
	local ch = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line)
	if cw <= 0 or ch <= 0 then return nil end

	local col = math.floor((cur.x - rect.x1) / cw) + 1
	local row = math.floor((cur.y - rect.y1) / ch) + 1

	if col < 1 then col = 1 end
	if row < 1 then row = 1 end
	return row, col
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHERE A THING WOULD GO, AIMED FROM ITS MIDDLE
--
--   "i noticed that the hitbox when dragging items around is the top
--    left cell for items, that makes large items to feel weird to move
--    around as i need to aim where to put the left top cell instead
--    where to put the center of the item"
--
-- Right, and it was never a decision - the square under the cursor was
-- the square the item's corner went in because that is the square
-- G.cell_at answers with. For a medkit the two are the same thing; for
-- a rifle the corner is two cells left of where you are pointing, and
-- you end up aiming at empty space beside the gap you mean.
--
-- So the cursor holds the MIDDLE. An item w cells across is offset by
-- floor((w-1)/2), which puts the cursor in the middle column of an odd
-- width and just left of centre on an even one - a rifle's third cell
-- of five, a pistol's first of two.
--
-- ONE ANSWER FOR BOTH the drop and the highlight that promises it,
-- because the two disagreeing by a cell is worse than either being
-- wrong on its own.
-- ============================================================
function G.aim(cc, w, h)
	local row, col = G.cell_at(cc)
	if not row then return nil end

	if type(w) == "number" and w > 1 then col = col - math.floor((w - 1) / 2) end
	if type(h) == "number" and h > 1 then row = row - math.floor((h - 1) / 2) end

	if col < 1 then col = 1 end
	if row < 1 then row = 1 end
	return row, col
end

-- Where the grid's own first square is on screen, right now.
function G.content(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st) then return nil end

	local ok, r = pcall(function()
		local fr = Frect():set(0, 0, 0, 0)
		cc.st:GetAbsoluteRect(fr)
		return fr
	end)
	if not (ok and r) then return nil end
	return r
end
-- ============================================================
-- IS ONE OF THE OTHER MOD'S WINDOWS IN THE WAY?
--
--   "when box window is over the inventory grid and i try to put
--    something inside then the item is dropped in the cell that was
--    directly under the window"
--
-- The box and rig windows float OVER the list. The container has no
-- idea they exist - IsCursorOverWindow answers for the list's own
-- rectangle and nothing else - so a drop meant for a window looked
-- exactly like a drop on the square underneath it.
--
-- WHOEVER IS ON TOP GETS THE DROP, which is the rule Armor Mag Pouches
-- already follows in the other direction ("Before the panel, because
-- the window is drawn on top of it: what you can see under the cursor
-- is what the drop should reach"). This file cannot see those windows,
-- so it asks, and standing aside is all it has to do: the drop then
-- falls through to that mod's own hook, which is the one that knows
-- what to do with it.
--
-- The order is not a choice either of them made. Zone Grid wraps
-- On_CC_DragDrop at actor_on_first_update and Armor Mag Pouches wraps
-- it when its file loads, so the grid's wrapper ends up outermost and
-- is asked first. Answering "not mine" is how the inner one gets its
-- turn.
--
-- Silent when the other mod is not installed, or is older than the
-- door: pcall, and a false.
-- ============================================================
local function amp_window_over()
	local ok, over = pcall(function()
		return zzz_armor_mag_pouches.grid_window_over()
	end)
	return (ok and over) and true or false
end


-- ============================================================
-- SOMETHING ARRIVING FROM SOMEWHERE ELSE
--
--   "items that are getting unequpped by hand from the character are
--    not placed when droped"
--
-- Dragging out of an equipment slot, off a body, or out of a trader's
-- list is not a MOVE within this list, so the drop below hands it
-- straight back to the window - which is right, because the window is
-- what actually transfers the object. But the square you let go over is
-- thrown away in the process, and the item then lands wherever the
-- window's packer found room first.
--
-- So the square is written into the layout BEFORE the window is allowed
-- to do the transfer. The packer reads the layout now (see
-- G.find_bounded), so by the time the object exists in this list there
-- is already an answer waiting for it - and if the transfer never
-- happens, an unused note against an id that is not here does no harm
-- and is overwritten the next time that item is put down.
-- ============================================================
-- ...AND NOTHING IS AIMED AT A SQUARE UNDER A WINDOW EITHER. This
-- catches an item arriving from somewhere else and points it at the
-- square under the cursor; if that square is behind one of the other
-- mod's windows, the item is not coming to the grid at all.
function G.aim_incoming(gui, bag_from, idx_from)
	if amp_window_over() then return false end
	if not (G.enabled and gui and gui.CC) then return false end

	-- A move inside one of our own lists is the drop's business.
	if ours(bag_from) then return false end

	local from = gui.CC[bag_from]
	if not from then return false end

	local obj = from.GetObj and from:GetObj(idx_from)
	if not obj then return false end

	-- WHICH of our lists the cursor is over. Only one is ever shown, but
	-- asking rather than assuming is what made the trade window work.
	local cc
	for _, name in ipairs(OUR_ORDER) do
		local c = gui.CC[name]
		local ok_s, shown = pcall(function() return c and c:IsShown() end)
		if ok_s and shown then
			local ok_o, over = pcall(function() return c:IsCursorOverWindow() end)
			if ok_o and over then cc = c break end
		end
	end
	if not (cc and writes_layout(cc.ID)) then return false end

	local id
	pcall(function() id = obj:id() end)
	if not id then return false end

	local w, h = G.size_of(obj:section())
	if not w then return false end

	-- Turned relative to however it lies, the same as every drop.
	local eff = G.rot[id] and true or false
	if w ~= h and G.hand_turned() then eff = not eff end
	if eff then w, h = h, w end

	local row, col = G.aim(cc, w, h)
	if not row then return false end

	local z = G.zones()
	if not (z and G.fits(z, row, col, w, h, cc.cols)) then return false end
	if not cc:IsFreeRoom(row, col, w, h) then return false end

	G.rot[id] = eff or nil
	G.pos[id] = { x = col, y = row, w = w, h = h }
	dbg("%s is coming in at %s,%s", tostring(id), tostring(row), tostring(col))
	return true
end

-- ============================================================
-- A SQUARE HELD FOR SOMETHING THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET
--
--   "when takeing item out of the box to inventory the items does not
--    go to the slot i hover over but to the first that is free"
--
-- Same table and the same idea as aim_incoming above, and a different
-- caller: that one is handed an object being DRAGGED in and can ask it
-- anything. This one is for an item about to be CREATED - a box handing
-- its contents back makes a server object now and the client object
-- turns up a frame or two later, so at the moment the square is chosen
-- there is nothing to pass to GetObj and nothing to read a section off.
--
-- The caller knows both, so the caller says both. Everything after that
-- is aim_incoming's tail, unchanged: the square under the cursor, is it
-- inside a zone, is it free, write it down.
--
-- MARKED AS THE PLAYER'S. Choosing where something lands is placing it
-- by hand, and a hand-placed item is not gathered up by the auto-stack
-- on the next rebuild. Without that the square would be honoured once
-- and lost on the first thing that rebuilt the list, which is the same
-- as not honouring it at all.
--
-- PUBLIC. Armor Mag Pouches calls this and does not require it: a note
-- against an id that never arrives is overwritten the next time that
-- item is put down, and no answer at all just means the packer picks.
-- ============================================================
function G.reserve(id, sec, turn)
	-- THE FRONT DOOR SAYS IT WAS KNOCKED ON. 1.98.2's exits all name
	-- themselves and the log showed none of them - so the question
	-- became whether this function runs at all when the sibling mod
	-- asks. One line answers it.
	dbg("reserve asked: %s (%s)", tostring(id), tostring(sec))
	if not (G.enabled and type(id) == "number" and type(sec) == "string") then
		return false
	end

	-- EVERY NO SAYS WHY. The reserve refused ammunition in a running
	-- game and the log had nothing to say about it - eight silent
	-- gates, one of them lying, and no way to tell which from outside.
	local function no(why, ...)
		dbg("reserve said no for %s (%s): " .. why, tostring(id),
		    tostring(sec), ...)
		return false
	end

	local gui = ui_inventory and ui_inventory.GUI
	if not (gui and gui.CC) then return no("no inventory gui") end

	local cc
	for _, name in ipairs(OUR_ORDER) do
		local c = gui.CC[name]
		local ok_s, shown = pcall(function() return c and c:IsShown() end)
		if ok_s and shown then
			local ok_o, over = pcall(function() return c:IsCursorOverWindow() end)
			if ok_o and over then cc = c break end
		end
	end
	if not cc then return no("the cursor is over none of our lists") end
	if not writes_layout(cc.ID) then
		return no("%s does not keep a layout", tostring(cc.ID))
	end

	local w, h = G.size_of(sec)
	if not w then return no("it has no grid size") end

	-- WHICH WAY IT LANDS. The caller says - it holds the entry and
	-- the key; this function holds neither.
	local turned = false
	if turn and w ~= h then w, h = h, w turned = true end

	local row, col = G.aim(cc, w, h)
	if not row then return no("no square under the cursor") end

	local z = G.zones()
	if not z then return no("no zones") end
	if not G.fits(z, row, col, w, h, cc.cols) then
		return no("%s,%s does not fit any section", tostring(row), tostring(col))
	end

	local ok_r, free = pcall(function() return cc:IsFreeRoom(row, col, w, h) end)
	if not ok_r then return no("the window would not answer IsFreeRoom") end

	-- ============================================================
	-- A TAKEN SQUARE MIGHT BE THE DESTINATION
	--
	--   "droping the same item ... onto an existing item also dont
	--    just makes a new instance on the first free slot"
	--
	-- The most natural drop there is - bandages onto your own stack
	-- of bandages - failed the free-room check every time. When the
	-- square is taken, ask WHO took it: a cell of this section with
	-- room under its ceiling is the pile being aimed at. The
	-- newcomer remembers the PILE'S anchor (the cursor may be on the
	-- middle of a wide pile) and is NOT marked by-hand, so the
	-- gathering pass walks it in through G.stack_at - and if the
	-- stacking rules refuse it there, it falls to the first gap as
	-- it always did. The aim degrades to the old answer, never to a
	-- refusal.
	-- ============================================================
	if not free then
		local lim = G.stack_limit(sec)
		if lim <= 1 then
			return no("%s,%s is taken and it does not stack",
			          tostring(row), tostring(col))
		end

		local pile
		for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
			local a = ci and ci.section == sec and ci.area
			if a and row >= a.y and row < a.y + (a.h or 1)
			     and col >= a.x and col < a.x + (a.w or 1)
			     and G.room_in(ci, lim) then
				pile = a
				break
			end
		end
		if not pile then
			return no("%s,%s is taken and holds no pile of it",
			          tostring(row), tostring(col))
		end

		G.remember(id, { x = pile.x, y = pile.y, w = w, h = h })
		dbg("holding the pile at %s,%s for %s (%s)",
		    tostring(pile.y), tostring(pile.x), tostring(id), tostring(sec))
		return true
	end

	G.rot[id] = turned or nil
	G.remember_mine(id, { x = col, y = row, w = w, h = h })
	dbg("holding %s,%s for %s (%s), which does not exist yet",
	    tostring(row), tostring(col), tostring(id), tostring(sec))
	return true
end

-- ============================================================
-- A SQUARE THAT IS ALREADY SPOKEN FOR, SHARED
--
--   "one item moves when i dropped it and the rest of the stack goes to
--    first free slot in invnetory instead"
--
-- G.reserve finds a FREE square under the cursor, which is right for
-- one thing arriving and useless for the second, third and fourth: the
-- first one has landed on it and it is not free any more.
--
-- This is the other half. It does not look for a square at all - it
-- copies the one another id already has, and marks the newcomer as a
-- RIDER so it does not compete for the anchor. Which is exactly what
-- the window's own packing does with a stack, and what G.rides is for.
--
-- PUBLIC, like G.reserve, and just as optional: no square for `like`
-- means no answer, and the packer picks as it always did.
-- ============================================================
function G.ride(id, like)
	if not (G.enabled and type(id) == "number" and type(like) == "number") then
		return false
	end

	local at = G.pos[like]
	if not (at and at.x and at.y) then return false end

	G.remember(id, at)
	G.rides(id)
	-- A rider lies the way its anchor lies - they share one square.
	G.rot[id] = G.rot[like] or nil

	dbg("%s rides in %s,%s with %s", tostring(id),
	    tostring(at.y), tostring(at.x), tostring(like))
	return true
end

function G.drop_at(gui, bag_from, idx_from)
	if not (G.enabled and gui and gui.CC) then return false end
	if not ours(bag_from) then return false end
	if amp_window_over() then return false end

	local cc = gui.CC[bag_from]
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return false end

	local ok_c, over = pcall(function() return cc:IsCursorOverWindow() end)
	if not (ok_c and over) then return false end

	local obj = cc:GetObj(idx_from)
	if not obj then return false end

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE SQUARES DECIDE. NOTHING ELSE DOES.
	--
	--   "if the item is dropped to different place and if any of the
	--    cells is already occupied the item can be moved there only if
	--    those occupied cells owned by the item itself, if any cell is
	--    occupied by other item then it cannot be moved there"
	--
	-- That is the whole rule and it is now the only one. There used to
	-- be a test up here that asked the window which CELL the cursor was
	-- over and handed the drop back if there was one, on the grounds
	-- that a drop onto another item is a stack or a swap. Two things
	-- were wrong with it.
	--
	-- It was WRONG IN GAME. GetCell_Focused hands back the OBJECT under
	-- the cursor, not a cell record - Armor Mag Pouches uses the same
	-- call to find the magazine an ammo box was dropped on. So reading
	-- an .ID off it got nil every time, "is this me" was never true,
	-- and every nudge of a wide item was refused with a red rectangle,
	-- which is exactly what you saw.
	--
	-- And it was UNNECESSARY. The room check below already answers the
	-- question properly: the item lets go of its own squares first, so
	-- squares it owns do not count against it, and any square another
	-- item holds does. Onto another item the room check says no, this
	-- returns false, and the window does its own stack or swap - which
	-- is what handing it back was for.
	-- ============================================================

	local w, h = G.size_of(obj:section())
	if not w then return false end

	-- THE R KEY HAS ITS SAY. The sibling mod owns the key and its MCM
	-- binding; the hand is read through it, and the footprint tested
	-- is the drop's own - turned RELATIVE to however the item already
	-- lies, so one press on a sideways rifle stands it back up.
	local did = obj:id()
	local eff = G.rot[did] and true or false
	if w ~= h and G.hand_turned() then eff = not eff end
	if eff then w, h = h, w end

	-- The size FIRST: where the corner goes depends on how big the
	-- thing is, because the cursor is holding its middle.
	local row, col = G.aim(cc, w, h)
	if not row then return false end

	-- Inside ONE zone. Half in your pockets and half in your pack is
	-- not a place a thing can be.
	local z = G.zones()
	if not (z and G.fits(z, row, col, w, h, cc.cols)) then return false end

	local ci = cc.cell[idx_from]
	local was = ci and ci.area

	-- Let go of where it is before asking whether it can go there, or an
	-- item can never be nudged one square: it is in its own way.
	if was then cc:FreeRoom(was.y, was.x, was.w, was.h) end

	if cc:IsFreeRoom(row, col, w, h) then
		-- WRITTEN WHERE THE MOVE LANDS, and nowhere else: a refused
		-- drop leaves the item lying exactly as it was.
		G.rot[did] = eff or nil

		local area = cc:TakeRoom(row, col, w, h)
		if ci then
			ci.area = area
			pcall(function() ci:Set(obj, area) end)
		end
		G.remember_mine(did, area)
		if ci and ci.childs then
			for cid in pairs(ci.childs) do G.remember(cid, area) end
		end
		dbg("moved %s to %s,%s", tostring(obj:id()), tostring(row), tostring(col))
		return true
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- OR ONTO ONE OF ITS OWN KIND, BY HAND
	--
	--   "add manual stacking too"
	--
	-- The square is taken, and it may be taken by exactly the thing this
	-- one belongs with. Dropping a bandage on a bandage now puts it in
	-- that stack, up to the same ceiling the automatic gathering uses -
	-- so a stack that is full refuses, and refusing looks the way it
	-- always did: the item goes back where it came from.
	--
	-- The cell under the CURSOR, not the corner the item would take: you
	-- point at the stack you mean.
	-- ============================================================
	local sec = obj:section()
	local into = G.cell_under(cc, idx_from)
	local may, why = G.pick_one(cc, into, obj, sec)

	if into and not may then
		dbg("%s would not go in that square: %s", tostring(sec), tostring(why))
	elseif not into then
		dbg("%s dropped on a square with no cell in it", tostring(sec))
	end

	if into and may then
		-- THE WHOLE STACK, not the one on top of it. A square holding
		-- three bandages is three bandages to the player, and dragging
		-- it somewhere means all three - so everything in the cell goes,
		-- in order, until the other one is full. What will not fit stays
		-- behind in the square it was in, which is still its square.
		local lim = G.stack_limit(sec)
		local going = { obj }
		if ci and ci.childs then
			for cid in pairs(ci.childs) do
				local o = level.object_by_id(cid)
				if o then going[#going + 1] = o end
			end
		end

		local moved = 0
		for i = 1, #going do
			local o = going[i]
			if G.room_in(into, lim) and G.same_stack(into, o, sec)
			   and into:AddChild(o) then
				local oid = o:id()
				cc:RemoveItem(o, sec)
				cc:AddIndex(oid, sec, into.indx)
				if into.area then G.remember(oid, into.area) end
				G.rides(oid)
				-- A rider lies the way its pile lies.
				G.rot[oid] = (into.ID and G.rot[into.ID]) or nil
				moved = moved + 1
			end
		end

		if moved > 0 then
			local left = cc.cell[idx_from]
			if was and left and left.ID then
				cc:TakeRoom(was.y, was.x, was.w, was.h)
			end
			dbg("stacked %s of %s into cell %s",
			    tostring(moved), tostring(sec), tostring(into.indx))
			return true
		end
	end

	if was then cc:TakeRoom(was.y, was.x, was.w, was.h) end
	return false
end

-- The cell the cursor is over, found from the squares rather than asked
-- of the window: GetCell_Focused hands back an OBJECT, not a cell, and
-- reading a cell field off it is how the nudge was broken for a week.
--
-- `skip` is the index being dragged, compared as a NUMBER. Two cells are
-- never compared - UICellItem has no equality operator and comparing
-- two of them takes the game down.
function G.cell_under(cc, skip)
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return nil end

	local row, col = G.cell_at(cc)
	if not row then return nil end

	for indx, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local a = ci and ci.ID and ci.area
		if a and indx ~= skip
		   and row >= a.y and row < a.y + (a.h or 1)
		   and col >= a.x and col < a.x + (a.w or 1) then
			return ci
		end
	end
	return nil
end

-- May this one join that cell? The same ceiling and the same rules the
-- gathering uses, asked of one named cell instead of all of them.
function G.pick_one(cc, ci, obj, sec)
	if not (cc and ci and obj and type(sec) == "string") then
		return false, "nothing to put it in"
	end
	if not (G.enabled and G.stack) then return false, "stacking is off" end
	if cc.disable_stack then return false, "this list does not stack" end

	local lim = G.stack_limit(sec)
	if lim <= 1 then return false, "no ceiling for it" end
	if not G.room_in(ci, lim) then return false, "that stack is full" end

	return G.same_stack(ci, obj, sec)
end

-- What is sitting under a rectangle of the grid, and how many things
-- are. `skip` is an index, compared as a NUMBER - two cells are never
-- compared, UICellItem has no equality operator.
function G.cell_over(cc, row, col, w, h, skip)
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return nil, 0 end

	local hit, n = nil, 0
	for indx, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local a = ci and ci.ID and ci.area
		if a and indx ~= skip
		   and not (row + h <= a.y or a.y + (a.h or 1) <= row
		            or col + w <= a.x or a.x + (a.w or 1) <= col) then
			hit = ci
			n = n + 1
		end
	end
	return hit, n
end

-- ============================================================
-- ONE OFF THE TOP OF A STACK YOU ARE HOLDING
--
--   "when a stack of items is held and i tap shift one of the items
--    from stack gets placed when the highlight shows, and clicking
--    shift in the same spot drops one more and one more there"
--
-- The tap puts down ONE of the ones riding along, at the square the
-- drag is aimed at. The one in your hand stays in your hand, so the
-- drag carries on and you let go of the rest wherever you like.
--
-- The second tap on the same square finds what the first tap put there
-- and adds to it, up to the ceiling - which is the "one more and one
-- more" of it. When that stack fills, the tap does nothing rather than
-- putting an item somewhere nobody pointed at.
-- ============================================================
-- The list of ours a drag is live in, if any. Asked by the split and by
-- the key, which has to know whether something is in the player's hand
-- before it decides whose key it is.
function G.drag_in(gui)
	if not (G.enabled and gui and gui.CC) then return nil end

	local mine = G.our_ccs(gui)
	for i = 1, #mine do
		local c = mine[i]
		if c and c.hold and c.hold.idx and c.cell and c.cell[c.hold.idx] then
			return c
		end
	end
	return nil
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHY THE TAP DOES NOT LET GO OF THE STACK
--
-- 1.75.0 emptied the hand after a tap, because the window runs the list
-- by itself whenever anything is held and the cursor is near an edge -
-- Scroll_DragDrop_Ctrl - and an empty hand gives it nothing to run for.
-- It was asked for and it was tried:
--
--   "the fix would be after clicking the drop button also force release
--    left click even if player is still holding it"
--   "does not help at all, still scroll by its own"
--
-- IT ALSO TOOK A BRAKE OFF THE WORSE OF THE TWO SCROLLERS. utils_ui's
-- On_Scroll, in order:
--
--     if self.disable_scroll then ... return end
--     if self.scroll_pause then ... return end
--     if self.hold.idx or self.owner.item_in_hold then ... return end
--     -- and here: any disagreement between where the engine says the
--     -- view is and where the container last put it is read as the
--     -- wheel, and answered by moving the list a whole row, every
--     -- frame, until they agree
--
-- An item in hand is one of the three things that stop that. Emptying
-- the hand while the button is still down leaves only scroll_pause
-- between a held button and the runaway - so a change meant to quieten
-- the edge-scroll unhooked a brake on the amplifier.
--
-- So the stack stays in your hand, and taking several off one grab
-- works as it always did. If the edge-scroll turns out to be the mover
-- after all, the answer is to stop THAT while a tap is in progress, not
-- to throw away a guard that holds off something worse.
-- ============================================================
function G.split_one(gui)
	if not (G.enabled and G.stack) then return false end

	local cc = G.drag_in(gui)
	if not (cc and writes_layout(cc.ID)) then return false end
	if cc.disable_stack then return false end

	local held = cc.cell[cc.hold.idx]
	local sec = held.section
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return false end

	local w, h = G.size_of(sec)
	if not w then return false end

	local row, col = G.aim(cc, w, h)
	if not row then return false end

	local z = G.zones()
	if not (z and G.fits(z, row, col, w, h, cc.cols)) then
		return false, "off the grid"
	end

	-- ONE OF THE ONES RIDING ALONG, and there is no guard above asking
	-- whether there are any: if the cell holds only the item you are
	-- carrying then `childs` is empty, this loop finds nothing, and the
	-- line below is the whole of the answer. A guard would be a second
	-- way of saying no and a line no test could ever tell apart.
	local kid
	for id in pairs(held.childs or {}) do kid = id break end
	local obj = kid and level.object_by_id(kid)
	if not obj then return false, "only the one in your hand is left" end

	-- Put back what the window will not take, so a refused tap costs
	-- nothing.
	local function give_back()
		pcall(function()
			held:AddChild(obj)
			cc:AddIndex(kid, sec, held.indx)
		end)
	end

	local into, over = G.cell_over(cc, row, col, w, h, cc.hold.idx)

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND THE LIST DOES NOT MOVE WHILE THIS HAPPENS
	--
	--   "when unstacking the inventory still scrolls up not fully but do
	--    a smaller or bigger jump. i changed the keybind to see if maybe
	--    shift by itself is doing it but no, the new keybind i set was
	--    doing that but shift wasnt then"
	--
	-- That experiment settled it: the jump follows the TAP, not the key,
	-- so it is this code and not the window's shift handling. And the
	-- only thing in here that touches the scroll is AddItemInCell, which
	-- ends in Scroll_Reinit - the window sizing its view to the content
	-- and putting the bar back by its own arithmetic, off row_end and a
	-- clamp to whatever the view can reach at that moment.
	--
	-- I am not going to work out which term in that is moving; the
	-- layout has not asked the window to do this since 0.9 and neither
	-- does this now. ignore_scroll while the cell is placed, and the bar
	-- put back where the player had it, which is the same thing
	-- G.restore does at the end of every rebuild.
	-- ============================================================
	local looking
	pcall(function() looking = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)

	local was_ignoring = cc.ignore_scroll
	cc.ignore_scroll = true

	local ok, done, why = pcall(function()
		-- An empty square: it starts a stack of its own there.
		if over == 0 then
			if not cc:IsFreeRoom(row, col, w, h) then
				return false, "no room there"
			end

			cc:RemoveItem(obj, sec)

			local area = cc:TakeRoom(row, col, w, h)
			local indx = cc.idxer + 1
			if cc:AddItemInCell(obj, sec, indx, area) then
				cc:AddIndex(kid, sec, indx)
				cc.idxer = indx
				G.remember_mine(kid, area)
				dbg("one %s put down at %s,%s", tostring(sec),
				    tostring(row), tostring(col))
				return true
			end

			cc:FreeRoom(area.y, area.x, area.w, area.h)
			give_back()
			return false
		end

		-- Or the stack the last tap started, which is the same square
		-- again.
		if over == 1 then
			local may, no = G.pick_one(cc, into, obj, sec)
			if not may then return false, no end

			cc:RemoveItem(obj, sec)

			if into:AddChild(obj) then
				cc:AddIndex(kid, sec, into.indx)
				if into.area then G.remember(kid, into.area) end
				G.rides(kid)
				dbg("one more %s onto the stack at cell %s", tostring(sec),
				    tostring(into.indx))
				return true
			end

			give_back()
			return false
		end

		return false, "more than one thing is under it"
	end)

	cc.ignore_scroll = was_ignoring and true or false
	G.look_again(cc, looking)

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND SAY WHERE THE LIST WAS, EITHER SIDE OF THAT
	--
	--   "the scroll jump is less ofter im sure but still hapens"
	--
	-- Less often, still there - so one cause went and another did not,
	-- and I do not know what the other is. Three things can move that
	-- bar and only one of them is a tap:
	--
	--   * this, which now holds the scroll still and puts it back;
	--   * G.restore, which does the same on every rebuild;
	--   * and the window's own Scroll_DragDrop_Ctrl, which scrolls the
	--     list a step at a time, by itself, whenever something is held
	--     and the cursor is over the strip at the top or bottom of the
	--     list. That is not a jump per tap at all - it is the list
	--     running while the cursor rests near an edge, which would read
	--     as a jump if it happened between one tap and the next, and
	--     which is why holding Shift used to make it worse.
	--
	-- So both of mine now say where the bar was before and after. If
	-- neither moved it, it is the third.
	-- ============================================================
	local ended
	pcall(function() ended = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)
	if looking ~= ended then
		dbg("!the tap moved the list: %s -> %s",
		    tostring(looking), tostring(ended))
	else
		dbg("the tap left the list at %s", tostring(looking))
	end

	if not ok then return false, tostring(done) end
	return done, why
end

-- ============================================================
-- PUT THE BAR BACK, AND DO NOT REBUILD THE VIEW TO DO IT
--
--   "when item is in a stack and held then if i pess the button to drop
--    one and then move my mouse while still holding left click, even a
--    one nanometer the ivnentory jumps UP no matter if i moved the mosue
--    up down left or right"
--
-- That is the whole answer, and it is a better bug report than anything
-- I worked out in three releases. On the NEXT FRAME, always UP, whatever
-- the mouse did. So the tap leaves the view in a state that the next
-- redraw resolves by going to the top - and my own tracing was blind to
-- it, because the number this file reads back is the one it just wrote.
-- The number was never wrong. The VIEW was.
--
-- Scroll_Reinit is what does that: it calls scroll:Clear() and then
-- AddWindow again, which is the engine's scroll view being emptied and
-- refilled. Whatever the engine makes of its offset in between, it
-- settles it on the next layout pass, and the next layout pass is the
-- next mouse movement.
--
-- And it was never needed here. That call exists to resize the view to
-- the content, and the content is `(row_end + 1) * cell` - row_end being
-- forced to the full height of the grid at the end of every rebuild, so
-- that the bottom of an empty rucksack can be reached. Putting one item
-- down cannot change it. There is nothing to resize.
--
-- So: write the window's own number, set the bar, and leave its view
-- alone.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- AND THE TRICK THE ENGINE'S OWN CODE USES
--
-- From utils_ui.UICellContainer:Scroll_SetPos, their comment, their
-- words:
--
--     -- This trick is used because for some reason the scroll doesn't
--     -- have full scale on resetting, unless if we set its pos at
--     -- least once
--     self.scroll:SetScrollPos(1)
--
-- So a scroll view that has just been rebuilt DOES NOT KNOW HOW TALL IT
-- IS until something writes a position into it. Until it does,
-- GetMaxScrollPos reads short - and Scroll_SetPos clamps against that
-- and then WRITES THE CLAMPED NUMBER BACK into scroll_pos. One frame of
-- a view that has not got its scale back is enough to record "the top"
-- as the place to go back to, permanently.
--
-- That is very probably the jump, and it is why writing the number once
-- was never enough: the write was fine and the view threw it away.
-- ============================================================
function G.put_back(cc, at)
	if not (cc and cc.scroll and type(at) == "number") then return end

	-- Their line, for their reason. It costs one call and it is the
	-- difference between a view that can hold the number and one that
	-- clamps it to zero. It also has to come FIRST, because everything
	-- below asks the view questions and a view with no scale answers
	-- zero to all of them.
	pcall(function() cc.scroll:SetScrollPos(1) end)

	-- NO FURTHER THAN THE LIST ACTUALLY GOES. Take a backpack off and
	-- the grid is shorter; a position from the old one is past the end
	-- of the new.
	--
	-- NOT THROUGH THE CONTAINER'S OWN Scroll_SetPos, which was tried and
	-- taken back out: it also applies "a grid shorter than its own
	-- window does not scroll at all", and that rule reads row_end while
	-- the view reads its own content height. The two do not agree in
	-- every state, and picking one of them blind is how the last four
	-- releases went. The view is the thing the player is looking at, so
	-- the view is the authority here - and the memory is made to match
	-- it below, which is all On_Scroll ever wanted.
	pcall(function()
		local far = cc.scroll:GetMaxScrollPos()
		if type(far) == "number" and at > far then at = far end
	end)
	if at < 0 then at = 0 end

	pcall(function() cc.scroll:SetScrollPos(at) end)

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND THE MEMORY IS WHAT THE VIEW ACTUALLY HOLDS
	--
	--   "turn it off and the issue is still here"
	--
	-- cc.scroll_pos is not a note to ourselves. Both branches of
	-- utils_ui's On_Scroll exist to reconcile it with where the view
	-- really is - one FORCES the view to it every frame while something
	-- is held, the other reads any disagreement as the wheel and answers
	-- with a whole row, every frame, until they agree.
	--
	-- Give either of them a number the view cannot hold and it can never
	-- finish. The log, with the mod that was blamed for this switched
	-- off entirely:
	--
	--     0 -> 158 | held=8 pause=true      sixteen times, identical
	--     5 -> 44 -> 87 -> 130 -> 158       then back to 0, then up
	--
	-- One disagreement, seen through the two branches. It got there
	-- because the memory was written by hand and the view was written
	-- through the engine object, which clamps silently - so the view
	-- took what it could hold and the memory kept what it was given.
	--
	-- The container's own Scroll_SetPos never has this problem: it
	-- clamps and writes the clamped number back. This does the same.
	-- ============================================================
	local landed
	pcall(function() landed = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)
	if type(landed) ~= "number" then landed = at end
	cc.scroll_pos = landed

	-- AND IT SAYS WHERE IT LANDED, because a caller that goes on
	-- comparing against what it ASKED for will read its own clamp as the
	-- window having moved the list behind its back - and a false report
	-- of this bug is worse than no report.
	return landed
end

function G.look_again(cc, looking)
	if not (cc and type(looking) == "number") then return end

	G.put_back(cc, looking)

	-- And watch the next few frames, because this is where it has been
	-- going wrong and nothing here can see that from inside this call.
	-- TWELVE, not four: a view gets its scale back on a layout pass, and
	-- the layout pass is the next time the mouse moves.
	G.after_tap = { bag = cc.ID, at = looking, left = 12 }
end

-- ============================================================
-- AND WHAT THE ENGINE DID WITH IT A FRAME LATER
--
-- Called from the drag's own per-frame path - the one that moves the
-- held icon with the cursor - for a few frames after a tap. If the list
-- is not where the tap left it, that is the reported jump, and this is
-- the first thing in four releases that is standing in the right place
-- to see it.
--
-- It puts it back as well. A window's worth of frames, then it stops,
-- so the list is the player's again - including the window's own
-- scrolling at the edges, which they use and asked to keep.
-- ============================================================
function G.tap_settled(cc)
	local w = G.after_tap
	if not (w and cc and cc.scroll) then return end

	-- ============================================================
	-- TWELVE MOUSE MOVEMENTS, NOT TWELVE FRAMES
	--
	-- This used to be called only from the drag's per-frame hook, which
	-- runs when the mouse moves and something is held - so counting
	-- calls WAS counting movements. It is called from the frame hook
	-- now, because a tap empties the hand and there is no drag left to
	-- hang off, and that one runs whether the mouse moved or not.
	--
	-- Twelve frames is a fifth of a second. Tap, sit still for a beat,
	-- then nudge the mouse, and the watch would be gone before the
	-- thing it watches for arrived - which is precisely the case that
	-- was reported. So the cursor is remembered and the count only
	-- comes down when it has actually changed.
	-- ============================================================
	local cur = GetCursorPosition()
	local x, y = cur and cur.x, cur and cur.y
	local moved = (x ~= w.cx) or (y ~= w.cy)
	w.cx, w.cy = x, y

	if moved then
		w.left = (w.left or 0) - 1
		if w.left <= 0 then G.after_tap = nil end
	end

	-- BOTH NUMBERS. The view's own position is only half of it: the
	-- other half is the container's memory of where it is looking, and
	-- that is the one Scroll_SetPos clamps and writes back every frame
	-- while something is in hand. A view sitting in the right place with
	-- a container that has written down zero is a list that jumps the
	-- moment anything asks it to redraw - and the old check here, which
	-- only ever read the view, could not see that at all.
	local now
	pcall(function() now = cc.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)
	local mine = cc.scroll_pos

	if now == w.at and mine == w.at then return end

	dbg("!the list slipped after the tap: wanted %s, the view says %s, "
	    .. "the window says %s - putting it back",
	    tostring(w.at), tostring(now), tostring(mine))

	G.put_back(cc, w.at)
end

-- ============================================================
-- ONE PER PRESS
--
-- A key that is already down does not keep pouring the stack out. The
-- release is what re-arms it, so this is the key's real state rather
-- than a guess at how fast a person taps - and if the engine repeats a
-- held key, a stack of forty does not land in one square while the
-- player is still deciding.
-- ============================================================
function G.on_key(gui, dik, action)
	if not G.enabled then return end
	if type(dik) ~= "number" or dik ~= G.split_key then return end

	local ev = rawget(_G, "ui_events")
	local down = ev and ev.WINDOW_KEY_PRESSED
	local up   = ev and ev.WINDOW_KEY_RELEASED

	-- The release is passed on unless the press was not: handing the
	-- window a release it never saw the press for is how a flag gets
	-- stuck, and holding_shift stuck on is worse than anything this
	-- feature does.
	if up and action == up then
		G.key_down = false
		if G.key_eaten then
			G.key_eaten = false
			return true
		end
		return
	end

	if not (down and action == down) then return end
	if G.key_down then return end
	G.key_down = true

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHILE SOMETHING IS IN YOUR HAND, THIS KEY IS OURS
	--
	--   "there is issue with invneotry sometimes scrolling up when
	--    clickig shift or holding it a bit longer, aslo when item is
	--    held and shift is clicked on slot that is occupied by different
	--    item nothing should happen as by default shift clicking when
	--    hovering over items, drops them on the ground"
	--
	-- Both of those are the same press reaching the window, and neither
	-- is worth working around one at a time:
	--
	--   * UICellContainer:OnKeyboard sets `scolling_power` to THREE
	--     while shift is down, and Scroll_DragDrop_Ctrl scrolls the
	--     list by that many cells a step whenever something is held and
	--     the cursor is near an edge. Tap shift mid-drag and the list
	--     runs for the top.
	--   * UIInventory:OnKeyboard sets `holding_shift`, and shift with
	--     the mouse is the window's own "put it on the ground". Trying
	--     to take one off a stack and losing the whole stack on the
	--     floor is not a trade anybody would make.
	--
	-- So while a drag of ours is live the press is not passed on AT
	-- ALL - whether or not there was anywhere to put one down. Nothing
	-- happens, which is what was asked for.
	--
	-- Only while dragging: shift is the window's key the rest of the
	-- time, and faster scrolling with it held is worth keeping.
	-- ============================================================
	local cc = G.drag_in(gui)
	if not cc then return end

	local ok, why = pcall(G.split_one, gui)
	if not ok then log("!split: %s", tostring(why)) end

	G.key_eaten = true
	return true
end

-- ============================================================
-- MAKING ROOM FOR THE RIG BLOCK
--
--   "i wanted so the slot is in scrollable space as well as the mag
--    slots from the rig, and they can go out of the visible space when
--    inventory is scrolled down, like in tarkov."
--
-- ROWS, then. 0.6 moved the bag's whole panel down instead, so the
-- block sat in a strip above the scrolling area and never moved. That
-- did keep it still, and still was not what was wanted.
--
-- Rows are part of the grid, so they scroll like everything else and
-- carry the rig block with them - Armor Mag Pouches anchors it to the
-- same scrolling content these rows are counted in. The rows are kept
-- EMPTY two ways over: no zone covers them, so this file will not offer
-- them; and they are taken in the container's own occupancy map, so the
-- window's own packing cannot use them either. That second one is what
-- stops an item appearing underneath the rig.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- ...AND A BLOCK THAT CANNOT BE MEASURED HAS NOT GONE AWAY
--
-- Armor Mag Pouches measures the block from its own DRAWN rectangle,
-- so before it has been drawn once - the first rebuild after a load -
-- there is nothing to measure and the answer is 0. Taking that as the
-- truth puts every section three rows too high for exactly one pass,
-- and the log shows what that costs:
--
--     layout: 19 of 34 kept, 10 outside the section ... (29699 at 11,5)
--     layout: 29 of 34 kept, 0 outside the section ...
--
-- Ten squares outside the section on the first pass and none on the
-- second, with nothing moved in between. The first pass repacked those
-- ten into the section it thought it had and WROTE THE NEW SQUARES
-- DOWN, so by the time the block was measured the layout was already
-- gone. Everything after that looks healthy, which is why this took
-- four goes to find.
--
-- THREE ANSWERS, NOT TWO. A height, and whether it was measured or
-- assumed:
--
--   measured        use it, and remember it
--   cannot measure  use the last one that WAS measured, and say so -
--                   G.head_sure goes false, and the layout is not
--                   written from a pass that is only assuming
--   no block at all  zero, and that is a measurement like any other
--
-- The third is what keeps the second honest: with Armor Mag Pouches
-- absent there is no block to wait for, so zero is the truth and the
-- remembered height is dropped rather than believed forever.
-- ============================================================
function G.head_rows()
	if not G.enabled then G.head_sure = true return 0 end

	local amp = rawget(_G, "zzz_armor_mag_pouches")
	if not (amp and amp.grid_block_rows) then
		G.head_sure, G.head_known = true, nil
		return 0
	end

	local ok, n = pcall(amp.grid_block_rows)
	if not ok or type(n) ~= "number" or n ~= n then n = 0 end

	n = math.floor(n)
	if n < 0 then n = 0 end
	if n > 20 then n = 20 end

	if n > 0 then
		G.head_known, G.head_sure = n, true
		return n
	end

	if G.head_known then
		G.head_sure = false
		return G.head_known
	end

	G.head_sure = true
	return 0
end

-- ============================================================
-- EVERYTHING THAT IS NOT A SECTION IS TAKEN
--
-- There are four kinds of square on this grid that no item may sit in:
-- the rows Armor Mag Pouches' rig block stands on, the gutter down the
-- left, the header row above each section, and any square to the right
-- of a section that is narrower than the panel.
--
-- They were reserved one rule at a time and each rule was another thing
-- to keep in step with G.fits. So they are not: this walks the grid and
-- takes every square G.fits refuses. One answer, asked once, and a
-- section that changes shape cannot leave a hole behind it.
--
-- Reset has just wiped the map and make_rows has just declared every
-- square free, so this is the only moment they can be claimed.
--
-- Twenty-odd rows by eight columns is two hundred questions on a
-- rebuild, which is nothing next to what a rebuild already does.
-- ============================================================
function G.take_dead(cc, z, rows)
	if not (cc and z and rows and cc.cols) then return 0 end

	local n = 0
	for r = 1, rows do
		for c = 1, cc.cols do
			if not G.fits(z, r, c, 1, 1, cc.cols) then
				local ok = pcall(function() cc:TakeRoom(r, c, 1, 1) end)
				if ok then n = n + 1 end
			end
		end
	end
	return n
end

-- ============================================================
-- ONE RECTANGLE, WRITTEN ONLY WHEN IT HAS CHANGED
--
-- The borders are now re-stated every frame rather than once per
-- rebuild, because once per rebuild is what left them behind:
--
--   "when i try to put and multi cell item when two sections border
--    eachother then the border visual gets bugged and does not mach the
--    space, fixes on invnetory reopen"
--
-- A drop can make the container grow a row and re-flow its scroll, and
-- nothing told the borders. Saying it every frame cannot go stale - but
-- every one of these is a window inside a scroll view, and a SetWndPos
-- on one of those is not free. So the numbers are remembered and the
-- engine is only called when they actually differ.
-- ============================================================
function G.put_st(st, x, y, w, h, a, r, g, b, show)
	local m = G.stm[st]
	if not m then m = {} G.stm[st] = m end

	if m.x ~= x or m.y ~= y then
		m.x, m.y = x, y
		pcall(function() st:SetWndPos(vector2():set(x, y)) end)
	end
	if m.w ~= w or m.h ~= h then
		m.w, m.h = w, h
		pcall(function() st:SetWndSize(vector2():set(w, h)) end)
	end
	if m.a ~= a or m.r ~= r or m.g ~= g or m.b ~= b then
		m.a, m.r, m.g, m.b = a, r, g, b
		pcall(function() st:SetTextureColor(GetARGB(a, r, g, b)) end)
	end

	show = show and true or false
	if m.vis ~= show then
		m.vis = show
		pcall(function() st:Show(show) end)
	end
end

-- ONE CONTROL PUT AWAY, AND NOT PUT AWAY TWICE. Show is a call across
-- into the engine like any other, and on a child of a scroll view it is
-- a re-flow of the parent. Three copies of these five lines had grown
-- up in this file before a fourth was written without them - the loop
-- that hides the unused grounds - and it cost three calls a frame for
-- as long as an inventory was open. One name now, so the fifth cannot.
function G.hide_st(w)
	if not w then return end
	local m = G.stm[w]
	if not m then m = {} G.stm[w] = m end
	if m.vis == false then return end
	m.vis = false
	pcall(function() w:Show(false) end)
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE GROUND IS MADE BEFORE ANY CELL IS, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE TRICK
--
-- Draw order is creation order and cannot be changed. The item cells
-- are children of the SAME window as everything this mod draws into a
-- list:
--
--     self.cell[indx] = UICellItem(self, self.st, indx)     -- utils_ui
--     cc.xml:InitStatic(cc.path .. ":st", cc.st)            -- G.new_st
--
-- and a cell is made lazily, the first time an item goes in that index.
-- So a static made during a draw pass is made AFTER every cell that
-- already exists, and would be drawn on top of their icons. The border
-- lines get away with that because they are one pixel at a cell
-- boundary; a solid rectangle would not.
--
-- InitControls is where the container makes self.st. Nothing has been
-- put in the list yet and no cell can exist, so anything made here is
-- behind all of them for the life of that container.
--
-- MADE HIDDEN AND NEVER REMADE. The draw pass only moves, tints and
-- shows them; G.new_st is never called for these again.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- ONE POOL PER CONTAINER, AND THE CONTAINER KEEPS IT
--
-- These pools used to live in tables keyed by the container's ID -
-- "actor_bag", "npc_bag" - which reads as one pool per list and is not
-- one. The window builds a container for EVERY grid size it can draw
-- at, all with the same ID: one live one and three hidden ones behind
-- it, all constructed in the same breath. The first one in owned the
-- pool and the other three drew into a window nobody could see.
--
-- It cost nothing while the first one made was the live one. It cost
-- everything the moment something re-made the pool later - which is
-- exactly what the corpse patch's handshake does - because then the
-- LAST container to ask owned it, and the last one is hidden.
--
-- A pool now lives on the container that made it, where it is exactly
-- as long-lived as the statics in it and there is nothing to look up.
-- ============================================================
function G.make_fill(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and ours(cc.ID)) then return end
	if cc.g_fill then return end

	local pool = {}
	for i = 1, FILL_MAX do
		local st = G.new_st(cc)
		if not st then break end
		pcall(function() st:Show(false) end)
		pool[i] = st
	end

	cc.g_fill = pool
	dbg("%s: %s ground(s) made before any cell", tostring(cc.ID), #pool)
end

function G.new_st(cc)
	local ok, st = pcall(function()
		local s = cc.xml:InitStatic(cc.path .. ":st", cc.st)
		s:InitTexture("ui_inGame2_white_rect")
		return s
	end)
	if not (ok and st) then return nil end
	return st
end

-- The grounds behind the fresh cells. Made once, in InitControls, for
-- the SAME reason the section grounds are (see G.make_fill): a static
-- born during a draw pass draws on top of every cell that already
-- exists, and a glow on top of an icon is not a glow behind it. Forty
-- is more fresh cells than a single looting produces; past that the
-- oldest simply go unlit until a hover thins the set.
local GLOW_MAX = 40

-- ============================================================
-- AND THE LINES, WHICH WERE THE ONE POOL MADE DURING A DRAW
--
--   "the grid lines are in front of the item icons"
--
-- The note above G.make_fill says why that cannot work, and this file
-- has been getting away with it on a technicality it wrote down at the
-- time:
--
--     "The border lines get away with that because they are one pixel
--      at a cell boundary; a solid rectangle would not."
--
-- Which is true of a ONE-SQUARE item, where a boundary line runs along
-- the icon's own edge and is invisible against it. It is not true of
-- anything taller or wider: an ammo box two squares tall has a
-- division straight across its middle, and being one pixel does not
-- help when the pixel is in the middle of a picture.
--
-- So the lines are made the way the grounds and the glows are: before
-- any cell exists, in InitControls, on the container that owns them.
--
-- ON THE CONTAINER, which is the other half of the same fix. This pool
-- was still keyed by cc.ID - the exact arrangement the note above
-- G.make_fill describes as a bug, kept because nothing had made it
-- bite yet. The window builds a container for every grid size it can
-- draw at, all with the same ID, and whichever asked LAST owned the
-- pool.
--
-- SIZED FROM THE SETTINGS RATHER THAN A CONSTANT. A section is four
-- frame lines, one division per column after the first and one per row
-- after the first, so the count follows the numbers the player set: a
-- 400-cell backpack four squares wide really is a hundred rows of
-- lines, and one constant would either starve that or make two hundred
-- windows for everybody who does not have it.
--
-- AND IT CAN STILL GROW. If this arithmetic is ever short the draw
-- makes what it is missing, exactly as it used to. Those late ones draw
-- on top - which is the bug this exists to stop - so the slack is
-- generous and the growth is a fallback, not the mechanism.
-- ============================================================
local EDGE_SLACK = 12

function G.edge_need()
	local cols = math.max(G.w_pack or 7, G.w_bare or 4, 1)
	local thin = math.max(math.min(G.w_pack or 7, G.w_bare or 4), 1)
	local cells = math.max(G.pack or 70, G.bare or 8, 1)

	-- The tallest a section can be is the most cells over the fewest
	-- columns, and two sections can be on screen at once.
	local rows = math.ceil(cells / thin)
	local per = 4 + (cols - 1) + (rows - 1)

	return clampi(per * 2 + EDGE_SLACK, 32, 600)
end

function G.make_edge(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and ours(cc.ID)) then return end
	if cc.g_edge then return end

	local pool = {}
	for i = 1, G.edge_need() do
		local st = G.new_st(cc)
		if not st then break end
		pcall(function() st:Show(false) end)
		pool[i] = st
	end

	cc.g_edge = pool
	dbg("%s: %s line(s) made before any cell", tostring(cc.ID), #pool)
end

function G.make_glow(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and ours(cc.ID)) then return end
	if cc.g_glow then return end

	local pool = {}
	for i = 1, GLOW_MAX do
		local st = G.new_st(cc)
		if not st then break end
		pcall(function() st:Show(false) end)
		pool[i] = st
	end
	cc.g_glow = pool
	dbg("%s: %s glow(s) made before any cell", tostring(cc.ID), #pool)
end

-- ============================================================
-- AND THE RED GROUNDS, WHICH ARE THE OTHER SIDE'S
--
-- Made for the list this mod otherwise never touches - a body, a
-- stash, a box - because that is the list the question is asked in
-- front of. Same pool, same reason for making it in InitControls: a
-- static born during a draw lands on top of the icons.
-- ============================================================
local NOFIT_MAX = 40

function G.make_nofit(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and cc.ID == "npc_bag") then return end
	if cc.g_nofit then return end

	local pool = {}
	for i = 1, NOFIT_MAX do
		local st = G.new_st(cc)
		if not st then break end
		pcall(function() st:Show(false) end)
		pool[i] = st
	end
	cc.g_nofit = pool
	dbg("%s: %s red ground(s) made before any cell", tostring(cc.ID), #pool)
end

-- ============================================================
-- ...AND AGAIN, WHEN SOMEBODY HAS LAID A FLOOR OVER THEM
--
--   "red highlight for items that cannot fit into invetory is not
--    present in bodies with tarkov-like looting patch"
--
-- Statics stack in the order they were made, and there is no way to
-- ask for one to be moved. The corpse patch paints the body's own grid
-- as ONE FILLED RECTANGLE across the whole list, in this same
-- InitControls hook - and after this one, because that mod loads later
-- and so wraps this wrapper. The red grounds were still there, still
-- being written to every frame, under a floor.
--
-- Neither mod can be told to go first: `self.st` does not exist until
-- the base has run, so nothing can make a static before the base
-- returns. So the patch says when its floor is down and the grounds
-- are made again on top of it.
--
-- The old pool is put away and forgotten. Forty windows, once in the
-- life of a container - and a container is made once.
-- ============================================================
function G.nofit_again(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and cc.ID == "npc_bag") then return end

	local old = cc.g_nofit
	if old then
		for i = 1, #old do
			local st = old[i]
			pcall(function() st:Show(false) end)
		end
	end

	cc.g_nofit = nil
	G.make_nofit(cc)
	dbg("%s: red grounds made again, on top of somebody else's floor",
	    tostring(cc.ID))
end

-- The box-hover wash: one window, made before any cell for the same
-- reason the glow's are - behind the icon, or it is not a wash.
function G.make_boxhl(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and ours(cc.ID)) then return end
	if cc.g_bhl then return end

	local st = G.new_st(cc)
	if not st then return end
	pcall(function() st:Show(false) end)
	cc.g_bhl = { st }
end

-- The xml this mod parses for itself. ONE node, and only because a
-- static carries text only if its node declares a <text> child, and the
-- container's node makes no such promise. Everything else is drawn on
-- statics made from the container's own node, which is guaranteed to
-- exist because the game built the container from it.
--
-- Lazily, never at chunk scope: CScriptXmlInit is an engine call.
function G.get_xml()
	if not G.xml then
		local ok, x = pcall(function()
			local x = CScriptXmlInit()
			x:ParseFile("zzz_amp_grid.xml")
			return x
		end)
		if not (ok and x) then return nil end
		G.xml = x
	end
	return G.xml
end

-- A caption. InitTextWnd rather than InitStatic: the text is the whole
-- point of it, and only a text window carries one directly.
--
-- ONE NODE PER SECTION, because a caption's FONT and COLOUR live in the
-- xml and the three sections are three colours. Falls back to the plain
-- node for a section name the xml has never heard of, so a new zone is
-- a caption in the wrong colour rather than no caption at all.
function G.new_txt(cc, name)
	local x = G.get_xml()
	if not x then return nil end

	local path = "grid:hdr_" .. tostring(name)
	local ok, w = pcall(function() return x:InitTextWnd(path, cc.st) end)
	if ok and w then return w end

	ok, w = pcall(function() return x:InitTextWnd("grid:hdr", cc.st) end)
	if not (ok and w) then return nil end
	return w
end

function G.put_txt(w, x, y, cw, ch, str, show)
	local m = G.stm[w]
	if not m then m = {} G.stm[w] = m end

	if m.x ~= x or m.y ~= y then
		m.x, m.y = x, y
		pcall(function() w:SetWndPos(vector2():set(x, y)) end)
	end
	if m.w ~= cw or m.h ~= ch then
		m.w, m.h = cw, ch
		pcall(function() w:SetWndSize(vector2():set(cw, ch)) end)
	end
	if m.txt ~= str then
		m.txt = str
		pcall(function() w:SetText(str) end)
	end

	show = show and true or false
	if m.vis ~= show then
		m.vis = show
		pcall(function() w:Show(show) end)
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT IS DRAWN ROUND A SECTION
--
--   "the borders to be different colors, also a little bit of spacing
--    between those sections (half a cell) and for the sections to have
--    a header as wide as the section with name (pockets, armour,
--    backpack) colors (pockets milkysh pink, armour granite, backpack
--    dark green) ... and to the left of those section an icon
--    representing where this section is from (2x2 cell size)"
--
-- Seven windows per section, all of them pooled and all of them written
-- only when a number has changed:
--
--     4   the lines round the section, in its own colour
--     1   the header bar, the same colour dimmed
--     1   the caption on it
--
-- The bar sits in the LOWER half of the row above the section, so the
-- empty upper half is the half-cell of air between one section and the
-- next. That gap is a real row of the grid rather than a fudge in the
-- drawing, so nothing can ever be placed in it.
-- ============================================================
function G.draw_bounds(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st) then return end

	local z = G.enabled and G.zones()
	if not z then return G.hide_bounds(cc) end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	local cw = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line) * ratio
	local ch = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line)

	-- THE LINES LIVE ON THE CONTAINER, made in InitControls before any
	-- cell existed - see G.make_edge. The ID-keyed table is kept only
	-- for a container that somehow never got one, which is a container
	-- whose lines will draw on top; it is not the road.
	if not cc.g_edge then
		G.edge[cc.ID] = G.edge[cc.ID] or {}
	end
	G.txt[cc.ID] = G.txt[cc.ID] or {}
	local pool, words = cc.g_edge or G.edge[cc.ID], G.txt[cc.ID]

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHERE A SECTION IS, ASKED ONCE
	--
	-- The frame and the ground under it are the same rectangle, and two
	-- copies of an arithmetic that must agree is how they stop
	-- agreeing. `y0` is the section's FIRST ITEM ROW and `rows` is how
	-- many there are, so this covers the squares and nothing else - the
	-- header's half-row of air above it is not part of the band, which
	-- is what "behind where the items can be stored" asked for.
	-- ============================================================
	local function band_rect(band)
		local right = band.x0 + band.cols - 1
		if right > cc.cols then right = cc.cols end

		return (band.x0 - 1) * cw, (band.y0 - 1) * ch,
		       (right - band.x0 + 1) * cw, band.rows * ch, right
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE GROUND, FIRST AND UNDERNEATH
	--
	--   "behind where the items can be stored, so not outside the
	--    backpack grid only where it is now"
	--
	-- Inset by the border at every edge, so the fill sits INSIDE the
	-- frame rather than under it: they are different things, the same
	-- way the divisions are inset from the frame below.
	--
	-- These windows were made in G.make_fill, long before any of this
	-- ran - see the note there for why they cannot be made here.
	-- ============================================================
	local ground = cc.g_fill
	local g = 0
	if ground then
		for i = 1, #z do
			if g >= #ground then break end
			local x0, y0, W, H = band_rect(z[i])
			g = g + 1
			G.put_st(ground[g], x0 + EDGE, y0 + EDGE,
			         W - EDGE * 2, H - EDGE * 2,
			         FILL_CLR[1], FILL_CLR[2], FILL_CLR[3], FILL_CLR[4],
			         G.border)
		end

		for k = g + 1, #ground do
			G.hide_st(ground[k])
		end
	end

	local n = 0
	for i = 1, #z do
		local band = z[i]

		local x0, y0, W, H, right = band_rect(band)

		-- ============================================================
		-- FOUR LINES ROUND IT, AND THE SQUARES INSIDE IT
		--
		--   "just make a thin grey border and subtle grid
		--    visualization"
		--
		-- The header's row is still there and still empty - it is the
		-- half-cell of air between one section and the next, and every
		-- square in the column is numbered downwards from the top, so
		-- taking that row out would move every item in every save. It
		-- simply has nothing drawn in it now.
		-- ============================================================
		local put = {
			{ x0,             y0,             W,    EDGE, EDGE_CLR },
			{ x0,             y0 + H - EDGE,  W,    EDGE, EDGE_CLR },
			{ x0,             y0,             EDGE, H,    EDGE_CLR },
			{ x0 + W - EDGE,  y0,             EDGE, H,    EDGE_CLR },
		}

		-- The divisions, INSET BY THE BORDER at both ends so a line
		-- never runs into the frame: they are different things, and a
		-- grid that touches its own edge reads as a table of boxes
		-- rather than as one area with squares in it.
		for c = 1, (right - band.x0) do
			put[#put + 1] = { x0 + c * cw, y0 + EDGE,
			                  CELL_EDGE, H - EDGE * 2, CELL_CLR }
		end
		for r = 1, band.rows - 1 do
			put[#put + 1] = { x0 + EDGE, y0 + r * ch,
			                  W - EDGE * 2, CELL_EDGE, CELL_CLR }
		end

		for k = 1, #put do
			n = n + 1
			if not pool[n] then
				pool[n] = G.new_st(cc)
				if not pool[n] then return end
			end

			local p = put[k]
			local c = p[5]
			G.put_st(pool[n], p[1], p[2], p[3], p[4],
			         c[1], c[2], c[3], c[4], G.border)
		end
	end

	-- Fewer zones than last time: whatever is left over is put away.
	-- COUNTED FROM n, which is how many windows this pass actually
	-- wrote. It used to be counted from a PER_ZONE constant times the
	-- number of sections, which is the same number until somebody
	-- changes what a section is made of and forgets the constant - and
	-- with one section on screen there is no state in which the two
	-- disagreeing shows up. A number that cannot be checked is worse
	-- than no number, so this is the one the loop just produced.
	G.hide_bounds(cc, n + 1)

	-- NOTHING WRITES CAPTIONS ANY MORE, so this hides every one that a
	-- session started before the headers went away may still be
	-- holding.
	--
	-- Kept rather than deleted with the drawing: G.new_txt and
	-- G.put_txt are general and the pool is real, and a window that
	-- exists and is never hidden is a word left on the screen - which
	-- this project has just spent a release on once.
	--
	-- IT USED TO ASK A TABLE THAT WAS NEVER FILLED. `local shown = {}`
	-- at the top of the draw, `if not shown[key]` here, and nothing in
	-- between - so the test was a constant and the table was one
	-- allocation a frame to say so. G.hide_st is already guarded, so
	-- this costs one table read per leftover caption and almost always
	-- runs no times at all.
	for _, w in pairs(words) do G.hide_st(w) end
end

-- Put the edges away, from `first` on. No argument means all of them,
-- which is what a zone list that has gone away needs.
function G.hide_bounds(cc, first)
	-- THE SAME POOL THE DRAW USED. Reading the ID-keyed one here while
	-- the draw wrote the container's would leave every line on screen
	-- for ever, which is the one thing hiding exists to stop.
	local pool = cc and (cc.g_edge or G.edge[cc.ID])
	if not pool then return end

	for k = (first or 1), #pool do
		G.hide_st(pool[k])
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHERE IT WOULD LAND
--
--   "would be nice to have highlight on cells that the item will go to
--    when dropped as now its hard to tell what the hitbox is, when the
--    item cannot fit those cells cuz they are blocked or some of the
--    cells are outside the slottable section the the highlight should
--    flash red."
--
-- One rectangle over the squares the thing would take, in the same
-- coordinates the cells themselves live in - so it cannot disagree with
-- where the drop actually goes, because both read G.cell_at.
--
-- Green means it would go there. Red means it would not, for either of
-- the two reasons: something is already in those squares, or they are
-- not all inside one section. The player does not need to be told which
-- - only that letting go now does nothing.
-- ============================================================
function G.held(gui)
	if not (gui and gui.CC) then return nil end

	local id = gui.item_in_hold
	if not id then return nil end

	local src = gui.CC[id]
	if not (src and src.hold) then return nil end

	local idx = src.hold.idx
	if not idx then return nil end

	local ok, obj = pcall(function() return src:GetObj(idx) end)
	if not (ok and obj) then return nil end
	return obj, src, idx
end

-- ============================================================
-- ...OR WHAT THE SIBLING MOD IS CARRYING
--
-- Armor Mag Pouches drags things that are not in the menu's hands: a
-- magazine out of a rig, an entry out of a box. Those are not objects
-- in a container - the box entry is not an object at all until it is
-- put down - so G.held finds nothing and the rectangle never appeared
-- on the way OUT of a box, only on the way in.
--
-- It answers with a section, which is all this needs.
-- ============================================================
function G.borrowed()
	local ok, sec, turned = pcall(function()
		return zzz_armor_mag_pouches.grid_carrying()
	end)
	if not (ok and type(sec) == "string" and sec ~= "") then return nil end
	return sec, turned and true or false
end

function G.draw_hover(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st) then return end

	local pool = cc.g_hi
	if not pool then pool = {} cc.g_hi = pool end

	local function off()
		-- All five - the rectangle and the box border's four
		-- hairlines - and the wash behind the icons with them.
		for i = 1, 5 do
			if pool[i] then G.put_st(pool[i], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false) end
		end
		local wash = cc.g_bhl and cc.g_bhl[1]
		if wash then G.put_st(wash, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false) end
	end

	if not G.enabled then return off() end

	local ui = rawget(_G, "ui_inventory")
	local gui = ui and ui.GUI
	local obj, src, idx = G.held(gui)

	local ok_o, over = pcall(function() return cc:IsCursorOverWindow() end)
	if not (ok_o and over) then return off() end

	local sec, turned
	if obj then
		local ok_s, got = pcall(function() return obj:section() end)
		if not ok_s then return off() end
		sec = got

		-- The item's own lie, turned once more while the key is down.
		local hid
		pcall(function() hid = obj:id() end)
		turned = ((hid and G.rot[hid]) and true or false)
		         ~= (G.hand_turned() and true or false)
	else
		sec, turned = G.borrowed()
		if not sec then return off() end
	end

	local w, h = G.size_of(sec)
	if not w then return off() end
	if turned and w ~= h then w, h = h, w end

	-- ============================================================
	-- OVER A BOX THAT WOULD TAKE IT, THE BOX IS THE ANSWER
	--
	--   "there would be no red highlight but instead the box would
	--    get a subtle white highlight and a very thin white border,
	--    till the item is dropped into it or moved away."
	--
	-- Whether the drop would land is the sibling mod's knowledge -
	-- the same door the drop itself goes through - so red never
	-- argues with a box that is about to say yes.
	--
	-- ...AND OUT OF A BODY'S LIST TOO, since 2.21.0. This used to be
	-- "only a drag from THIS list", and the reason given was that no
	-- other drag could land in a box - which was true right up until
	-- the release that made one. Which list it came from is passed on
	-- rather than judged here, because the gates for a drag off a
	-- corpse are not the same as the gates for one out of your own
	-- bag, and they belong with the drop that enforces them.
	-- ============================================================
	local bid
	if obj and src and (src.ID == cc.ID or src.ID == "npc_bag") then
		local okb, got = pcall(function()
			return zzz_armor_mag_pouches.grid_box_wants(cc, sec, obj, src.ID)
		end)
		if okb and type(got) == "number" then bid = got end
	end

	if bid then
		local area
		for _, bci in pairs(cc.cell or {}) do
			if bci and bci.ID == bid and bci.area then area = bci.area break end
		end
		if area then
			for i = 1, 5 do
				if not pool[i] then pool[i] = G.new_st(cc) end
			end
			if pool[1] then
				local br = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
				if not (br and br > 0) then br = 1 end
				local gs, gl = cc.grid_size, cc.grid_line

				-- WHERE THE CELL ACTUALLY IS. The window places a cell at
				--
				--   x(col) = (col - 1) * size * ratio + col * line * ratio
				--   y(row) = (row - 1) * size         + row * line
				--
				-- - the column COUNT multiplies the grid line, not the
				-- count minus one - so a rectangle at (col-1)*(size+line)
				-- sat one grid line up and to the left of the icon:
				--
				--   "its not correctly aligned to the grid see
				--    screenshots. its few pixels up and to the left."
				local bxp = ((area.x - 1) * gs + area.x * gl) * br
				local byp = (area.y - 1) * gs + area.y * gl
				local bwp = (area.w * gs + (area.w - 1) * gl) * br
				local bhp = area.h * gs + (area.h - 1) * gl

				-- The subtle wash, BEHIND the icon - the window made in
				-- InitControls, before any cell existed. Drawn from the
				-- hover pool it was a fog on the box's own picture:
				--
				--   "the highlight is in front of the box"
				--
				-- No behind-window (a container from before this feature)
				-- means no wash; the hairlines still say yes.
				local wash = cc.g_bhl and cc.g_bhl[1]
				if wash then
					G.put_st(wash, bxp, byp, bwp, bhp,
					         34, 255, 255, 255, true)
				end
				G.put_st(pool[1], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false)

				-- The very thin border rides IN FRONT, where a hairline
				-- can be seen against the icon.
				if pool[2] then G.put_st(pool[2], bxp, byp,
				                         bwp, 1, 160, 255, 255, 255, true) end
				if pool[3] then G.put_st(pool[3], bxp, byp + bhp - 1,
				                         bwp, 1, 160, 255, 255, 255, true) end
				if pool[4] then G.put_st(pool[4], bxp, byp,
				                         1, bhp, 160, 255, 255, 255, true) end
				if pool[5] then G.put_st(pool[5], bxp + bwp - 1, byp,
				                         1, bhp, 160, 255, 255, 255, true) end
				return
			end
		end
	end

	local row, col = G.aim(cc, w, h)
	if not row then return off() end

	local z = G.zones()
	local inside = z and G.fits(z, row, col, w, h, cc.cols) or false

	-- FULLY OUTSIDE EVERY SECTION IS NOT A REFUSAL, it is dead panel:
	--
	--   "if full highlight will be outside of the usable grid it
	--    should be hidden."
	--
	-- A red rectangle there marked squares the player could never
	-- use. Partly inside stays red - part of the item IS over usable
	-- squares and it will not go there, which is exactly what red is
	-- for.
	if not inside then
		if not (z and G.touches(z, row, col, w, h, cc.cols)) then
			return off()
		end
	end

	-- ITS OWN SQUARES ARE NOT IN ITS WAY. Nudging a thing one column
	-- across would otherwise always read as blocked, which is the same
	-- mistake the drop itself had to be taught.
	local free = false
	if inside then
		-- Only something already IN this list has squares of its own to
		-- stand aside from. What the sibling mod is carrying is not in
		-- any list, so there is nothing to free and nothing to put back.
		local ci = src and (src.ID == cc.ID) and cc.cell and cc.cell[idx]
		local was = ci and ci.area
		if was then pcall(function() cc:FreeRoom(was.y, was.x, was.w, was.h) end) end

		local ok_f, res = pcall(function() return cc:IsFreeRoom(row, col, w, h) end)
		free = ok_f and res or false

		if was then pcall(function() cc:TakeRoom(was.y, was.x, was.w, was.h) end) end
	end

	if not pool[1] then
		pool[1] = G.new_st(cc)
		if not pool[1] then return end
	end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	local cw = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line) * ratio
	local ch = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line)

	-- Not over a willing box: the border hairlines and the wash rest.
	for i = 2, 5 do
		if pool[i] then G.put_st(pool[i], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false) end
	end
	local wash0 = cc.g_bhl and cc.g_bhl[1]
	if wash0 then G.put_st(wash0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false) end

	if inside and free then
		G.put_st(pool[1], (col - 1) * cw, (row - 1) * ch, w * cw, h * ch,
		         90, 130, 210, 130, true)
	else
		G.put_st(pool[1], (col - 1) * cw, (row - 1) * ch, w * cw, h * ch,
		         110, 210, 80, 70, true)
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE CELL IS DRESSED THE WAY THE ITEM LIES
--
-- The window draws every cell upright - utils_ui reads the section's
-- own grid numbers and knows nothing about the rot table - so a
-- sideways item's cell is corrected after the fact: heading on the
-- icon (Sota's own Add_Icon branches on GetHeading() > 0 and does its
-- rotated arithmetic when it is set), a re-Set so every layer
-- follows, and the cell's rectangle swapped to the footprint its
-- squares already hold.
--
-- A SWEEP, NOT A HOOK. Cells are made and remade down half a dozen
-- paths - restore, drop, stack, refresh - and catching every one is
-- how one gets missed. The sweep rides the window's own per-frame
-- update, compares one float per cell, and touches nothing when
-- nothing changed. It keeps running while the rot table is empty once
-- it has ever dressed anything, because the last sideways item
-- leaving must stand its recycled cell back up.
-- ============================================================
local ROT_RAD = -math.pi / 2

-- Every element of a cell that can carry a heading. The attachments
-- and icon layers are included so the community rotation fix
-- (utils_ui_icon_rotation_fix - already in this install for Sota's
-- turned equipment slots) does its rotated placement for them, and
-- so an UNdressed cell stands them all back up: these are pooled,
-- and a pooled static never stands back up on its own.
function G.cell_heads(ci, out)
	local function take(e) if e then out[#out + 1] = e end end
	take(ci.ico)      take(ci.shadow)
	-- NOT the attachments. Setting their headings up front handed
	-- them to the community fix's rotated arithmetic (tuned to the
	-- UI pack's icon sizing, not ours) - and a scope static BORN
	-- during the re-run never had a heading at all, which is exactly
	-- "the suppressor turned and the scope did not". They are placed
	-- naturally and then turned by the attachment pass in turn_cell.
	-- NOT the icon layers. Those are the tier text and the faction
	-- icon - texture-text, meant to be READ - and a word does not
	-- rotate with its item. They are stood up and re-anchored by the
	-- badge pass in turn_cell instead.
	return out
end

-- The six attachment overlays a cell can carry, nil-safely.
function G.att_list(ci, out)
	local function take(e) if e then out[#out + 1] = e end end
	take(ci.ico_scope) take(ci.shadow_ico_scope)
	take(ci.ico_sil)  take(ci.shadow_sil)
	take(ci.ico_gl)   take(ci.shadow_gl)
	return out
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE ART THAT RIDES A GUN'S OWN ICON
--
-- Two machineries paint on a weapon: the engine attachments
-- (Add_Attachements - a scope, a silencer, a launcher read off the
-- OBJECT) and the ltx icon layers (Add_Layers - "1icon_layer" on the
-- section, one numbered set per attachment, which is how GAMMA's
-- modular attachments and a magazine's cartridges are drawn). Both
-- are ART: they lie along the gun and turn with it. The functor
-- layers AFTER the ltx ones are the badges - tier text, the faction
-- icon - and a word does not rotate. This count is the border.
-- ============================================================
function G.art_layers(sec)
	local n = 0
	while SYS_GetParam(0, sec, (n + 1) .. "icon_layer") ~= nil do
		n = n + 1
	end
	return n
end

-- One overlay onto a TURNED base: the community fix's own derivation,
-- MIRRORED, because Sota's slots turn +90 and our cells turn -90 -
-- which is exactly why handing our overlays to its branch put "the
-- supressor in the wrong spot". Everything is derived from the base's
-- LIVE quad, in the base's child space. Clockwise, a point (px,py) of
-- the upright art lands at
--
--   ( w_b/2 - (py - h_b/2)*ratio , h_b/2 + (px - w_b/2) )
--
-- so the upright corner lands at (w_b/2 + h_b/2*ratio, h_b/2 - w_b/2),
-- the walk runs -y across and +x down, and the centre-aligned overlay
-- is anchored by its own turned half-quad (-h_s/2*ratio, +w_s/2).
-- What this file last wrote to each overlay, so the sweep can tell
-- "where I put it" from "where somebody moved it". Weak keys: the
-- statics are pooled per dialog and go when their dialog goes.
G.artm = G.artm or setmetatable({}, { __mode = "k" })

function G.place_turned(ele, base, x, y, w_s, h_s, k, ratio, dx, dy)
	local w_b, h_b = base:GetWidth(), base:GetHeight()
	local x_s = (w_b / 2 + (h_b / 2) * ratio) - y * ratio * k
	local y_s = (h_b / 2 - w_b / 2) + x * k
	local px = x_s - (h_s / 2) * ratio + (dx or 0)
	local py = y_s + w_s / 2 + (dy or 0)
	ele:SetWndPos(vector2():set(px, py))
	ele:SetWndSize(vector2():set(w_s, h_s))
	G.artm[ele] = { px, py }
end

-- ============================================================
-- DID SOMEBODY MOVE THE ART?
--
--   "i equip or unequip another gun. then the attachments on rotated
--    guns get moved and show incorectly untill i pick up the
--    affected guns and drop them one by one."
--
-- The window's Update re-runs the layer machinery on cells it never
-- re-Sets - an equip, a refresh, a scope change anywhere - and the
-- community fix, seeing the heading our dress left on each overlay,
-- re-places it by its +90 walk. The cell's heading and rectangle
-- both still read right, so the sweep's own test cannot see it. The
-- art can: a shown overlay standing anywhere but the spot this file
-- gave it - or one this file never placed at all, which is an
-- attachment added this very frame - answers yes.
-- ============================================================
function G.art_adrift(ci, sec)
	local function moved(ele)
		if not ele then return false end
		local shown = false
		pcall(function() shown = ele:IsShown() end)
		if not shown then return false end

		local want = G.artm[ele]
		if not want then return true end

		local p
		pcall(function() p = ele:GetWndPos() end)
		if not p then return false end
		return math.abs(p.x - want[1]) > 0.5
		    or math.abs(p.y - want[2]) > 0.5
	end

	local att = G.att_list(ci, {})
	for i = 1, #att do
		if moved(att[i]) then return true end
	end

	local n = G.art_layers(sec)
	for i = 1, n do
		if moved(ci.layer and ci.layer[i]) then return true end
	end

	return false
end

function G.turn_attachments(cc, ci, obj)
	local sec = ci.section
	if not (sec and ci.ico) then return end

	local gs = cc.grid_size or 41
	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	-- The attachment's own size, the way the layer machinery sizes it.
	local function dims(sec_l, lscale)
		local isc = tonumber(SYS_GetParam(2, sec_l, "inv_grid_scale", 1)) or 1
		if isc <= 0 then isc = 1 end
		local w = math.ceil((tonumber(SYS_GetParam(2, sec_l, "inv_grid_width", 1)) or 1) * gs / isc)
		local h = math.ceil((tonumber(SYS_GetParam(2, sec_l, "inv_grid_height", 1)) or 1) * gs / isc)
		return w * lscale, h * lscale
	end

	local function num(sec_m, key)
		return tonumber(SYS_GetParam(2, sec_m, key, 0)) or 0
	end

	local function turn_one(ele, sh, x, y, sec_l, lscale)
		if not (ele and type(sec_l) == "string") then return end
		local shown = false
		pcall(function() shown = ele:IsShown() end)
		if not shown then return end

		lscale = lscale or 1
		local k = lscale * (gs / 50)
		local w_s, h_s = dims(sec_l, lscale)

		ele:EnableHeading(true)
		ele:SetHeading(ROT_RAD)
		G.place_turned(ele, ci.ico, x, y, w_s, h_s, k, ratio)
		if sh then
			sh:EnableHeading(true)
			sh:SetHeading(ROT_RAD)
			G.place_turned(sh, ci.ico, x, y, w_s, h_s, k, ratio, 1, 2)
		end
	end

	-- The engine attachments: the same sections and keys the layer
	-- machinery itself reads to place them upright.
	local ui = rawget(_G, "utils_item")
	if ui and obj then
		pcall(function()
			local scope = (not ui.has_scope(sec)) and ui.get_attached_scope(obj)
			if scope then
				local sm
				pcall(function()
					sm = ui.get_param(sec, obj:id(), "scopes_sect", "string", false)
				end)
				if type(sm) ~= "string" then sm = sec end
				turn_one(ci.ico_scope, ci.shadow_ico_scope,
				         num(sm, "scope_x"), num(sm, "scope_y"), scope)
			end
		end)
		pcall(function()
			local sil = ui.get_attached_silencer(obj)
			if sil then
				turn_one(ci.ico_sil, ci.shadow_sil,
				         num(sec, "silencer_x"), num(sec, "silencer_y"), sil)
			end
		end)
		pcall(function()
			local gl = ui.get_attached_gl(obj)
			if gl then
				turn_one(ci.ico_gl, ci.shadow_gl,
				         num(sec, "grenade_launcher_x"),
				         num(sec, "grenade_launcher_y"), gl)
			end
		end)
	end

	-- The ltx art layers - GAMMA's modular attachments, a magazine's
	-- cartridges - each turned at its own offsets and scale.
	local n = G.art_layers(sec)
	for i = 1, n do
		local L = ci.layer and ci.layer[i]
		local sec_l = SYS_GetParam(0, sec, i .. "icon_layer")
		local ls = tonumber(SYS_GetParam(2, sec, i .. "icon_layer_scale", 1)) or 1
		if ls <= 0 then ls = 1 end
		pcall(function()
			turn_one(L, nil,
			         num(sec, i .. "icon_layer_x"),
			         num(sec, i .. "icon_layer_y"), sec_l, ls)
		end)
	end
end

function G.set_heads(ci, on)
	pcall(function()
		local els = G.cell_heads(ci, {})
		for i = 1, #els do
			els[i]:EnableHeading(on and true or false)
			els[i]:SetHeading(on and ROT_RAD or 0)
		end
	end)
end

function G.turn_cell(cc, ci, on)
	G.set_heads(ci, on)

	-- The attachment overlays' headings are OURS to manage - the
	-- layer machinery reads them and never writes them - and they are
	-- cleared before the redraw EITHER WAY: Set's own run then makes
	-- and places every overlay (a scope born this frame included)
	-- from the plain branch's known spot, and an undress never lets a
	-- pooled overlay be placed by the rotated branch against a base
	-- that is standing up. A dress re-turns them in its final pass
	-- below, once the turned quad they will be walked against exists.
	pcall(function()
		local att = G.att_list(ci, {})
		for i = 1, #att do
			att[i]:EnableHeading(false)
			att[i]:SetHeading(0)
		end
		-- The icon layers as well: the art among them is turned by
		-- the final pass, and a pooled layer left turned would be
		-- placed by the community fix's rotated branch - the wrong
		-- walk - the moment Set re-runs the machinery.
		for i = 1, #(ci.layer or {}) do
			local L = ci.layer[i]
			if L then
				L:EnableHeading(false)
				L:SetHeading(0)
			end
		end
	end)

	-- Redrawn through the window's own Set, so every layer follows
	-- the heading it now sees.
	local obj = ci.ID and level.object_by_id(ci.ID)
	if obj and ci.area then pcall(function() ci:Set(obj, ci.area) end) end

	if not on then
		-- Set re-positions the bar and NEVER re-sizes it, so a width
		-- this dressing clamped would outlive the rotation - and a
		-- recycled cell would hand it to the next item. The born
		-- width goes back by hand.
		pcall(function()
			if ci.bar and ci.gbw then
				ci.bar:SetWndSize(vector2():set(ci.gbw, ci.bar:GetHeight()))
			end
		end)
		return
	end

	-- The one thing Set writes for an upright cell that a turned one
	-- needs different: its rectangle.
	local w, h = G.size_of(ci.section)
	if not w then return end

	local gs = cc.grid_size or 0
	local gl = cc.grid_line or 0
	local wpx = w * gs + (w - 1) * gl
	local hpx = h * gs + (h - 1) * gl

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	local cw2, ch2 = hpx * ratio, wpx

	pcall(function()
		ci.cell:SetWndSize(vector2():set(cw2, ch2))
		if ci.hl then utils_xml.sync_element(ci.hl, ci.cell) end
	end)

	-- AND THE ICON ITSELF, by the engine's arithmetic and not the UI
	-- pack's. Sota's Add_Icon sizes a rotated icon (w, h*ratio); the
	-- engine's rotate_pt applies the aspect correction INSIDE the
	-- turn, so that ratio squashes the picture - "icon texture
	-- squished", in the player's words, and the boxes never had it
	-- because they size by the engine math. Raw cell pitch on both
	-- axes, centred in the turned cell by hand.
	local iw, ih = w * gs, h * gs

	-- ITS POSITION IS ITS CENTRE. These statics are centre-aligned -
	-- utils_xml.align_to_center writes base:GetWidth()/2 and nothing
	-- else, which only centres anything because the window treats its
	-- position as its middle. rotate3 placed them by top-left
	-- arithmetic, and the centre landed where the corner should have
	-- been: "to the left and up of the real location", by half the
	-- icon. The cell's half-size is the whole of the answer.
	local ix, iy = cw2 / 2, ch2 / 2

	pcall(function()
		ci.ico:SetWndSize(vector2():set(iw, ih))
		ci.ico:SetWndPos(vector2():set(ix, iy))
		if ci.shadow then
			ci.shadow:SetWndSize(vector2():set(iw, ih))
			ci.shadow:SetWndPos(vector2():set(ix + 1, iy + 1))
		end
	end)

	-- THE FURNITURE FOLLOWS THE CELL. The condition bar, the count,
	-- the part dots and the uses text are all placed by the window's
	-- own Update pass against the cell's dimensions - and that pass
	-- ran inside Set, BEFORE this function swapped the rectangle, so
	-- every overlay sat where the upright cell used to be. The stored
	-- W and H are overwritten with the turned truth (Sota's bar reads
	-- self.H, not the live rectangle) and the pass is run once more:
	-- everything that reads the cell's dimensions - which is
	-- everything - lands on the turned cell this time. Layers born
	-- inside Set get their heading before it, so the community fix
	-- places them rotated.
	ci.W, ci.H = cw2, ch2
	G.set_heads(ci, true)

	-- THE BAR CHAIN ONLY, not the whole Update. Re-running Update
	-- re-entered every mod's counter wrap too, and the QoL bundle's
	-- stacks-to-uses counter multiplied its sum on the extra passes -
	-- "3 uses ... shows 9". Everything that sat in the wrong place
	-- hangs off Add_ProgressBar in this install: the bar itself, and
	-- the part dots and uses/condition text that Item UI Improvements
	-- wraps into it. The counter chain keeps its one Set-time run;
	-- its position is clamped below.
	if obj then
		pcall(function()
			ci:Add_ProgressBar(nil, obj, ci.section, obj:clsid())
		end)

		-- ...AND THE COUNTER CHAIN, which holds the tier letters and
		-- short names. 2.0.4 left it out fearing it was what
		-- multiplied the uses - and the multiplication survived the
		-- removal, which acquits re-entry. Both chains recompute and
		-- overwrite; the diagnostic wrap installed with the UI hooks
		-- watches the counter's inputs to find the real multiplier.
		pcall(function()
			ci:Add_Counter(nil, obj, ci.section)
		end)

		-- THE ATTACHMENTS TURN THE WAY SOTA'S EQUIPMENT SLOTS TURN
		-- THEM. The chase was stood down once ("i guess its not that
		-- of an big issue rn") and reopened with the answer read out
		-- of the UI pack's own machinery: the community fix's
		-- Create_Layer branches on the OVERLAY's heading and derives
		-- every position from the BASE's LIVE quad. So the whole
		-- trick is order.
		--
		-- 2.0.8 set headings and re-ran inside Set, when the base
		-- still wore Set's own sizing; this function then rewrote the
		-- quad UNDER the placed overlays - "the scope and supressor
		-- are in wrong places". Now the machinery runs once more HERE,
		-- at the very end: every overlay already exists (Set made the
		-- newborns, headless, at known spots - see the clear at the
		-- top), each is handed the gun's own heading, and the rotated
		-- branch walks the quad this function just wrote - the art
		-- that is actually on screen.
		--
		-- An install without the community fix has no rotated branch;
		-- this run then repeats the plain placement, which is the
		-- stood-down behaviour, and nothing is worse than it was.
		-- Not the community fix's branch any more: its walk is
		-- written for Sota's +90 slots and put "the supressor in the
		-- wrong spot" on our -90 cells. Our own pass reads the same
		-- ltx and turns every piece of art - the engine attachments
		-- AND the section's icon layers - against the finished quad.
		pcall(function() G.turn_attachments(cc, ci, obj) end)
	end

	-- And the two stragglers. The bar is pinned to the turned cell's
	-- bottom at the turned width; the count, whose spot comes from a
	-- fixed xml offset, is clamped inside the cell.
	pcall(function()
		if ci.bar then
			local bh = ci.bar:GetHeight()

			-- THE BAR IS NEVER WIDENED - its width is its MEANING. A
			-- uses bar draws its uses as fixed-size segments, so
			-- stretching it to the turned cell's width turned three
			-- pips into nine ("shows 9 uses") - and since nothing in
			-- the window ever writes a bar's width after creation,
			-- the stretch outlived the rotation until a rebuild made
			-- a new cell. The born width is remembered on first
			-- touch, the dress only MOVES the bar (clamped when the
			-- turned cell is narrower than it), and the undress puts
			-- the born width back.
			ci.gbw = ci.gbw or ci.bar:GetWidth()
			local bw = ci.gbw
			if bw > cw2 then bw = cw2 end

			ci.bar:SetWndSize(vector2():set(bw, bh))
			ci.bar:SetWndPos(vector2():set(0, ch2 - bh))
		end
	end)
	pcall(function()
		if ci.cnt then
			local p = ci.cnt:GetWndPos()
			local x3, y3 = p.x, p.y
			local w3, h3 = ci.cnt:GetWidth(), ci.cnt:GetHeight()
			if x3 + w3 > cw2 then x3 = cw2 - w3 end
			if y3 + h3 > ch2 then y3 = ch2 - h3 end
			if x3 ~= p.x or y3 ~= p.y then
				ci.cnt:SetWndPos(vector2():set(x3, y3))
			end
		end
	end)

	-- THE BADGES KEEP THEIR CORNER. The tier text and the faction
	-- icon are icon layers; left to the layer machinery they sit
	-- where the unrotated art's corner used to be. They are stood
	-- up (older dressings turned them) and re-anchored to the TURNED
	-- cell's bottom-right, stacked upward in the order the natural
	-- layout stacked them - tier at the bottom, faction above, the
	-- same corner they hold on every upright item. Layer statics are
	-- centre-aligned children of the ico, whose own centre sits on
	-- the cell's centre, so cell coordinates convert by the
	-- half-quad offset.
	pcall(function()
		if not (ci.layer and #ci.layer > 0) then return end

		-- The first art_layers(sec) of these are ART - a scope drawn
		-- on the gun, a cartridge on the magazine - turned by the
		-- attachment pass above and none of the badge pass's
		-- business. The badges are the functor layers after them.
		local nart = G.art_layers(ci.section)

		local up = {}
		for i = 1, #ci.layer do
			local L = ci.layer[i]
			if L and i > nart then
				pcall(function()
					L:EnableHeading(false)
					L:SetHeading(0)
				end)
				local shown = false
				pcall(function() shown = L:IsShown() end)
				if shown then
					local p = L:GetWndPos()
					up[#up + 1] = { L = L, y = (p and p.y) or 0 }
				end
			end
		end
		if #up == 0 then return end
		table.sort(up, function(a, b) return a.y > b.y end)

		local lx = cw2 / 2 - iw / 2
		local ly = ch2 / 2 - ih / 2
		local bottom = ch2 - 1

		for i = 1, #up do
			local L = up[i].L
			local lw, lh = L:GetWidth(), L:GetHeight()
			L:SetWndPos(vector2():set(
				(cw2 - 1 - lw / 2) - lx,
				(bottom - lh / 2) - ly))
			bottom = bottom - lh - 1
		end
	end)
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT YOU HAVE JUST PICKED UP, LIT UNTIL YOU LOOK AT IT
--
--   "new items get a subtle highlight ... i pick up something
--    without thinking what it is and i cant find it in invnetory"
--
-- A soft amber ground behind every fresh cell. Behind, so the icon
-- covers it and only the transparent margins glow - subtle by
-- construction. The mark is spent the instant the cursor is over that
-- cell (cc.hover.idx, the window's own record of what it is pointing
-- at): touching is seeing, which is the clearing the player chose.
-- ============================================================
function G.draw_fresh(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st) then return end

	local pool = cc.g_glow
	local function hideall()
		if pool then
			for i = 1, #pool do
				G.put_st(pool[i], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false)
			end
		end
	end

	if not (G.enabled and G.new_glow) then return hideall() end

	-- TOUCHING IS SEEING. The cell under the cursor is looked at, so
	-- its ids stop being new - whether or not anything is even fresh,
	-- because a hover is a hover.
	local hi = cc.hover and cc.hover.idx
	if hi and cc.cell and cc.cell[hi] then
		local ci = cc.cell[hi]
		if ci.ID then G.fresh[ci.ID] = nil end
		if ci.childs then
			for cid in pairs(ci.childs) do G.fresh[cid] = nil end
		end
	end

	if not next(G.fresh) then return hideall() end
	if not pool then return end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end
	local cw = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line) * ratio
	local ch = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line)

	local n = 0
	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local a = ci and ci.area
		local new = false
		if a then
			if ci.ID and G.fresh[ci.ID] then new = true end
			if not new and ci.childs then
				for cid in pairs(ci.childs) do
					if G.fresh[cid] then new = true break end
				end
			end
		end

		if new and n < #pool then
			n = n + 1
			G.put_st(pool[n],
			         (a.x - 1) * cw, (a.y - 1) * ch,
			         a.w * cw, a.h * ch,
			         64, 255, 190, 70, true)
		end
	end

	for k = n + 1, #pool do
		G.put_st(pool[k], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false)
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT YOU CANNOT TAKE, MARKED WHERE YOU ARE LOOKING AT IT
--
--   "add also a red highlight under items that could not fit into
--    inventory in its current state (to show player if he needs to
--    make some room)."
--
-- Drawn like the fresh glow and for the same reasons: behind the icon,
-- from a pool made before any cell, through put_st so a frame where
-- nothing changed writes nothing.
-- ============================================================
function G.draw_nofit(cc)
	if not (cc and cc.st and cc.ID == "npc_bag") then return end

	local pool = cc.g_nofit
	local function hideall()
		if pool then
			for i = 1, #pool do
				G.put_st(pool[i], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false)
			end
		end
	end

	if not (G.enabled and G.mark_full) then return hideall() end
	if not pool then return end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end
	local cw = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line) * ratio
	local ch = (cc.grid_size + cc.grid_line)

	local n = 0
	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local a = ci and ci.area
		if a and ci.section and n < #pool then
			-- THE ID, NOT THE OBJECT. would_fit fetches it if it
			-- gets as far as needing one, which on a settled frame it
			-- does not.
			if not G.would_fit(nil, ci.section, ci.ID) then
				n = n + 1
				G.put_st(pool[n],
				         (a.x - 1) * cw, (a.y - 1) * ch,
				         a.w * cw, a.h * ch,
				         90, 210, 60, 60, true)
			end
		end
	end

	for k = n + 1, #pool do
		G.put_st(pool[k], 0, 0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, false)
	end
end

function G.dress_cells(cc)
	if not (next(G.rot) or G.rot_seen) then return end
	if not (cc and cc.cell) then return end

	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	for _, ci in pairs(cc.cell) do
		local ico = ci and ci.ID and ci.ico
		if ico then
			local want = (G.enabled and G.rot[ci.ID]) and true or false
			local ok, head = pcall(function() return ico:GetHeading() end)
			local turned = ok and ((head or 0) ~= 0)

			local stale = ok and (turned ~= want)

			-- A MOVED CELL KEEPS ITS TURN AND LOSES ITS RECTANGLE:
			-- drop_at re-Sets the cell on its new area, and Set
			-- writes the rectangle upright while leaving the heading
			-- alone. Judged by heading only, the sweep walked past a
			-- wide cell wearing a sideways picture - so a turned cell
			-- is judged by its rectangle too.
			if not stale and want and turned then
				local w2, h2 = G.size_of(ci.section)
				if w2 then
					local gs2 = cc.grid_size or 0
					local gl2 = cc.grid_line or 0
					local wantw = (h2 * gs2 + (h2 - 1) * gl2) * ratio
					local okw, cur = pcall(function()
						return ci.cell:GetWidth()
					end)
					if okw and math.abs((cur or 0) - wantw) > 0.5 then
						stale = true
					end
				end
			end

			if stale then
				G.turn_cell(cc, ci, want)
				if want then G.rot_seen = true end
			elseif want and turned then
				-- The cell is dressed and reads right - but the ART
				-- may have been re-placed behind our back by an
				-- Update this file never sees. Watched, not assumed;
				-- nothing is written when nothing moved.
				if G.art_adrift(ci, ci.section) then
					local aobj = ci.ID and level.object_by_id(ci.ID)
					if aobj then
						pcall(function()
							G.turn_attachments(cc, ci, aobj)
						end)
					end
				end
			end
		end
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE HELD ICON LIES THE WAY THE DROP WILL
--
--   "in normal inventory the icon when holding an item is always in
--    the 'normal' unrotated state"
--
-- The bag's drag picture is cc.hold.ico: one pooled static the
-- container sizes from the section's own numbers when a drag starts
-- and then syncs to the cursor. While the held item's effective lie -
-- stored XOR the R key - is sideways, it gets the heading and the
-- raw-pitch quad, the same arithmetic as a cell's icon; when the lie
-- stands up, it is undressed. Watched every frame because the R key
-- can turn mid-drag, and written only on a change of state.
-- ============================================================
function G.dress_hold(cc)
	local hico = cc.hold and cc.hold.ico
	if not hico then return end

	local want = false
	local w, h

	local idx = cc.hold.idx
	local ci = idx and cc.cell and cc.cell[idx]
	if G.enabled and ci and ci.ID and ci.section then
		w, h = G.size_of(ci.section)
		if w and w ~= h then
			local eff = G.rot[ci.ID] and true or false
			if G.hand_turned() then eff = not eff end
			want = eff
		end
	end

	-- NOT BEFORE THE CONTAINER HAS BUILT IT. The window sizes this
	-- static a tenth of a second into the drag; a dressing that ran
	-- earlier was overwritten by that late setup - the quad back at
	-- its ratio-carrying size with the heading still on, which is the
	-- squish again, on the cursor this time. Hidden means not built.
	local ok_s, shown = pcall(function() return hico:IsShown() end)
	if not (ok_s and shown) then return end

	local ok, head = pcall(function() return hico:GetHeading() end)
	if not ok then return end

	local gs = cc.grid_size or 0
	local ratio = utils_xml.screen_ratio()
	if not (ratio and ratio > 0) then ratio = 1 end

	local turned = (head or 0) ~= 0
	if turned == want then
		if not want then return end
		-- Turned and meant to be - and still re-checked against the
		-- raw pitch, because the setup has already proven it can
		-- write after us once.
		local okw, cw = pcall(function() return hico:GetWidth() end)
		if not (okw and w) then return end
		if math.abs(cw - w * gs) <= 0.5 then return end
	end

	pcall(function()
		hico:EnableHeading(want and true or false)
		hico:SetHeading(want and ROT_RAD or 0)
		if w then
			if want then
				-- Raw pitch on both axes - the engine turns the
				-- aspect correction in with the quad. Same rule as
				-- the cells.
				hico:SetWndSize(vector2():set(w * gs, h * gs))
			else
				-- Back to the size the container gave it.
				hico:SetWndSize(vector2():set(
					math.ceil(w * gs * ratio), h * gs))
			end
		end
	end)
end

-- ============================================================
-- WHAT DID NOT FIT
--
-- AddItem answers nil for two very different things: "there was no
-- room" and "I am already showing that one". Dropping on the second
-- would throw away a perfectly placed item every time the window
-- refreshed, so the id is checked against what the container ended up
-- holding rather than against the return value alone.
-- ============================================================
function G.on_add(self, obj, placed)
	if not (G.enabled and G.drop and obj) then return end

	-- ONLY THE REAL INVENTORY MAY PUT SOMETHING ON THE GROUND. A
	-- filtered view refusing an item means it did not fit among the
	-- half of your kit that view is showing, which is not a reason to
	-- throw it away.
	if not writes_layout(self.ID) then return end
	if placed ~= nil then return end

	local ok, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
	if not (ok and type(id) == "number") then return end

	-- It IS in the container - this was a duplicate add, not an
	-- overflow.
	if self.indx_id and self.indx_id[id] then return end

	-- The SECTION, not just a flag: the tick has to be able to ask how
	-- big the thing is before deciding it can never be carried.
	local oks, sec = pcall(function() return obj:section() end)
	G.queued[id] = (oks and sec) or true
	dbg("no room for %s", tostring(id))
end

-- ============================================================
-- AND THE SAME QUESTION WITH THE INVENTORY SHUT
--
-- Everything above only happens while you are looking at the window.
-- Loot picked up in the world would sit there unnoticed until you
-- opened it, and then forty things would hit the ground at once.
--
-- So the carried set is packed on a throttle, by the same rules, and
-- what is left over is dropped. This is the enforcement; the window is
-- only where you see it.
-- ============================================================
-- HOW LONG A THING STAYS EQUIPMENT AFTER IT LEAVES ITS SLOT. Long
-- enough for the longest borrow any mod makes of your hands - a ledge
-- climb is a second or two - and short enough that a gun you really did
-- put in a full bag reaches the ground while you are still standing
-- over the spot. The tick that reads this runs once a second.
local SLOT_GRACE = 10000

-- [item id] = the last time_global() it was seen worn, wielded or on
-- the belt. Ids, so it survives the object being handed around; pruned
-- in the same walk that reads it, so it cannot grow.
G.was_slot = G.was_slot or {}

function G.carried()
	local actor = db.actor
	if not actor then return {} end

	-- What is worn, wielded or on the belt is not in the bag and is
	-- never dropped. Slot 0..15 covers every equipment slot the engine
	-- has, including the outfit and the backpack granting the space.
	-- WHAT ARMOR MAG POUCHES IS HOLDING ON TO. A worn rig is an item in
	-- the bag rather than something in an engine slot, so nothing above
	-- would have skipped it - and dropping it is what made taking a
	-- backpack off take the rig off too. Its magazines go with it.
	local skip = {}
	local amp = rawget(_G, "zzz_armor_mag_pouches")
	if amp and amp.grid_keep then
		local ok, kept = pcall(amp.grid_keep)
		if ok and type(kept) == "table" then
			for id in pairs(kept) do skip[id] = true end
		end
	end

	local now = time_global()

	for i = 0, 15 do
		local ok, o = pcall(function() return actor:item_in_slot(i) end)
		if ok and o then
			local oki, id = pcall(function() return o:id() end)
			if oki then
				skip[id] = true
				G.was_slot[id] = now
			end
		end
	end
	pcall(function()
		actor:iterate_belt(function(_, o)
			if o then
				skip[o:id()] = true
				G.was_slot[o:id()] = now
			end
		end)
	end)

	-- ============================================================
	-- ...AND WHAT WAS IN A SLOT A MOMENT AGO
	--
	--   "when having full or almost full invneotry and trying to ledge
	--    grabbing. causes to drop an item and then crash"
	--
	-- The log, in this mod's own lines and then the engine's:
	--
	--   [GRID] ceiling wpn_ithacam37_stakeout_20x70 (kind w_shotgun) = 1
	--   [GRID] 71 of 72 cells, 42 carried, 1 over
	--   [GRID] no room: 1 item(s) put on the ground
	--   [GRID] 36998 (wpn_ithacam37_stakeout_20x70) left the actor
	--   ![player_hud::StopScriptAnim()] invalid script_anim_part 255
	--     demonized_ledge_grabbing.script(1165)
	--   CWeaponMagazined::GetBriefInfo   <- access violation, no Lua
	--
	-- Ledge Grabbing puts the weapon IN YOUR HANDS into the ruck for
	-- the length of the climb - db.actor:move_to_ruck(activeWpn) - and
	-- gives it back at the end. For that second or two the gun is not
	-- in a slot, so everything above counted it as cargo; on a full bag
	-- that is one item over capacity, and the one item over was the
	-- gun. Then the climb ended and put a weapon that was by then lying
	-- on the ground back into a slot, and the HUD read its ammo readout
	-- through a pointer to something that had left the actor. That is
	-- an engine crash with no Lua in the stack at all.
	--
	-- A THING BEING ANIMATED IS STILL EQUIPMENT. An item counts as worn
	-- for a few seconds after the last time it was actually seen in a
	-- slot, which covers any mod that borrows your hands and gives them
	-- back - and costs, in the only case it changes anything, a few
	-- seconds' delay before a gun you deliberately stuffed into a full
	-- bag hits the ground.
	-- ============================================================
	for id, when in pairs(G.was_slot) do
		if now - when <= SLOT_GRACE then
			skip[id] = true
		else
			G.was_slot[id] = nil
		end
	end

	local out = {}
	pcall(function()
		actor:iterate_inventory(function(_, o)
			if not o then return end

			local id = o:id()
			if skip[id] then return end

			local sec = o:section()

			-- A quest item on the ground is a broken quest. Never.
			if SYS_GetParam(1, sec, "quest_item") then return end

			-- ...NOR MONEY, WHICH IS ALREADY A NUMBER BY THE TIME
			-- ANYONE LOOKS. The game banks a note the moment it
			-- reaches you; this is about the frame or two before it
			-- does, in which a full bag would otherwise pick your
			-- roubles as the thing to put on the grass.
			if G.is_money(sec) then return end

			local w, h = G.size_for(id, sec)
			if not w then return end

			out[#out + 1] = { id = id, sec = sec, w = w, h = h }
		end)
	end)

	return out
end

-- First fit, biggest first - the same order the window packs in, so
-- what this decides and what you see agree. Pure, and given the list
-- rather than reading the actor, so it can be checked without a game.
--
-- NOT called G.pack. `G.pack` is the backpack's cell count, and a
-- function of that name is silently replaced by a number the first
-- time settings are read - which is a mod that works until somebody
-- opens the options menu. The tests found it; the name is the fix.
function G.place(items, z, panel_cols)
	-- ONE NUMBER PER CELL, NOT ONE STRING PER CELL. This ran once a
	-- second for the whole game and built a string - hashed it,
	-- interned it, gave it to the collector - for every cell of every
	-- test it made. A row is worth 256 columns and no grid is a
	-- quarter that wide, so row * 256 + column is the same key said in
	-- a way that costs nothing.
	local taken = {}
	local function free(r, c, w, h)
		for y = r, r + h - 1 do
			local row = y * 256
			for x = c, c + w - 1 do
				if taken[row + x] then return false end
			end
		end
		return true
	end
	local function take(r, c, w, h)
		for y = r, r + h - 1 do
			local row = y * 256
			for x = c, c + w - 1 do taken[row + x] = true end
		end
	end

	local placed, over, used = {}, {}, 0

	-- ============================================================
	-- A STACK IS ONE SQUARE, AND THE COUNTING HAS TO AGREE
	--
	-- Five bandages in one square is five items and one footprint. If
	-- this counted five footprints it would decide you were over
	-- capacity while the window was showing you plenty of room - and
	-- what follows from "over capacity" is things on the ground.
	--
	-- So the riders are set aside first: for a section with a ceiling of
	-- five, one item in every five needs a square and the rest are
	-- already placed by definition. Which of them is which does not
	-- matter to anybody - they are interchangeable, that is what makes
	-- them stackable.
	--
	-- ============================================================
	-- AND IT FOLDS WHAT THE GRID ACTUALLY FOLDS
	--
	-- This used to fold only sections with NO condition, because those
	-- were the only ones that stacked. Worn things stack now, and an
	-- estimator that still counts them one to a square decides you are
	-- over capacity while the window is showing you room - and what
	-- follows from "over capacity" is things on the ground.
	--
	-- FOOD IS STILL ONE TO A SQUARE HERE, because whether two tins go
	-- together depends on how rotten each one is and guessing which
	-- pairs off against which would be counting a grid nobody is
	-- looking at. That is the conservative direction: it can only ever
	-- say a load is bigger than it turns out to be.
	-- ============================================================
	local stacks = {}
	local loose_items = {}

	for i = 1, #items do
		local it = items[i]
		local lim = G.stack_limit(it.sec)
		local cond = G.spoils(it.sec)

		if lim > 1 and not cond then
			local t = stacks[it.sec]
			if not t then t = { lim = lim, n = 0 } stacks[it.sec] = t end
			t.n = t.n + 1

			-- One in every `lim` carries the square; the others ride.
			if ((t.n - 1) % lim) == 0 then
				loose_items[#loose_items + 1] = it
			else
				placed[#placed + 1] = it
			end
		else
			loose_items[#loose_items + 1] = it
		end
	end

	items = loose_items

	-- WHERE THINGS ALREADY ARE COMES FIRST, AND THIS IS THE WHOLE FIX.
	--
	-- This used to repack everything from scratch, biggest first, and
	-- drop whatever was left over. So taking your backpack off did not
	-- drop what was in the backpack - it dropped whichever items the
	-- repacking happened to leave until last, including things that had
	-- been sitting safely in your pockets the whole time. And because
	-- the window shows the remembered layout, gaps were plainly visible
	-- next to the message saying there was no room.
	--
	-- Honouring the layout means the answer matches the screen: what is
	-- placed stays placed, and only what genuinely cannot go anywhere
	-- is over.
	local known, loose = {}, {}
	for i = 1, #items do
		local it = items[i]
		if G.pos[it.id] then known[#known + 1] = it else loose[#loose + 1] = it end
	end

	table.sort(known, function(a, b)
		local pa, pb = G.pos[a.id], G.pos[b.id]
		if pa.y ~= pb.y then return pa.y < pb.y end
		if pa.x ~= pb.x then return pa.x < pb.x end
		return tostring(a.id) < tostring(b.id)
	end)

	for i = 1, #known do
		local it = known[i]
		local at = G.pos[it.id]
		if G.fits(z, at.y, at.x, it.w, it.h, panel_cols)
		   and free(at.y, at.x, it.w, it.h) then
			take(at.y, at.x, it.w, it.h)
			placed[#placed + 1] = it
			used = used + it.w * it.h
		else
			loose[#loose + 1] = it
		end
	end

	-- And the rest into the first gap that takes them, biggest first so
	-- a rifle is not shut out by a row of bandages.
	table.sort(loose, function(a, b)
		if a.w ~= b.w then return a.w > b.w end
		if a.h ~= b.h then return a.h > b.h end
		return tostring(a.id) < tostring(b.id)
	end)

	for i = 1, #loose do
		local it = loose[i]
		local got = false
		local sq = G.squares(z, it.w, it.h, panel_cols)

		for j = 1, #sq do
			if free(sq[j].r, sq[j].c, it.w, it.h) then
				take(sq[j].r, sq[j].c, it.w, it.h)
				placed[#placed + 1] = it
				used = used + it.w * it.h
				got = true
				break
			end
		end

		if not got then over[#over + 1] = it end
	end

	return placed, over, used
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE ONE DOOR THE GAME OFFERS
--
--   "block pickup/loot (item stays where it is)"
--
-- Cond_Move sends ActorMenu_on_item_before_move and obeys
-- flags.ret_value, and BOTH ways of looting go through it - the drag
-- and the take-all button, which calls Cond_Move once per item. So
-- this is a real refusal: the thing never leaves the body.
--
-- ONLY OUT OF THE OTHER SIDE'S LIST, and only while looting. Moving
-- something the other way is you emptying your own bag, which never
-- needs refusing, and a trade is a purchase with its own rules.
--
-- SAID ONCE, NOT FORTY TIMES. A take-all asks this per item and every
-- refusal would be its own line on screen.
-- ============================================================
G.said_full = G.said_full or 0
local SAY_MS = 2000

function G.on_before_move(flags, npc_id, obj, mode, bag)
	if not (G.enabled and G.full == "block") then return end
	if not (flags and obj and mode == "loot") then return end

	-- The bag ids are the engine's enum, not names. A build without it
	-- is a build this cannot judge, and not judging means allowing.
	local E = rawget(_G, "EDDListType")
	if not (E and E.iDeadBodyBag and bag == E.iDeadBodyBag) then return end

	local oks, sec = pcall(function() return obj:section() end)
	if not (oks and type(sec) == "string") then return end
	local oki, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
	id = oki and id or nil

	-- NOTHING THAT NEEDS NO ROOM IS EVER REFUSED, and no squares are
	-- spent on it either: money becomes a number and a filed item goes
	-- into a case. Asked here rather than trusted from would_fit,
	-- because that answer is memoised for a quarter of a second and
	-- this one decides whether the bag is charged for it.
	if G.needs_no_room(sec) then return end

	if G.would_fit(obj, sec, id) then
		-- Allowed - and those squares are spoken for from here on.
		G.fit_spend(sec, id)
		return
	end

	flags.ret_value = false

	local now = time_global()
	if now - G.said_full > SAY_MS then
		G.said_full = now
		log("no room for %s - left where it was", tostring(sec))
		pcall(function()
			actor_menu.set_msg(1,
				game.translate_string("st_grid_no_room_left"), 4)
		end)
	end
end

-- Which ids another mod says may not be put on the ground, however
-- little room there is. Optional in every direction: no Armor Mag
-- Pouches, no door, no answer, and the spill behaves as it always did.
--
-- G.told_kept is what stops this being said once a second for as long
-- as the bag is full; it is cleared the moment there is nothing left
-- being kept.
G.told_kept = G.told_kept or {}

function G.nodrop()
	local safe = {}

	local amp = rawget(_G, "zzz_armor_mag_pouches")
	if amp and amp.grid_nodrop then
		local ok, t = pcall(amp.grid_nodrop)
		if ok and type(t) == "table" then
			for id in pairs(t) do safe[id] = true end
		end
	end

	return safe
end

function G.spill(over)
	local actor = db.actor
	if not (actor and over and #over > 0) then return 0 end

	local n = 0
	for i = 1, #over do
		local obj = level.object_by_id(over[i].id)
		if obj then
			local ok = pcall(function() actor:drop_item(obj) end)
			if ok then
				n = n + 1
			else
				log("!could not drop %s", tostring(over[i].sec))
			end
		end
	end

	if n > 0 then
		log("no room: %s item(s) put on the ground", tostring(n))
		local ok_news = pcall(function()
			actor_menu.set_msg(1,
				game.translate_string("st_grid_no_room") .. " (" .. n .. ")", 4)
		end)
		if not ok_news then
			pcall(news_manager.send_tip, db.actor,
			      game.translate_string("st_grid_no_room"), nil, nil, 4000)
		end
	end

	return n
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE TICK
--
-- Once a second. The packing is a few dozen rectangles against a few
-- hundred cells - nothing beside what the inventory does every time it
-- refreshes - but it is not free either, and nothing about a carrying
-- limit needs to be answered sixty times a second.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- A NEW GAME HANDS YOU YOUR KIT IN A PILE
--
--   "i need to address the issue with starting a new game with this
--    mod. as when spawned the whole inventory gets dropped on the
--    ground cuz character does not spawns with armour and backpack
--    equipped"
--
-- Exactly so. The loadout puts a jacket and a pack IN YOUR BAG rather
-- than on your body, and until they are on you the grid is the eight
-- squares of your own pockets - so a full starting loadout is over
-- capacity from the first second, and the tick below did what it is
-- told to do with things that will not fit.
--
--   "can we make so the items are automaticly eqipped? like weapons as
--    those do not go into the invnetory but automaticly to the weapons
--    slots"
--
-- That is the right shape and the game already agrees with it: a
-- section declares which slot it belongs in, and the inventory's own
-- Equip does no more than read that number and call move_to_slot. So
-- this does the same thing, once, for the two slots that grant squares.
--
-- ONCE PER SAVE, and remembered, because a player who has deliberately
-- taken their coat off must not be dressed again on every load.
--
-- Slot numbers here are the window's: the section's own `slot`
-- parameter plus one. 7 is the outfit and 13 is the backpack, which is
-- how the rest of this file already asks about them.
-- ============================================================
local DRESS = { 7, 13 }

function G.dress()
	local actor = db.actor
	if not (actor and G.enabled) then return 0 end

	-- Only slots that are EMPTY. Anything already worn is the player's
	-- decision and is not touched.
	local want = {}
	for i = 1, #DRESS do
		local ok, worn = pcall(function() return actor:item_in_slot(DRESS[i]) end)
		if ok and not worn then want[DRESS[i]] = true end
	end
	if not next(want) then return 0 end

	-- The first thing in the bag that says it belongs in one of them.
	-- A second jacket is left where it is; this is about being dressed,
	-- not about choosing the best kit for you.
	local pick = {}
	pcall(function()
		actor:iterate_inventory(function(_, o)
			if not o then return end
			local oks, sec = pcall(function() return o:section() end)
			if not (oks and type(sec) == "string") then return end

			local slot = tonumber(SYS_GetParam(2, sec, "slot"))
			if not slot then return end
			slot = slot + 1

			if want[slot] and not pick[slot] then pick[slot] = o end
		end, actor)
	end)

	local n = 0
	for slot, obj in pairs(pick) do
		local ok = pcall(function() actor:move_to_slot(obj, slot) end)
		if ok then
			n = n + 1
			log("put on %s", tostring(obj:section()))
		else
			log("!could not put on %s", tostring(obj:section()))
		end
	end
	return n
end

local EVERY = 1000

-- How long after the world starts before anything may be put on the
-- ground. A load, a level change and a new game all have a moment where
-- the outfit is not readable yet, and an inventory measured in that
-- moment is a naked one.
local SETTLE = 5000


function G.on_update()
	if not G.enabled then return end
	if not db.actor then return end

	local now = time_global()
	if now - (G.last or 0) < EVERY then return end
	G.last = now

	-- ============================================================
	-- NOTHING GOES ON THE GROUND WHILE THE WORLD IS STILL ARRIVING
	--
	-- The clock starts the first time this runs. Until it has been going
	-- a few seconds nothing is dropped - and if this save has never been
	-- dressed, that is the moment the jacket and the pack go on.
	--
	-- The dressing gets its own tick: putting a backpack on changes how
	-- many squares there are, and the accounting below should be done
	-- against the answer AFTER it, not before.
	-- ============================================================
	if not G.began then G.began = now end
	if now - G.began < SETTLE then return end

	if not G.dressed then
		G.dressed = true
		local ok, n = pcall(G.dress)
		if ok and n and n > 0 then
			log("dressed you in what you started with (%s)", tostring(n))
			return
		end
	end

	local z = G.zones()
	if not z then return end

	local _, _, cells = G.capacity()
	local items = G.carried()
	local placed, over, used = G.place(items, z, G.panel_cols)

	-- AND HOW MANY REBUILDS THE IDLE CHECK SAVED SINCE THE LAST TICK.
	-- Folded into the line that was already printed once a second
	-- rather than said on its own: a performance fix that prints four
	-- lines a second is not one. The number IS the report - a corpse
	-- that used to cost seventy-nine rebuilds and now costs three is a
	-- sentence a player can send back.
	dbg("%s of %s cells, %s carried, %s over, %s rebuild(s) skipped",
	    tostring(used), tostring(cells), tostring(#items), tostring(#over),
	    tostring(G.idle))
	G.idle = 0

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE WINDOW'S REFUSALS, CONFIRMED BEFORE ANYTHING IS DROPPED
	--
	--   "when i move items in my inventory when the vendor/trade window
	--    is open and then i close it the item gets droped with 'not
	--    enough space' warning when there is plenty of space ofc as i
	--    was able to move it around even"
	--
	-- The log has it exactly:
	--
	--     [GRID] no square for wpn_ak74u_tac_ac10632 (5x2)
	--     [GRID] no room for 4173
	--     [GRID] no room: 1 item(s) put on the ground
	--     [GRID] 72 of 113 cells, 47 carried, 0 over
	--
	-- Forty-one free cells and NOTHING over. The refusal was real but
	-- it was TEMPORARY: the window fills the list itself, greedily, in
	-- sort order, BEFORE the layout is put back - and a five-wide rifle
	-- late in that order can easily find no run of five in a
	-- half-packed grid. The restore places it a moment later, correctly,
	-- and the queue drops it anyway.
	--
	-- So the queue is now a SUSPICION, not a verdict. An item is only
	-- put on the ground if the layout above agrees there is nowhere for
	-- it, or if there is no square anywhere in the grid its size could
	-- ever go - which is the one thing the window knows and the
	-- simulation does not.
	--
	-- It also has to still be yours. An item queued on its way OUT of
	-- your bag - sold, or dragged into the offer - is not carried any
	-- more, and dropping it was a second way to lose something.
	-- ============================================================
	local suspect = G.queued
	G.queued = {}

	-- It is kept as a NOTE now and nothing more. Everything the queue
	-- could tell us, the layout above says better and later:
	--
	--   * a thing that is yours and can go nowhere - too wide for any
	--     section, or in a grid that is genuinely full - is already in
	--     `over`, which is the list being spilled below;
	--   * and a thing that is no longer yours, because you sold it or
	--     dragged it into the offer, must not be put on the ground at
	--     all, however loudly the window refused it on the way past.
	--
	-- So there is nothing left for it to decide.
	if next(suspect) then
		local mine = {}
		for i = 1, #items do mine[items[i].id] = true end

		for id in pairs(suspect) do
			dbg("the window refused %s; %s", tostring(id),
			    mine[id] and "the layout has it" or "it is not yours any more")
		end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND WHAT ARRIVED WITHOUT THE PLAYER ASKING IS NOT SPILLED
	--
	--   "the mag that gets unloaded tries to go into invnetory and cant
	--    so gets dropped. thats is a big issue."
	--
	-- The whole of it, in this mod's own lines:
	--
	--   [AMP-DBG] room_in_pouch(37654) -> false
	--   [GRID] no square for mag_mp443_9x19_default (1x2)
	--   [GRID] 54 of 56 cells, 50 carried, 1 over
	--   [GRID] no room: 1 item(s) put on the ground
	--
	-- Two free cells, and a magazine that needs two ONE ABOVE THE
	-- OTHER. Repacking might have found room and might not - at 54 of
	-- 56 it would have to be perfect - and it would rearrange
	-- everything the player put where they put it. Losing the magazine
	-- is the certain harm. So it is simply not dropped.
	--
	-- The spill is for a load you chose: things picked up, or a
	-- backpack taken off. Armor Mag Pouches names the ids that arrived
	-- without you having any say - today, a magazine out of your own
	-- gun - and those stay in the inventory instead, unplaced, exactly
	-- as everything does with this switch off.
	--
	-- SAID ONCE PER ITEM. The tick runs every second and the state
	-- lasts until the player makes room; one line and one message, not
	-- one a second.
	-- ============================================================
	if G.drop and #over > 0 then
		local safe = G.nodrop()
		local down, kept = {}, 0

		for i = 1, #over do
			local it = over[i]
			if safe[it.id] then
				kept = kept + 1
				if not G.told_kept[it.id] then
					G.told_kept[it.id] = true
					log("no room for %s, but it came out of your gun - "
					    .. "keeping it", tostring(it.sec))
					pcall(function()
						actor_menu.set_msg(1,
						    game.translate_string("st_grid_kept"), 4)
					end)
				end
			else
				down[#down + 1] = it
			end
		end

		if #down > 0 then G.spill(down) end
		if kept == 0 then G.told_kept = {} end
	else
		G.told_kept = {}
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE WRAPS
--
-- At chunk scope, the way the parent mod does it: naming utils_ui here
-- is what makes Anomaly's lazy script loader load it, and a wrap
-- installed in on_game_start would be behind anything that wrapped at
-- load time.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- THE CEILING, WHERE THE WINDOW ASKS ABOUT STACKING
--
-- One question is asked before an item is given a square: is there
-- already a cell for one of these. It is asked by the window's own
-- packing AND by this mod's layout, which is why the ceiling goes here
-- and nowhere else - there is one answer and no second opinion to keep
-- in step with it.
--
-- This only ever NARROWS what the window allows. Nothing the base
-- refuses is let through.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- AND PUT BACK IF SOMETHING ELSE TAKES IT
--
-- FindSimilar is not a function this mod owns. Anomaly Magazines
-- replaces the whole of it in rax_stacking_control.script; the icon
-- framework ships its own; whichever chunk runs last wins, and Anomaly
-- loads a script the first time anything names it, so the order is not
-- something either mod decides. Wrapped at chunk scope AND again every
-- time the inventory is built, over whatever is there by then.
--
-- Comparing two FUNCTIONS is ordinary Lua and is not the engine-window
-- comparison this project bans - that is about UICellItem, which has no
-- equality operator at all.
-- ============================================================
function G.wrap_similar()
	local cls = utils_ui and utils_ui.UICellContainer
	if not cls then return end

	local now = cls.FindSimilar
	if type(now) ~= "function" then return end
	if G.similar_ours and now == G.similar_ours then return end

	local base = now

	local mine = function(self, obj, sec)
		local ci = base(self, obj, sec)
		if not ci then return ci end
		if not (G.enabled and G.stack and ours(self.ID)) then return ci end

		local s = sec
		if type(s) ~= "string" then
			local ok, got = pcall(function() return obj:section() end)
			s = ok and got or nil
		end
		if type(s) ~= "string" then return ci end

		local lim = G.stack_limit(s)
		if lim <= 1 then return false end
		if G.room_in(ci, lim) then return ci end

		-- The window asks this when something ARRIVES - a pickup, a
		-- refresh - so it is always a new arrival and may join a by-hand
		-- pile (to_hand = true). See G.next_stack.
		return G.next_stack(self, obj, s, lim, true)
	end

	G.similar_ours = mine
	cls.FindSimilar = mine
	log("the ceilings sit on top of the window's own stacking")
end

G.wrap_similar()

-- ============================================================
-- THE ONE HOOK THAT RUNS BEFORE ANY CELL EXISTS
--
-- InitControls is where a container makes self.st, which is the window
-- every cell is later parented to. Nothing is in the list yet, so a
-- static made here is behind every icon for the life of the container -
-- which is the only way to put a solid colour under them. See
-- G.make_fill.
--
-- AFTER THE BASE, because self.st does not exist until it has run.
-- ============================================================
local base_InitControls = utils_ui.UICellContainer.InitControls

if base_InitControls then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:InitControls(owner, prof, ele_base)
		local r = base_InitControls(self, owner, prof, ele_base)

		local ok, err = pcall(G.make_fill, self)
		if not ok then log("!ground: %s", tostring(err)) end

		-- THE LINES, BEFORE ANY CELL. They used to be made during the
		-- draw and therefore on top of every icon already in the list -
		-- invisible on a one-square item, a line straight across the
		-- middle of anything bigger. See G.make_edge.
		local oke, erre = pcall(G.make_edge, self)
		if not oke then log("!grid lines: %s", tostring(erre)) end

		local okg, errg = pcall(G.make_glow, self)
		if not okg then log("!glow ground: %s", tostring(errg)) end

		local okb, errb = pcall(G.make_boxhl, self)
		if not okb then log("!box wash: %s", tostring(errb)) end

		-- AND THE OTHER SIDE'S, which is the one list this mod draws in
		-- that is not its own.
		local okn, errn = pcall(G.make_nofit, self)
		if not okn then log("!no-room ground: %s", tostring(errn)) end

		return r
	end
end

local base_FindFreeCell = utils_ui.UICellContainer.FindFreeCell

if base_FindFreeCell then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:FindFreeCell(obj, sec)
		-- ...AND WHILE THE LAST-RESORT RECOVERY IS RUNNING. G.restore
		-- sets this only after it has established that it could place
		-- NOTHING, at which point a bounded search is a search that has
		-- already answered no to everything and an unbounded one is the
		-- player's inventory back. See the end of G.restore.
		if G.recovering or not (G.enabled and ours(self.ID)) then
			return base_FindFreeCell(self, obj, sec)
		end

		-- `dunno` is find_bounded saying it could not work out where
		-- anything goes - as opposed to saying there is no room, which
		-- is a real answer and the reason this grid is bounded. See the
		-- block at the top of that function: the difference between the
		-- two is the difference between a bad layout and no inventory.
		local ok, res, dunno = pcall(G.find_bounded, self, obj, sec)
		if ok then
			if not dunno then return res end

			if not G.told_unbounded then
				G.told_unbounded = true
				log("!no sections to search in - the window's own packer "
				    .. "is placing things this pass")
			end
			return base_FindFreeCell(self, obj, sec)
		end

		log("!bounded search: %s", tostring(res))
		return base_FindFreeCell(self, obj, sec)
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- EVERY FRAME, FOR THE TWO THINGS THAT CANNOT WAIT FOR A REBUILD
--
-- The borders, because a drop can re-flow the panel under them and
-- nothing announces it; and the drop highlight, which only exists
-- while the cursor is moving. Both write through G.put_st, so a frame
-- where nothing changed costs a handful of table compares.
-- ============================================================
local base_Update = utils_ui.UICellContainer.Update

if base_Update then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:Update(item_info, no_info, hide)
		local res = base_Update(self, item_info, no_info, hide)

		-- NOT gated on G.enabled here. Both of these have something to
		-- PUT AWAY when the mod is switched off in the menu, and a
		-- guard out here would leave whatever was last drawn on the
		-- screen for the rest of the session.
		-- THE OTHER SIDE'S LIST, and the only thing drawn in it: which
		-- of what you are looking at you have no room for. Outside the
		-- `ours` block because it is not ours - and unconditional for
		-- the same reason the block below is, so switching the mark off
		-- puts away what was last drawn.
		-- THE MENU, ASKED FOR AGAIN AFTER A ROW WAS CLICKED. Cleared
		-- BEFORE it is acted on: a throw in here must not leave a note
		-- that asks for the same menu on every frame for the rest of
		-- the session.
		if G.bal_reopen then
			local want = G.bal_reopen
			G.bal_reopen = nil
			pcall(function()
				local g = ui_inventory and ui_inventory.GUI
				local o = want.id and level.object_by_id(want.id)
				if g and o and g.InitProperties then
					g:InitProperties(o, want.bag)
				end
			end)
		end

		-- DEBUG BALANCING, in EVERY list rather than only ours: the
		-- question "what does this mod think this is" is asked about
		-- things in a corpse and on a trader's shelf at least as often
		-- as about things you already own.
		if G.balance then
			pcall(G.draw_balance, self)
		end

		if self.ID == "npc_bag" then
			pcall(G.draw_nofit, self)
		end

		if ours(self.ID) then
			-- ============================================================
			-- AND THE FRAME A DEFERRED FILL FINISHES ON
			--
			-- G.restore stands aside while another mod is filling this
			-- container - see the note there. That mod announces it is
			-- done by clearing `deferred`, and nothing else announces
			-- it: there is no callback and no rebuild afterwards. So
			-- the end of it is watched for here, once, and the layout
			-- is put back on the frame it becomes possible.
			--
			-- Without this the items arrive in the WINDOW's order
			-- instead of the squares the player left them in, which is
			-- a quieter bug than a blank panel and a worse one to live
			-- with.
			-- ============================================================
			if G.defer_wait and G.defer_wait[self.ID]
			   and not self.deferred then
				G.defer_wait[self.ID] = nil
				pcall(G.relayout, self)
			end

			local t0 = G.clock_in()

			pcall(G.claim, self)
			pcall(G.draw_bounds, self)
			pcall(G.draw_hover, self)
			pcall(G.draw_fresh, self)
			pcall(G.dress_cells, self)
			pcall(G.dress_hold, self)

			G.clock_out(t0)

			-- AND THE FRAMES AFTER A TAP. This used to hang off the
			-- drag's own per-frame hook, which stops running the moment
			-- the hand is empty - and the tap empties it now. The list
			-- can still slip there, so the watch stands here instead,
			-- where it runs held or not. See G.tap_settled for why the
			-- countdown is in mouse movements.
			if G.enabled and G.after_tap
			   and G.after_tap.bag == self.ID then
				pcall(G.tap_settled, self)
			end
		end

		return res
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE DROP THE WINDOW NEVER MENTIONS TO ANYONE
--
--   "item cannot be nudged if the droped on the part of the item
--    itself"
--
-- Right, and it is not this mod refusing it - the mod is never asked.
-- utils_ui.script, UICellContainer:On_Drag:
--
--     if (not idx) and self.hold.idx and (self.hold.idx ~= self.hover.idx) then
--         self:Callback( "On_CC_DragDrop", self.ID, self.hold.idx )
--     end
--
-- Letting go over the cell the item is ALREADY IN is not reported at
-- all: as far as the window is concerned you picked it up and put it
-- back, and there is nothing to tell anybody about. For a list that is
-- true. For a grid it is not - a wide item still covers its own cell
-- from one square away, so that silence is exactly the nudge.
--
-- So this listens for the drop the window drops. Everything else about
-- the drag is left alone, including the base's own call for every
-- other kind of drop.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- WHO MOVED THE LIST
--
--   "i dont holding the item near the edge to scroll. i know thius
--    feature and it works. the inventory scrolls by its own even if the
--    item drop keybind (shift) is clicked when i hold it in the MIDDLE
--    of the ivnentory."
--
-- Right, and that rules out the edge strips - which I had just spent a
-- release on. It also rules out everything I have looked at so far: the
-- tap's own tracing says the bar is in the same place before and after,
-- eight times out of eight, and the rebuild says the same.
--
-- So something moves it that is not the tap and not the rebuild, and
-- guessing which has now cost three releases. There is exactly one
-- function in the window that can move that bar, and it is this one.
-- Wrapped, read-only, the base always called: when the position really
-- changes while a list of ours is on screen, the log gets the numbers
-- AND THE CALL STACK - which names whoever asked for it.
--
-- Bounded to a dozen lines a session, because a call stack per frame
-- would be its own bug.
-- ============================================================
local base_SetPos = utils_ui.UICellContainer.Scroll_SetPos

if base_SetPos then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:Scroll_SetPos(Y, force)
		-- FORTY, because a runaway is a burst - nine in a row in the
		-- log that started this - and twelve is spent before the
		-- interesting part of it.
		local watch = G.debug and G.enabled
		              and (G.said_scroll or 0) < 40 and ours(self.ID)

		local was
		if watch then
			pcall(function() was = self.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)
		end

		local r = base_SetPos(self, Y, force)
		if not watch then return r end

		local now
		pcall(function() now = self.scroll:GetCurrentScrollPos() end)

		if was ~= now then
			G.said_scroll = (G.said_scroll or 0) + 1

			-- ============================================================
			-- WHICH GUARD WAS OFF, WHICH IS THE ACTUAL QUESTION
			--
			-- The stack this used to print named nobody - two frames of
			-- this wrapper and a compressed "[1]" where the caller
			-- should be. Four releases were spent reasoning around that.
			--
			-- There are three things that can move this bar and each is
			-- held off by a different flag, so the flags AT THE MOMENT
			-- OF THE MOVE say which one it was:
			--
			--   held nothing, pause false   the wheel amplifier in
			--                               On_Scroll, which reads any
			--                               disagreement as the wheel
			--   something held              Scroll_DragDrop_Ctrl, the
			--                               window running the list
			--                               while the cursor is at an
			--                               edge
			--   ignore true                 ours, and it says so
			--
			-- Read only. Nothing here is written to.
			-- ============================================================
			local hold, own, pause, dis, disdd, ign, pow
			pcall(function()
				hold  = self.hold and self.hold.idx
				own   = self.owner and self.owner.item_in_hold
				pause = self.scroll_pause
				dis   = self.disable_scroll
				disdd = self.disable_scroll_dragdrop
				ign   = self.ignore_scroll
				pow   = self.scolling_power
			end)

			local who
			pcall(function() who = debug.traceback("", 2) end)

			dbg("the list moved %s -> %s | held=%s in_hold=%s pause=%s "
			    .. "dis=%s disdd=%s ignore=%s power=%s\n%s",
			    tostring(was), tostring(now), tostring(hold), tostring(own),
			    tostring(pause), tostring(dis), tostring(disdd),
			    tostring(ign), tostring(pow), tostring(who))
		end

		return r
	end
end

local base_On_Drag = utils_ui.UICellContainer.On_Drag

if base_On_Drag then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:On_Drag(idx, tg, set)
		-- ============================================================
		-- THE FRAME THE JUMP HAPPENS ON
		--
		--   "if i pess the button to drop one and then move my mouse
		--    while still holding left click, even a one nanometer the
		--    ivnentory jumps UP"
		--
		-- On_Drag with `set` false IS that mouse movement - it is the
		-- window syncing the dragged icon to the cursor, and it runs on
		-- every frame the mouse moves. So this is the frame the player
		-- is describing, and until now nothing of mine ran on it.
		--
		-- EVERYTHING I HAVE MEASURED WAS MEASURED SYNCHRONOUSLY: the
		-- tap reads the scroll back the instant it writes it and gets
		-- its own number, which is why four releases of tracing found
		-- nothing. The engine settles its view on a later layout pass,
		-- and this is the pass.
		--
		-- So: for a few frames after a tap, if the list is no longer
		-- where the tap left it, say so and put it back. The saying so
		-- is the measurement I could not take; the putting back is the
		-- repair, on the frame the symptom appears.
		-- ============================================================
		if (not set) and G.enabled and G.after_tap
		   and G.after_tap.bag == self.ID then
			pcall(G.tap_settled, self)
		end

		-- BEFORE the base, which is where hold.idx stops existing.
		if set and not idx and G.enabled and ours(self.ID) then
			-- The drag is over; nothing left to hold in place.
			G.after_tap = nil

			local held  = self.hold  and self.hold.idx
			local under = self.hover and self.hover.idx

			if held and under and held == under then
				local ok, err = pcall(G.drop_at, self.owner, self.ID, held)
				if not ok then log("!nudge: %s", tostring(err)) end
			end
		end

		return base_On_Drag(self, idx, tg, set)
	end
end

local base_AddItem = utils_ui.UICellContainer.AddItem

if base_AddItem then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:AddItem(obj, sec, info)
		local res = base_AddItem(self, obj, sec, info)
		pcall(G.on_add, self, obj, res)

		-- AND NOTHING IS REMEMBERED HERE, WHICH COST A RELEASE.
		--
		-- This used to record wherever the window had just put the
		-- item. But the window's own rebuild packs everything from
		-- scratch BEFORE the layout is put back - so every open
		-- overwrote the remembered squares with the fresh packing, and
		-- then faithfully restored that. A drag worked and was undone
		-- by the next rebuild, which is exactly what was reported.
		--
		-- The layout is decided in one place - G.restore - and read
		-- back off the screen in another - G.capture, which runs
		-- BEFORE the base tears anything down. Neither of them is here.
		return res
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE REBUILD
--
-- Reinit is the window throwing its layout away: Reset, then every
-- item added again in sort order. It runs on opening the inventory, on
-- looting, on selling, on picking anything up. Left alone it is the
-- re-sort.
--
-- Read the old layout off the cells first, then put them back where
-- they were.
-- ============================================================
local base_Reinit = utils_ui.UICellContainer.Reinit

if base_Reinit then
	function utils_ui.UICellContainer:Reinit(t, tf)
		if not (G.enabled and ours(self.ID) and t) then
			return base_Reinit(self, t, tf)
		end

		pcall(G.capture, self)

		local ok, done = pcall(G.restore, self, t)
		if not (ok and done) then
			if not ok then log("!restore: %s", tostring(done)) end
			return base_Reinit(self, t, tf)
		end

		pcall(G.draw_bounds, self)
	end
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE DRAG, WHICH LIVES ON THE MENU AND NOT THE CONTAINER
--
-- ui_inventory.UIInventory is not built when scripts load, so this one
-- wrap waits for the game to be running. Everything else is at chunk
-- scope where it belongs.
-- ============================================================
-- ============================================================
-- WHERE THE LAYOUT IS ACTUALLY LOST
--
-- 0.2 wrapped UICellContainer:Reinit and nothing happened, which is
-- what "items resort on reopen but can be moved around just dont
-- remember where they were" was: the drag worked, and every rebuild
-- threw the answer away.
--
-- The reason is that the inventory's own open and refresh DO NOT go
-- through Reinit for the actor's bag. UIInventory fills it itself, per
-- mode - inventory, looting, trading - six functions in all. Reinit is
-- only one of the ways in.
--
-- So each of those is wrapped, and each does the same two things: read
-- the layout off the cells BEFORE the base tears them down, and lay it
-- out again AFTER the base has filled the container. Laying it out
-- again from what the container ended up holding means the base's own
-- work - parsing the inventory, the picker, whatever else - is left
-- entirely alone.
--
-- ui_inventory.UIInventory is not built when scripts load, so this
-- waits for the game to be running.
-- ============================================================
-- Every path the window has for filling a list. RMode is the
-- workbench, which also shows actor_bag; the install loop skips any
-- name this build of the game does not have, so listing it costs
-- nothing and forgetting it would cost a re-sort.
local MODES = {
	"IMode_ResetInventories", "IMode_RefreshInventories",
	"LMode_ResetInventories", "LMode_RefreshInventories",
	"TMode_ResetInventories", "TMode_RefreshInventories",
	"RMode_ResetInventories", "RMode_RefreshInventories",
}

function G.install_ui()
	-- Whoever loaded last owns FindSimilar; take it back now that every
	-- mod's chunk has run.
	pcall(G.wrap_similar)

	local ui = rawget(_G, "ui_inventory")
	ui = ui and ui.UIInventory
	if not ui then
		log("no inventory menu - the layout cannot be kept")
		return
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE THREE METHODS THE MENU CALLS BY NAME
	--
	-- Each unwraps the window's own calling convention and hands a
	-- section to a function that can be asked without a game. Read out
	-- of InitProperties rather than assumed - a name is given an ID and
	-- a precondition is given an OBJECT, which is not a symmetry
	-- anybody would guess.
	-- ============================================================
	function ui.GRID_BalName(self, id, bag, _, i)
		local ok, s = pcall(function()
			return G.bal_row_name(i, G.bal_sec(id))
		end)
		return (ok and type(s) == "string") and s or ""
	end

	function ui.GRID_BalCond(self, obj, bag, _, i)
		local ok, yes = pcall(function()
			local sec
			pcall(function() sec = obj and obj:section() end)
			return G.bal_row_show(i, sec)
		end)
		return (ok and yes) and true or false
	end

	function ui.GRID_BalAct(self, id, bag, _, i)
		local sec = G.bal_sec(id)
		if not sec then return end
		pcall(G.bal_row_do, i, sec)

		-- ...AND ASK FOR THE MENU BACK. Not here: the box calls this
		-- and then hides itself on the very next line, so a Reset from
		-- inside would be shown and hidden in the same breath. Left as
		-- a note for the frame after, which is what makes clicking +5
		-- four times in a row possible.
		G.bal_reopen = { id = id, bag = bag }
	end

	-- The rows themselves go in when a menu is actually built, because
	-- the table they go into belongs to the window object and that is
	-- made after this runs.
	if not G.props_base and type(ui.InitProperties) == "function" then
		G.props_base = ui.InitProperties
		ui.InitProperties = function(self, obj, bag)
			if G.enabled and G.balance then
				pcall(G.bal_rows_into, self)
			end
			return G.props_base(self, obj, bag)
		end
		log("the item menu will carry the stack rows")
	end

	if not G.dd_base and type(ui.On_CC_DragDrop) == "function" then
		G.dd_base = ui.On_CC_DragDrop
		ui.On_CC_DragDrop = function(self, bag_from, idx_from)
			local ok, mine = pcall(G.drop_at, self, bag_from, idx_from)
			if ok and mine then
				-- The little shuffle of a thing settling into the
				-- square you pointed at. Through the sibling's voice
				-- box - one switch, one throttle, one volume knob -
				-- and silence when it is not installed.
				pcall(function()
					zzz_armor_mag_pouches.grid_sfx("shuffle")
				end)
				return
			end
			if not ok then log("!drag: %s", tostring(mine)) end

			-- Not ours to move, but it may be ours to CATCH: see
			-- G.aim_incoming. Written before the base, because the base
			-- is what does the transfer and the packer reads it.
			local ok_a, err = pcall(G.aim_incoming, self, bag_from, idx_from)
			if not ok_a then log("!incoming: %s", tostring(err)) end

			return G.dd_base(self, bag_from, idx_from)
		end
		log("items can be moved by hand")
	end

	-- The window's own keyboard, for the tap that takes one off a stack
	-- while it is held. Wrapped rather than replaced, and the base is
	-- called whatever this does with the key, so nothing else that
	-- listens for it loses its press.
	if not G.key_base and type(ui.OnKeyboard) == "function" then
		G.key_base = ui.OnKeyboard
		ui.OnKeyboard = function(self, dik, action)
			-- Handled here means handled: see G.on_key. Anything else
			-- goes to the base untouched, including this key when
			-- nothing is being dragged.
			local ok, eaten = pcall(G.on_key, self, dik, action)
			if ok and eaten then return true end
			return G.key_base(self, dik, action)
		end
		log("one can be tapped off a stack while it is held")
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- AND WHEN THE WINDOW ITSELF BINS SOMETHING, IT SAYS SO
	--
	-- 63 things "left the actor" in the last log and 60 of them were the
	-- game shutting down. Of the three that were not, I still cannot
	-- tell a thing thrown on the ground from a thing drunk, eaten or
	-- loaded into a magazine - `actor_on_item_drop` is the only hook
	-- and it says an item went, not why.
	--
	-- The window has exactly one route to the ground, Action_Drop, and
	-- it takes it when a drag ends over its bin. So that is wrapped -
	-- read-only, the base always runs - and the next log tells the two
	-- apart: a "left the actor" with this line above it was binned, and
	-- one without it was used or given away.
	-- ============================================================
	if not G.bin_base and type(ui.Action_Drop) == "function" then
		G.bin_base = ui.Action_Drop
		ui.Action_Drop = function(self, obj, bag)
			pcall(function()
				local sec
				pcall(function() sec = obj:section() end)
				dbg("the window is putting %s on the ground, from %s",
				    tostring(sec), tostring(bag))
			end)
			return G.bin_base(self, obj, bag)
		end
	end

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE COUNTER UNDER WATCH
	--
	--   "the uses indicator is still bugged and multiplies by 3"
	--
	-- No suite can see which of the counter's inputs lies in the
	-- running game, and two theories have already died. So the game
	-- is asked directly, the way the reserve was in 1.98.2: a
	-- read-only wrap, debug-gated through dbg's own switch, that
	-- logs what Add_Counter was GIVEN for multiuse items - the cell,
	-- the object, every child id - and the text it wrote. The next
	-- log with a tripled bottle in it names the ghosts.
	-- ============================================================
	if not G.cnt_base and rawget(_G, "utils_ui")
	   and utils_ui.UICellItem
	   and type(utils_ui.UICellItem.Add_Counter) == "function" then
		G.cnt_base = utils_ui.UICellItem.Add_Counter
		utils_ui.UICellItem.Add_Counter = function(cself, xml, obj, sec)
			G.cnt_base(cself, xml, obj, sec)
			pcall(function()
				if not (obj and sec and IsItem("multiuse", sec)) then
					return
				end
				local ids = {}
				for id in pairs(cself.childs or {}) do
					ids[#ids + 1] = tostring(id)
				end
				local txt
				pcall(function()
					txt = cself.cnt and cself.cnt:IsShown()
					      and cself.cnt:TextControl():GetText() or "-"
				end)
				local uses
				pcall(function() uses = obj:get_remaining_uses() end)
				dbg("cnt: %s cell=%s id=%s uses=%s childs=[%s] text=%s",
				    tostring(sec), tostring(cself.indx),
				    tostring(cself.ID), tostring(uses),
				    table.concat(ids, ","), tostring(txt))
			end)
		end
		log("the uses counter is under watch")
	end

	G.mode_base = G.mode_base or {}
	local n = 0

	for i = 1, #MODES do
		local name = MODES[i]
		if type(ui[name]) == "function" and not G.mode_base[name] then
			G.mode_base[name] = ui[name]

			-- ============================================================
			-- A REFRESH THAT CHANGED NOTHING IS NOT WORTH A REBUILD
			--
			--   "the fps drop for him when looting bodies but stay
			--    fine in stashes and vendors"
			--
			-- Measured, from that player's log: a corpse open and
			-- nobody touching anything drove 79 full relayouts in 18
			-- seconds - 4.4 a second, against 0.4 at a vendor - and
			-- every one came out identical.
			--
			-- The window's own refresh is INCREMENTAL: it adds what is
			-- missing and removes what has gone, and with nothing to
			-- do it does nothing at all. All the work was this mod's,
			-- laying out a list that had not changed.
			--
			-- ONLY THE REFRESH MODES. The Reset ones call Reinit,
			-- which tears the container down and repacks it - the same
			-- ids can come back in completely different squares, and
			-- skipping there would leave the window's packing on
			-- screen instead of the player's layout.
			--
			-- THE CAPTURE STILL RUNS, for every mode. It is the walk
			-- over the cells; the restore is the cc:Reset() and the
			-- forty-odd packer searches, and that is what this saves.
			-- ============================================================
			local watch = string.find(name, "Refresh", 1, true) and true or false

			ui[name] = function(self, ...)
				-- EVERY list of ours, not one. The trade window fills
				-- actor_trade_bag and leaves actor_bag hidden, so a
				-- wrap that only knew one name did nothing at a
				-- trader.
				local mine = G.our_ccs(self)
				if G.enabled then
					for i = 1, #mine do pcall(G.capture, mine[i]) end
				end

				-- BY NAME, not by position: our_ccs is asked again
				-- after the base and a list that appeared or went away
				-- in between would shift every index under us.
				if watch and G.enabled then
					for i = 1, #mine do
						local cc = mine[i]
						G.was[cc.ID] = G.was[cc.ID] or {}
						G.nwas[cc.ID] = G.id_set(cc, G.was[cc.ID])
					end
				end

				local r = G.mode_base[name](self, ...)

				mine = G.our_ccs(self)
				if G.enabled then
					for i = 1, #mine do
						local cc = mine[i]

						if watch and G.same_ids(cc, G.was[cc.ID],
						                        G.nwas[cc.ID]) then
							G.idle = G.idle + 1
						else
							pcall(G.relayout, cc)
						end
					end
				end

				-- The answer written down is only good for the call it
				-- was taken in: anything else that changes the list
				-- between now and the next refresh - a drag, a drop,
				-- an item picked up in the world - must not be able to
				-- match against it.
				if watch and G.enabled then
					for i = 1, #mine do G.nwas[mine[i].ID] = nil end
				end

				return r
			end
			n = n + 1
		end
	end

	log("the layout is kept across %s of the window's own rebuilds", tostring(n))
end

-- ============================================================
-- ACROSS A SAVE
--
-- A layout that is forgotten when you quit is not a layout. Stored
-- flat - id:x:y:w:h, comma separated - because m_data is written for
-- every save and a table per item is a great deal of it.
-- ============================================================
function save_state(m_data)
	local out = {}
	for id, p in pairs(G.pos) do
		out[#out + 1] = string.format("%s:%s:%s:%s:%s:%s:%s", id, p.x, p.y,
		                              p.w or 1, p.h or 1,
		                              G.byhand[id] and 1 or 0,
		                              G.rot[id] and 1 or 0)
	end
	m_data.grid_pos = (#out > 0) and table.concat(out, ",") or nil

	-- ============================================================
	-- THE SQUARES ARE MEANINGLESS WITHOUT THIS, and it has to be the
	-- RIGHT one of two numbers that usually agree.
	--
	--   G.head_was     the frame the squares are IN. Only reflow writes
	--                  it, and only as it shifts them into that frame,
	--                  so it is true by construction.
	--   G.head_known   the most recent measurement from anywhere.
	--                  G.zones asks G.head_rows every time something
	--                  wants to know where a section starts, and none of
	--                  those callers moves a square.
	--
	-- They drift the moment the block changes height without a rebuild:
	--
	--     after a rebuild:          head_was=5  head_known=5  square at 14
	--     block grew, zones asked:  head_was=5  head_known=8  square at 14
	--
	-- This used to write head_known, which was harmless while the number
	-- was only a hint about where sections go. It stopped being harmless
	-- when reflow started SHIFTING BY IT: a save claiming eight where
	-- the squares are in five puts the whole arrangement three rows out
	-- on the way back in - inside the section, so nothing is refused and
	-- nothing is repacked, it is simply not where it was left.
	--
	-- head_was is set on the first rebuild of any session, before a
	-- single square is written, because G.restore calls reflow before it
	-- places anything. head_known is the fallback for a save from before
	-- this, where it is either nil or the frame anyway.
	-- ============================================================
	local frame = G.head_was
	if type(frame) ~= "number" or frame <= 0 then frame = G.head_known end
	m_data.grid_head = (type(frame) == "number" and frame > 0)
	                   and frame or nil

	-- Whether this save has been dressed. Without it a player who has
	-- taken their coat off on purpose is put back into it every load.
	m_data.grid_dressed = G.dressed and true or nil

	-- ============================================================
	-- WHAT YOU CHANGED IN THE MENU
	--
	-- One string, `s|section|number` and `k|kind|number` joined by
	-- commas. Neither a section name nor a kind can contain a comma or
	-- a bar, so nothing has to be escaped and a line that does not
	-- match the pattern on the way back in is simply dropped.
	-- ============================================================
	local ov = {}
	for k, v in pairs(G.stk_over.sec)  do ov[#ov + 1] = "s|" .. k .. "|" .. v end
	for k, v in pairs(G.stk_over.kind) do ov[#ov + 1] = "k|" .. k .. "|" .. v end
	table.sort(ov)
	m_data.grid_over  = (#ov > 0) and table.concat(ov, ",") or nil
	m_data.grid_scope = (G.bal_scope == "kind") and "kind" or nil
end

function load_state(m_data)
	G.pos = {}
	G.byhand = {}
	G.rot = {}

	-- WHAT YOU CHANGED IN THE MENU, back out of the save. Replaced
	-- rather than merged: a load is a different world, and carrying one
	-- save's balancing into another is the kind of thing you would
	-- never think to look for.
	G.stk_over = { sec = {}, kind = {} }
	G.bal_scope = (m_data and m_data.grid_scope == "kind") and "kind" or "sec"

	local ovs = m_data and m_data.grid_over
	if type(ovs) == "string" then
		for one in string.gmatch(ovs, "[^,]+") do
			local w, key, val = string.match(one, "^([sk])|([^|]+)|(%d+)$")
			val = tonumber(val)
			if w and key and val and val > 0 then
				G.stk_over[(w == "k") and "kind" or "sec"][key] =
					clampi(math.floor(val), 1, 999)
			end
		end
	end
	G.stk_memo, G.stk_why = {}, {}

	-- A save with no note has never been dressed - which is a new game,
	-- or this mod arriving in an existing one. Both want the same thing.
	G.dressed = (m_data and m_data.grid_dressed) and true or false
	G.began = nil

	-- Read BEFORE the squares are, and used until the block can be
	-- measured again, so the first rebuild after this load reads them in
	-- the units they were written in.
	local h = m_data and m_data.grid_head
	G.head_known = (type(h) == "number" and h > 0 and h <= 20)
	               and math.floor(h) or nil

	-- AND reflow STARTS FROM IT, so the first rebuild after this load
	-- moves the squares with the block instead of watching them fall off
	-- the end of a section that has not finished growing. See G.reflow.
	-- A save without a height leaves this nil, which is what it always
	-- was.
	G.head_was = G.head_known

	local s = m_data and m_data.grid_pos
	if type(s) ~= "string" then return end

	-- The sixth field is new and a save written before it has five. Read
	-- both, or every square in an older save is thrown away by a pattern
	-- that no longer matches it.
	local n, by = 0, 0
	for one in string.gmatch(s, "[^,]+") do
		-- Seven fields now; a six-field save is from before rotation
		-- and a five-field one from before by-hand. Read all three, or
		-- an older save's squares are thrown away by a pattern that no
		-- longer matches them.
		local id, x, y, w, h, mine, rot =
			string.match(one, "^(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+)$")
		if not id then
			id, x, y, w, h, mine =
				string.match(one, "^(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+)$")
		end
		if not id then
			id, x, y, w, h = string.match(one, "^(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+):(%d+)$")
		end
		if id then
			id = tonumber(id)
			G.pos[id] = { x = tonumber(x), y = tonumber(y),
			              w = tonumber(w), h = tonumber(h) }
			if mine == "1" then G.byhand[id] = true by = by + 1 end
			if rot == "1" then G.rot[id] = true end
			n = n + 1
		end
	end
	log("%s remembered positions, %s of them put there by hand",
	    tostring(n), tostring(by))
end

-- An item that has left the actor has no square any more, and leaving
-- the entry behind means the next thing to take that id inherits it.
--
-- AND IT SAYS SO. "trying to unstack and by accident dropping stuff" is
-- a report I cannot reproduce and have no mechanism for: the window
-- only puts things on the ground when the drag ends over its bin, and
-- this mod only spills when the tick says you are over capacity, which
-- the log says you have never been. So the next log gets a line at the
-- moment anything hits the ground, next to the taps - and then it is
-- either one of those two or something neither of us has thought of.
-- THE ENGINE SLOTS A NEW WEAPON WHEN A SLOT IS FREE - right for a
-- pickup, wrong for a swap: a scoped gun built out of a bag-laid gun
-- belongs where its line was lying. The legacy is PEEKED, not
-- consumed - the square itself is claimed when the bag packs it.
-- move_to_ruck is a Modded Exes export; on a plainer engine the
-- pcall swallows it and the gun simply keeps the slot.
function G.on_take(obj)
	if not G.enabled then return end

	local ok, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
	if not (ok and type(id) == "number") then return end

	-- A NEW THING TO LOOK AT - but ONLY a genuinely new one.
	--
	--   "highlight whole inventory on saveload and probably area change
	--    too."
	--
	-- On a load or an area change the engine hands every item you were
	-- already carrying back through this callback, one at a time - and
	-- marking all of them fresh lit the whole bag. What tells a re-add
	-- apart from a pickup is that a re-added item ALREADY HAS ITS
	-- SQUARE: G.pos survives both (it is the saved layout), while a
	-- thing just picked up has none yet. So the mark waits until after
	-- the pos check below, and until the world has settled - the same
	-- SETTLE the spill waits out, which covers the load burst that
	-- arrives before the layout is read back.
	if G.pos[id] then return end

	if G.new_glow and G.began and (time_global() - G.began) >= SETTLE then
		G.fresh[id] = true
	end

	local sec
	pcall(function() sec = obj:section() end)
	if type(sec) ~= "string" then return end

	local L = G.legacy_find(sec, true)
	if not (L and L.bag) then return end

	pcall(function()
		for k = 1, 13 do
			local si = db.actor:item_in_slot(k)
			if si and si:id() == id then
				db.actor:move_to_ruck(obj)
				dbg("%s (%s) is pulled back out of slot %s - its line "
				    .. "was bag-laid", tostring(id), tostring(sec),
				    tostring(k))
				break
			end
		end
	end)
end

function G.on_drop(obj)
	if not obj then return end
	local ok, id = pcall(function() return obj:id() end)
	if not ok then return end

	local sec
	pcall(function() sec = obj:section() end)
	dbg("%s (%s) left the actor", tostring(id), tostring(sec))

	-- THE SQUARE OUTLIVES THE OBJECT, BRIEFLY. A swap destroys this
	-- object and creates a kin under a new id a beat later; the
	-- square, the by-hand mark and the lie wait three seconds for
	-- find_bounded to hand them over. `bag` records whether the
	-- departed was laid out rather than worn, because only a
	-- bag-laid line may pull its successor back out of a weapon slot.
	local p = G.pos[id]
	if p and type(sec) == "string" then
		local slotted = false
		pcall(function()
			for k = 1, 13 do
				local si = db.actor and db.actor:item_in_slot(k)
				if si and si:id() == id then slotted = true break end
			end
		end)
		G.legacy[#G.legacy + 1] = {
			sec = sec, x = p.x, y = p.y, w = p.w, h = p.h,
			mine = G.byhand[id] and true or nil,
			rot = G.rot[id] and true or nil,
			bag = (not slotted) or nil,
			at = time_global(),
		}
		while #G.legacy > 4 do table.remove(G.legacy, 1) end
	end

	G.forget(id)
end

-- ============================================================
-- THE DOOR OTHER MODS COME IN THROUGH
--
-- `G` is a local table. `zzz_amp_grid.foo` resolves against this
-- file's GLOBALS, and a local is not one of them - so every call from
-- another mod found nil, and every one of those calls is wrapped in a
-- pcall, so nothing was ever printed about it.
--
-- Three things were quietly not working because of this and one of
-- them is new:
--
--   reserve      a square held for an item that does not exist yet
--   split_key    read as a fallback to LSHIFT every time, so the
--                setting did nothing
--   pack_slot    the backpack slot had nowhere to stand, so it was
--                never drawn - which is how this was found
--
-- SO EVERY NAME ANOTHER MOD IS MEANT TO CALL IS DECLARED HERE, at file
-- scope, and nowhere else. A function that is not in this block is
-- private, and one that is here is a promise.
-- ============================================================
function pack_slot(cc) return G.pack_slot(cc) end
function pack_glyph() return G.pack_glyph() end
-- The third argument is the LIE - and this shim silently dropped it
-- for seven releases, so a box take-out never landed turned in the
-- bag. Every name in this block is a promise; a promise forwards its
-- whole signature.
function reserve(id, sec, turn) return G.reserve(id, sec, turn) end
function ride(id, like) return G.ride(id, like) end
function room_for(cc, id, sec) return G.room_for(cc, id, sec) end

-- ============================================================
-- HOW MANY OF A THING SHARE ONE SQUARE
--
--   "everything that fits can be stored there, with the same stack
--    sizes as in inventory"
--
-- The rigs' slots are grids now, and a square in a rig has to stack
-- exactly as deep as the same square in your bag - which means asking
-- the file that decides it rather than keeping a second copy of those
-- ceilings in the other mod. One answer, and no pair to keep in step.
--
-- A DOOR, so a top-level function: G.stack_limit is a field of a local
-- and is nil to every other file, which is a mistake this project has
-- shipped twice and now has an audit rule for.
-- ============================================================
function stack_limit(sec) return G.stack_limit(sec) end

-- ============================================================
-- HOW A PARTICULAR ITEM IS LYING RIGHT NOW
--
--   "the rotation is not saved between inventory/rig/boxes"
--
-- G.size_of answers for a SECTION - the icon as it was drawn. G.size_for
-- answers for the item, swapped when this mod has it lying on its side,
-- and until now nothing outside this file could ask.
--
-- So a thing turned in the bag and dragged into a rig arrived upright,
-- because the rig asked about the section and the section does not know
-- which way up anything is. A DOOR, so a top-level function.
-- ============================================================
function size_for(id, sec) return G.size_for(id, sec) end

-- For a mod that draws in the body's list after this one does. See
-- G.nofit_again: whoever paints last is on top, and this is how the
-- red grounds get to be last.
function nofit_again(cc) return G.nofit_again(cc) end

-- A VALUE, not a function, because that is how it is read. Kept in
-- step by refresh_settings, which is the one place the setting is
-- turned into a number.
split_key = nil

function on_game_start()

	log("version %s loading", VERSION)
	G.refresh_settings()

	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_update", G.on_update)
	RegisterScriptCallback("on_option_change", G.refresh_settings)

	-- THE DOOR, and the count behind it. Something arriving by any road
	-- at all changes what fits, so the memo goes with it - which is
	-- what keeps a take-all honest between the window's redraws.
	RegisterScriptCallback("ActorMenu_on_item_before_move", G.on_before_move)
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_item_take", G.on_take_fit)
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_first_update", G.install_ui)
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_item_drop", G.on_drop)
	RegisterScriptCallback("actor_on_item_take", G.on_take)
	RegisterScriptCallback("save_state", save_state)
	RegisterScriptCallback("load_state", load_state)

	log("version %s ready", VERSION)
end
